window.MIBEM_INDEX = [{"n":17,"l":"Start here 0","t":"Start here: what you will learn and how","h":"Start here: what you will learn and how","u":"computational/start-here.html","x":"Topics/Start hereStart here 0 Start here: what you will learn and how You do not need any background in materials, computing or advanced mathematics to begin. If you can do basic arithmetic and you are curious about why steel is strong, why glass is brittle or why a chip can hold billions of switches, this page is the front door. Reading timeabout 8 min Builds onNothing: this is the place to start Leads toS1. Numbers, units, graphsS3. Atoms and materials CategoryStart here After reading this page you should be able to: Say in plain words what computational materials science is for. Explain what a model and a simulation are, using an everyday example. Describe the three levels of this site and how long each takes. Know where to start, and how to study in a way that actually sticks."},{"n":17,"l":"Start here 0","t":"Start here: what you will learn and how","h":"1. What this subject is","u":"computational/start-here.html#what","x":"Everything around you is a material: the steel in a bridge, the glass of a window, the plastic of a bottle, the silicon in a phone. What a material does (how strong it is, whether it conducts electricity, how it reacts to heat or to water) depends on what it is made of and how its tiny parts, its atoms, are arranged. People who work on materials want to answer questions such as: Which mix of metals will make a lighter aircraft part that is still strong? Why does this steel crack in the cold? How can a battery material store more energy? The traditional way to find out is to make samples and test them. That is slow, expensive and sometimes impossible: you cannot easily look at what individual atoms are doing, and you cannot afford to melt and test a million recipes. Computational materials science uses computers to help. We write down rules for how atoms, electrons, heat and forces behave, and then we let the computer work out what follows. The result is a prediction: how a crystal will deform, which structure is most stable, how fast atoms will move at a given temperature. Good predictions tell researchers which experiments are worth doing, explain results that are hard to see, and sometimes replace experiments that are impossible."},{"n":17,"l":"Start here 0","t":"Start here: what you will learn and how","h":"2. Models and simulations","u":"computational/start-here.html#model","x":"Two words appear everywhere on this site, so let us pin them down with a cup of tea. A model is a simplified description of something that keeps the parts that matter and ignores the rest. For a cooling cup of tea, a very simple model says: the tea loses heat at a rate proportional to how much hotter it is than the room. That one sentence ignores the shape of the cup, the steam, the draught from the window. It is not perfect, but it is useful, and you can test it. A simulation is the step-by-step calculation of what a model predicts. If the model says how fast the temperature changes, a simulation takes the temperature now, applies the rule for one minute, gets the temperature a minute later, and repeats. A computer can do this millions of times a second. That is the whole recipe, and it is the same for every simulation in this series: (1) describe the state of the system, (2) write the rule for how it changes, (3) apply the rule over and over. What changes from topic to topic is what the state is (positions of atoms, concentrations, temperatures in a part) and how good the rule is. The last page of the Start here level, Your first simulation, builds one yourself. There is one more essential step, which is what separates science from guessing: checking. A model is only trustworthy if its predictions agree with measurements, or with a case where the answer is known exactly. You will see this idea on every page."},{"n":17,"l":"Start here 0","t":"Start here: what you will learn and how","h":"3. Zooming in on a material","u":"computational/start-here.html#scales","x":"A steel beam looks smooth and uniform. Zoom in and a different picture appears at each step, and different methods are needed for each. Each method covers a limited window of size and time. No single method covers everything, so the series teaches them one at a time and then shows how they connect. At the scale of electrons (about a tenth of a nanometre, a nanometre being a billionth of a metre) the rules of quantum physics decide how atoms bond. At the scale of atoms (a few nanometres) we can follow groups of atoms moving and vibrating, and watch defects move. At the scale of the microstructure (micrometres, millionths of a metre) we see grains, tiny crystals packed together, and particles of other phases growing and shrinking. At the scale of a component (millimetres to metres) we ask how the whole part heats, bends and survives."},{"n":17,"l":"Start here 0","t":"Start here: what you will learn and how","h":"4. The three levels","u":"computational/start-here.html#levels","x":"The site is organised in three levels so that you can enter at the right place. The times are honest estimates of reading plus doing the exercises, not just skimming. Level Who it is for What it covers Time Start here (7 pages) Anyone with school-level maths and science, or who feels rusty Numbers and units, graphs, calculus ideas, atoms and bonds, forces and energy, chance and averages, a first simulation in code about 6 to 8 hours Background (5 pages) People who have done the level above or have a first year of university science The mathematics, physics, materials science, numerical methods and programming that the methods rely on about 8 to 10 hours Core topics (11 pages) Everyone who wants to use or understand the methods Statistical mechanics, thermodynamics, density functional theory, molecular dynamics, Monte Carlo, diffusion, phase-field, finite elements, machine learning about 15 to 20 hours Altogether that is about 30 to 40 hours. Working steadily at a few hours a week, expect roughly two to four months from zero to a solid first pass through the whole series, and longer if you also do every extra exercise and project. A faster pace is possible if some of it is already familiar."},{"n":17,"l":"Start here 0","t":"Start here: what you will learn and how","h":"5. How to learn from these pages","u":"computational/start-here.html#how","x":"Reading alone is the weakest way to learn. These habits make the time count: Do the demos. Every page has interactive pieces. Move the sliders before you read the explanation, and predict what will happen first. Try the questions before opening the answers. Struggling for a minute, even if you get it wrong, helps you remember more than reading the answer straight away. Keep a notebook. After each page write three sentences in your own words: what it was about, one thing that surprised you, one thing you are still unsure of. This takes five minutes and is the best memory aid there is. Use the words. Terms with a dotted underline show a short definition when you hover, tap or focus them, and the glossary collects them all. New words are the main barrier, not hard ideas. Go down a level, not harder. If a page feels like a wall, the right response is to read the page it builds on (shown at the top under “Builds on” and on the topic map), not to push through confused. Come back. Revisit a page a few days and then a few weeks later and try its questions again. Spaced repetition is how things move into long-term memory. Do something small yourself. From page 7 on you will run short pieces of code. Typing them in, changing a number and seeing what breaks teaches more than reading them."},{"n":17,"l":"Start here 0","t":"Start here: what you will learn and how","h":"6. Find your starting point","u":"computational/start-here.html#quiz","x":"This quiz takes about two minutes. It is not graded, and nothing you answer is stored or sent anywhere. It only points you to the pages that are likely to be useful. This quiz needs JavaScript. Without it, simply start with the first Start here page, Numbers, units and graphs, and move on whenever the material feels familiar."},{"n":17,"l":"Start here 0","t":"Start here: what you will learn and how","h":"7. The path in order","u":"computational/start-here.html#order","x":"Start here: Numbers, units and graphs; Rates of change; Atoms, bonds and materials; Forces, energy, heat and motion; Chance, averages and error bars; Your first simulation. Background: Mathematics; Physics; Materials science essentials; Numerical methods; Programming and workflow. Core topics: start with the Introduction, then follow the topic map toward whichever method interests you most. Key takeaways Computational materials science uses computers to predict how materials behave, by describing atoms, energy and heat with rules and calculating what follows. A model is a simplified description; a simulation is the step-by-step calculation of what the model predicts, and it must be checked against reality. The site has three levels: Start here (school-level), Background (university-level refresher) and Core topics (the methods). Learn actively: try every demo, attempt each question before opening the answer, and use the glossary when a word is new. If a page feels too hard, go one level down instead of pushing on; that is the intended way to use this site."},{"n":17,"l":"Start here 0","t":"Start here: what you will learn and how","h":"8. Check your understanding","u":"computational/start-here.html#check","x":"In your own words, what is the difference between a model and a simulation? A model is a simplified description of how something behaves (the rule). A simulation is the calculation that applies the rule step by step to find out what happens. You can have a model without simulating it (for example, solving it with pen and paper), but a simulation always needs a model. Why can’t we just look at atoms to see what a material is doing? Atoms are about a tenth of a nanometre across, far smaller than visible light can resolve, and the interesting events (atoms moving, bonds forming) are extremely fast and happen inside the material. Special microscopes can image atoms in thin samples, but not follow everything inside a working component, which is why simulation is so useful. You find a Core topic page too hard. What should you do? Look at the “Builds on” list at the top of the page (or the topic map) and read those pages first. The levels are designed so that each page rests on the ones before it, so going down one level is the intended solution, not a failure."},{"n":17,"l":"Start here 0","t":"Start here: what you will learn and how","h":"9. Where to go for more","u":"computational/start-here.html#refs","x":"These are free or widely available and fit this level. Khan Academy, free courses from basic algebra to calculus and introductory physics and chemistry. 3Blue1Brown, “Essence of calculus” and “Essence of linear algebra”, short video series with excellent pictures. MIT OpenCourseWare, free lecture notes and videos, including introductory solid-state chemistry and materials courses. Lee, J. G., Computational Materials Science: An Introduction, CRC Press (an introductory textbook on the same subject). Callister, W. D. and Rethwisch, D. G., Materials Science and Engineering: An Introduction, Wiley (the standard first textbook on materials). Next topicNumbers, units and graphs"},{"n":18,"l":"Start here 1","t":"Numbers, units and graphs","h":"Numbers, units and graphs","u":"computational/numbers-units-and-graphs.html","x":"Topics/Start hereStart here 1 Numbers, units and graphs Science speaks in numbers with units, pictures called graphs, and a little algebra. This page teaches exactly the amount you need for everything that follows, with worked examples you can check yourself. Reading timeabout 13 min Builds onS0. Start here Leads toS2. Rates of changeS4. Forces, energy, heatS5. Chance and averagesB1. Mathematics CategoryStart here After reading this page you should be able to: Write and compare very large and very small numbers in scientific notation. Convert between units without mistakes, and spot an answer whose units are wrong. Rearrange simple equations and read a graph of a line, a curve, an exponential or a power law. Explain what a logarithm is and why scientists plot on log scales. You need: basic arithmetic (adding, multiplying, fractions, percentages). Everything else is explained here."},{"n":18,"l":"Start here 1","t":"Numbers, units and graphs","h":"1. Why start with this","u":"computational/numbers-units-and-graphs.html#why","x":"An atom is about 0.0000000001 metres across; a bridge is about a hundred metres long. A day has 86 400 seconds; an atom vibrates in a ten-thousand-billionth of a second. To describe such different sizes we need a compact way to write numbers, a careful way to handle units, and graphs that show how one quantity depends on another. These are the tools, and once you are comfortable with them nearly every equation in the series becomes readable."},{"n":18,"l":"Start here 1","t":"Numbers, units and graphs","h":"2. Very big and very small numbers","u":"computational/numbers-units-and-graphs.html#notation","x":"Powers of ten Instead of writing a long string of zeros we use powers of ten. 103 means 10 × 10 × 10 = 1000. 106 is a million. For small numbers, a negative power means divide: 10−3 = 1/1000 = 0.001, and 10−9 is a billionth. The exponent (the small raised number) counts the zeros, and its sign says whether the number is big or small. Scientific notation writes any number as a value between 1 and 10 times a power of ten: The Avogadro number: 602 200 000 000 000 000 000 000 = 6.022 × 1023. The spacing between atoms in a metal: 0.00000000025 m = 2.5 × 10−10 m. A trick for converting: count how many places the decimal point moves. Moving it left makes the exponent positive, moving right makes it negative. Calculating with powers of ten To multiply, multiply the numbers and add the exponents. To divide, divide the numbers and subtract the exponents. For example: (3 × 105) × (2 × 10−2) = 6 × 103 (6 × 108) / (3 × 102) = 2 × 106 Worked example. How many atoms fit in a row across 1 millimetre (10−3 m) if the spacing is 2.5 × 10−10 m? Divide: (1 × 10−3) / (2.5 × 10−10) = 0.4 × 107 = 4 × 106, four million atoms. A computer simulation holding a million atoms in a cube therefore covers a cube only about a hundred atoms on a side, which is tiny. Orders of magnitude and significant figures Two numbers differ by one order of magnitude if one is about ten times the other. Comparing powers of ten tells you at a glance whether something is a small correction or a huge effect. The significant figures of a number are the digits that carry information: 2.5 × 10−10 has two. Never quote an answer with more figures than the least precise number you started with, and do not round in the middle of a calculation, only at the end. Prefix Symbol Meaning Example tera T 1012 1 THz = a trillion vibrations per second giga G 109 1 GPa = a billion pascals (pressure or stress) mega M 106 1 MPa = a million pascals kilo k 103 1 kJ = 1000 joules milli m 10−3 1 meV = a thousandth of an electronvolt micro µ 10−6 1 µm = a millionth of a metre; a human hair is about 70 µm wide nano n 10−9 1 nm = a billionth of a metre pico p 10−12 1 ps = a trillionth of a second femto f 10−15 1 fs = a thousandth of a trillionth of a second One extra unit appears constantly in materials science: the Ångström, 1 Å = 10−10 m = 0.1 nm, about the size of an atom."},{"n":18,"l":"Start here 1","t":"Numbers, units and graphs","h":"3. Units and conversions","u":"computational/numbers-units-and-graphs.html#units","x":"A number without a unit is almost meaningless: “the rod is 5 long” could be 5 millimetres or 5 kilometres. Always carry the unit, and treat it like part of the number: you can multiply and divide units, and you can only add or compare quantities that have the same unit. Converting by multiplying by one A conversion factor is a fraction equal to 1, because the top and bottom are the same amount in different units: (10−9 m)/(1 nm) = 1. Multiply by it so that the unit you do not want cancels: 5 nm × (10−9 m / 1 nm) = 5 × 10−9 m Worked example. Convert 72 km/h to metres per second. Use 1 km = 1000 m and 1 h = 3600 s: 72 km/h × (1000 m / 1 km) × (1 h / 3600 s) = 20 m/s Check the units Units are a free error detector. If you compute a speed and the units come out as seconds, something is wrong. For instance, distance divided by time gives m/s, so a distance of 100 m covered in 25 s gives 100/25 = 4 m/s; the same numbers in the other order would give 0.25 s/m, which is not a speed. Checking units at the end of every calculation catches many mistakes. Units you will meet in this series Temperature: the kelvin (K) starts at absolute zero. To convert, K = °C + 273.15. A difference of 1 K equals a difference of 1 °C. Energy: the joule (J) is the standard unit, but at the atomic scale it is far too big, so scientists use the electronvolt (eV): 1 eV = 1.602 × 10−19 J. For chemical reactions you will also see kJ/mol (energy per mole of atoms), where 1 eV per atom = 96.5 kJ/mol. A typical chemical bond is about 1 to 4 eV. Pressure and stress: the pascal (Pa), which is a newton of force per square metre. Steel yields at about 250 million pascals, written 250 MPa. Worked example. A bond has energy 2 eV. In kJ/mol that is 2 × 96.5 = 193 kJ/mol. Try it yourself with the converter below, which does the arithmetic and lets you build a feel for the sizes. This interactive converter needs JavaScript to run."},{"n":18,"l":"Start here 1","t":"Numbers, units and graphs","h":"4. Algebra: working with unknowns","u":"computational/numbers-units-and-graphs.html#algebra","x":"Algebra uses letters for numbers we do not know yet, or that can change. An equation says two things are equal, and we can do the same thing to both sides without breaking the equality. To solve an equation means to rearrange it until the unknown is alone on one side. Worked example. Solve 3x + 6 = 21. Subtract 6 from both sides: 3x = 15. Divide both sides by 3: x = 5. Check by putting 5 back: 3 × 5 + 6 = 21. ✓ Always check. Formulas can be rearranged the same way. Speed = distance / time can be written v = d/t. Multiply both sides by t to get d = v t; divide both sides by v to get t = d/v. One formula gives three questions you can answer. Exponents (powers) and roots an means multiply a by itself n times. The rules you will use most: am × an = am+n (add exponents when multiplying), for example 103 × 102 = 105. (am)n = amn. a−n = 1/an, for example 2−3 = 1/8. a1/2 = √a, the square root: the number which multiplied by itself gives a. So 161/2 = 4. Proportionality and percentages “y is proportional to x” (written y ∝ x) means doubling x doubles y. If y ∝ x2, doubling x makes y four times bigger; if y ∝ 1/x, doubling x halves y. Many laws in this series are of this kind: the area of a circle is proportional to the square of its radius, and the distance atoms spread by diffusion is proportional to the square root of time. A percentage change is (new − old)/old × 100%: going from 200 to 230 is a 15% increase."},{"n":18,"l":"Start here 1","t":"Numbers, units and graphs","h":"5. Functions and graphs","u":"computational/numbers-units-and-graphs.html#graphs","x":"A function is a rule that takes an input and gives an output, like a machine. We write y = f(x): put in x, get y. A graph shows the function as a picture: the horizontal axis (x) is the input, the vertical axis (y) the output, and each point shows one input and its output. A good graph always labels both axes with the quantity and its unit. Four shapes cover most of what you will see: Straight line, y = m x + c. The number m is the slope (how much y rises for each step of 1 in x) and c is the intercept (where the line crosses the y axis). A constant speed gives a straight distance–time graph whose slope is the speed. Parabola, y = a(x − x0)2 + c, a bowl or hill shape. The bottom of any smooth valley looks like a parabola, which is why it describes the energy of atoms near their preferred positions. Exponential, y = Aekx, where e ≈ 2.718 is a special number. With k positive it grows faster and faster (doubling every fixed amount, the doubling time ln 2/k); with k negative it dies away (a half-life). Radioactive decay, cooling and diffusion into a solid all behave this way. Power law, y = A xp. Doubling x multiplies y by 2p. With p = 2 it is a parabola through the origin; with p = 1/2 it is a square root. Use the explorer to see how each shape responds to its settings. Change the sliders and watch what the numbers do. This interactive graph explorer needs JavaScript to run."},{"n":18,"l":"Start here 1","t":"Numbers, units and graphs","h":"6. Logarithms","u":"computational/numbers-units-and-graphs.html#logs","x":"A logarithm answers the question “what power?” Since 103 = 1000, we say log10(1000) = 3: the power of ten that gives 1000. Likewise log10(0.001) = −3, and log10(1) = 0. The logarithm to base e is called the natural logarithm, written ln, and it undoes the exponential: ln(ex) = x. Two rules make logarithms useful: log(a b) = log a + log b (multiplying turns into adding) and log(an) = n log a (a power turns into a multiplication). Why scientists use log scales Many quantities span enormous ranges: diffusion coefficients can differ by a factor of 1010 between two elements. On an ordinary axis the small ones are invisible; on a log scale every step of one unit is a factor of ten, so all of them fit and can be compared. A second reason is more powerful: an exponential becomes a straight line on a log scale, because ln(Aekx) = ln A + k x. A straight line is easy to fit and to read. Try it in the explorer: choose the exponential, then tick “Log scale on the y axis”. This is how activation energies are measured. Many processes speed up with temperature as D = D0 e−Q/(kT). Taking the log gives ln D = ln D0 − (Q/k) × (1/T), a straight line when ln D is plotted against 1/T, with slope −Q/k. Reading the slope gives the energy barrier Q. You will meet this again in diffusion and kinetics."},{"n":18,"l":"Start here 1","t":"Numbers, units and graphs","h":"7. Slopes and straight-line fits","u":"computational/numbers-units-and-graphs.html#slope","x":"The slope of a straight line through two points (x1, y1) and (x2, y2) is the rise over the run: slope = (y2 − y1) / (x2 − x1) Worked example. A line passes through (0, 2) and (4, 10). Slope = (10 − 2)/(4 − 0) = 8/4 = 2. The intercept is 2, so y = 2x + 2. The slope has units: if y is distance in metres and x is time in seconds, the slope is 2 m/s, a speed. Real measurements scatter, so the points do not lie exactly on a line. A line of best fit is the line that passes as close as possible to all the points; the standard method, called least squares, chooses the line that minimises the sum of the squared vertical distances to the points. Spreadsheets and Python do this for you (the first simulation page shows how). What you should do is look at the points and the line together: if the points curve systematically away from the line, a straight line is the wrong model."},{"n":18,"l":"Start here 1","t":"Numbers, units and graphs","h":"8. Angles and arrows","u":"computational/numbers-units-and-graphs.html#angles","x":"Two last ideas appear repeatedly. First, sine and cosine. In a right-angled triangle with an angle θ, sin θ is the length of the side opposite divided by the hypotenuse (the longest side), and cos θ is the side next to the angle divided by the hypotenuse. As θ goes round a circle, sin and cos trace out smooth repeating waves between −1 and 1, which is why they describe vibrations, sound and light. Angles are often given in radians, where a full turn is 2π (about 6.28), so π radians = 180°. Second, a vector is a quantity with a size and a direction, such as a force or a velocity, drawn as an arrow. In two dimensions we describe it by its components, how far it goes along x and y. The vector (3, 4) points 3 along and 4 up, and by Pythagoras its length is √(32 + 42) = √25 = 5. To add vectors, add their components. Key takeaways Scientific notation (such as 6.0 x 10^23) lets you write very large and very small numbers and compare sizes at a glance. A number without a unit is incomplete; convert by multiplying by factors equal to one, and check that units balance. A function turns an input into an output; its graph shows how, and the shape (line, curve, exponential) tells you the kind of process. A logarithm asks 'what power?', and plotting on a log scale turns exponential curves into straight lines. The slope of a straight line is a rate: how much y changes for each step in x."},{"n":18,"l":"Start here 1","t":"Numbers, units and graphs","h":"9. Check your understanding","u":"computational/numbers-units-and-graphs.html#check","x":"Try each question on paper first. Convert 250 pm (picometres) to Ångströms. 1 pm = 10−12 m and 1 Å = 10−10 m, so 1 pm = 0.01 Å. Then 250 pm = 2.5 Å, a typical distance between atoms. Calculate (3 × 105) × (2 × 10−2). Multiply the numbers: 3 × 2 = 6. Add the exponents: 5 + (−2) = 3. The answer is 6 × 103 = 6000. Solve 5x − 7 = 18. Add 7 to both sides: 5x = 25. Divide by 5: x = 5. Check: 5 × 5 − 7 = 18. ✓ A quantity doubles every 3 hours and starts at 1. What is it after 12 hours? 12 hours is four doubling times, so the value is 24 = 16. What is log10(0.001)? 0.001 = 10−3, so the logarithm is −3. A straight line passes through (1, 5) and (3, 11). Find its equation. Slope = (11 − 5)/(3 − 1) = 3. Use y = 3x + c with the point (1, 5): 5 = 3 + c, so c = 2. The line is y = 3x + 2."},{"n":18,"l":"Start here 1","t":"Numbers, units and graphs","h":"10. Where to go for more","u":"computational/numbers-units-and-graphs.html#refs","x":"Khan Academy: Pre-algebra, Algebra 1, and Algebra 2 (free). Kreyszig, E., Advanced Engineering Mathematics, Wiley (for later reference). Taylor, J. R., An Introduction to Error Analysis, University Science Books (on units, uncertainties and significant figures). Previous topicStart here: what you will learn and how Next topicRates of change: calculus from scratch"},{"n":19,"l":"Start here 2","t":"Rates of change: calculus from scratch","h":"Rates of change: calculus from scratch","u":"computational/rates-of-change-calculus.html","x":"Topics/Start hereStart here 2 Rates of change: calculus from scratch Calculus is the mathematics of change. Nearly every law in this series says how fast something changes: how quickly heat flows, how fast atoms move, how a force follows from an energy. This page builds the three main ideas (slope, area and equations for rates) from pictures, without proofs. Reading timeabout 11 min Builds onS1. Numbers, units, graphs Leads toS6. Your first simulationB1. Mathematics CategoryStart here After reading this page you should be able to: Explain what a derivative is and read it as the slope of a curve at a point. Differentiate simple functions and use the result to find where a curve has a minimum. Explain what an integral is and calculate simple ones. Recognise an equation of the form “rate ∝ amount” and describe its solution. You need: the previous page, Numbers, units and graphs (graphs, slopes, exponentials)."},{"n":19,"l":"Start here 2","t":"Rates of change: calculus from scratch","h":"1. Change and rates","u":"computational/rates-of-change-calculus.html#rates","x":"Suppose a car travels 150 metres in 10 seconds. Its average speed is the distance divided by the time: 150/10 = 15 m/s. On a graph of distance against time, this is the slope of the straight line joining the start and the end. But a real car speeds up and slows down, and the speedometer shows something different: its speed right now. How can we measure speed at a single instant, when no time passes? The answer is to look at shorter and shorter intervals. The average speed over the last second is a good guess, over the last tenth of a second a better one, over the last thousandth better still. As the interval shrinks toward zero, the average settles toward a definite number, the instantaneous rate of change. That is the central idea of calculus, and it is the step that the first demo below lets you take."},{"n":19,"l":"Start here 2","t":"Rates of change: calculus from scratch","h":"2. The derivative","u":"computational/rates-of-change-calculus.html#derivative","x":"The slope of the straight line between two points on a curve is called a secant. Move the second point closer to the first and the secant swings toward the tangent, the line that just touches the curve at one point. The slope of the tangent is the derivative of the function at that point. In symbols, for a function f(x): f′(x) = limh→0 [ f(x + h) − f(x) ] / h The expression is the slope of the secant (rise over run) with gap h; the “lim” says “what it approaches as h shrinks to zero”. The derivative is also written df/dx (read “dee f by dee x”), and if the variable is time, dx/dt. Its unit is the unit of f per unit of x: the derivative of position (metres) with respect to time (seconds) is velocity in m/s. This interactive demo needs JavaScript to run. Derivatives you can write down You do not have to compute limits each time. A short table of results covers nearly everything: Function Derivative Example a constant, c 0 a flat line has slope zero x 1 the line y = x has slope 1 xn n xn−1 d(x2)/dx = 2x; d(x3)/dx = 3x2 ekx k ekx d(e−0.5t)/dt = −0.5 e−0.5t sin x cos x slope of a sine wave at 0 is 1 cos x −sin x ln x 1/x Two rules let you combine them. A constant multiplies through: d(3x2)/dx = 3 × 2x = 6x. Sums are done term by term: d(5 + 2t − t2)/dt = 0 + 2 − 2t = 2 − 2t. Worked example. A ball’s height is h(t) = 20t − 5t2 metres. Its velocity is the derivative, v(t) = 20 − 10t m/s. At t = 1 s the velocity is 10 m/s (still rising); at t = 2 s it is 0 (the top of the flight); at t = 3 s it is −10 m/s (falling). The derivative of velocity, −10 m/s2, is the acceleration due to gravity (the minus sign says “downward”), found by differentiating twice."},{"n":19,"l":"Start here 2","t":"Rates of change: calculus from scratch","h":"3. Finding the bottom of a valley","u":"computational/rates-of-change-calculus.html#minima","x":"A ball rolls to the lowest point of a valley and stays there. At the very bottom the ground is flat for an instant: the slope is zero. So to find a minimum (or a maximum) of any smooth curve, set the derivative to zero and solve. This is how equilibrium positions are found throughout science, because systems tend to settle where their energy is lowest. Worked example. The energy of two atoms depends on their separation x (in some units) as U(x) = (x − 2)2 + 1. Then U′(x) = 2(x − 2). Setting it to zero gives x = 2, and the energy there is U(2) = 1. So the preferred separation is 2. Zero slope alone does not tell you whether you are at a valley or a hilltop. For that look at the second derivative, the derivative of the derivative, which measures how fast the slope changes (the curvature). If U″ > 0 the curve bends upward like a bowl: a minimum, a stable equilibrium. If U″ < 0 it bends downward: a maximum, unstable. In the example U″ = 2 > 0, so it is a stable minimum. The curvature also has a physical meaning: it is the stiffness. A steep-sided narrow valley (large U″) means any displacement is strongly resisted, like a stiff spring. For a spring the energy is ½k(x − x0)2, whose second derivative is exactly the spring constant k. Near the bottom, nearly every energy curve looks like such a parabola, which is why springs describe vibrating atoms so well."},{"n":19,"l":"Start here 2","t":"Rates of change: calculus from scratch","h":"4. Adding up: area and integrals","u":"computational/rates-of-change-calculus.html#area","x":"Calculus has a second main idea, the reverse of finding a rate: adding up many small pieces. If a car moves at a constant 20 m/s for 5 s, it covers 20 × 5 = 100 m, the area of a rectangle on a speed–time graph (height 20, width 5). If the speed changes, cut the time into many thin strips, treat the speed as constant in each strip, and add the areas. Thinner strips give a better answer, and in the limit of infinitely thin strips the total is called the integral, written with an elongated S for “sum”: distance = ∫ab v(t) dt The integral equals the area under the curve between a and b. Move the slider to add more rectangles and watch the sum approach the exact area. This interactive demo needs JavaScript to run. Integrals can often be found by running the derivative table backwards: ∫ xn dx = xn+1/(n + 1). This reveals the deep link: differentiation and integration undo each other. Worked examples. A speed that rises steadily as v = 2t from 0 to 4 s covers a distance equal to the area of a triangle, ½ × 4 × 8 = 16 m. The same result from the rule: ∫04 2t dt = [t2]04 = 16 − 0 = 16. And ∫03 x2 dx = [x3/3]03 = 27/3 = 9, the exact value the area demo approaches. Integrals appear whenever a total is built from varying pieces: the energy absorbed is the integral of power over time, the amount of material in a region is the integral of concentration over volume, and the average of a quantity is its integral divided by the length of the interval."},{"n":19,"l":"Start here 2","t":"Rates of change: calculus from scratch","h":"5. Equations about rates","u":"computational/rates-of-change-calculus.html#de","x":"The most important use of derivatives in science is writing laws as equations about how fast something changes. These are called differential equations. The simplest and most common says that the rate of change is proportional to the amount present: dN/dt = −k N Read it as: “the amount N decreases at a rate proportional to itself”. The more there is, the faster it goes; as it dwindles, the loss slows. Its solution is an exponential decay, N(t) = N0 e−kt, where N0 is the starting amount. You can check by differentiating: the derivative of N0 e−kt is −k N0 e−kt = −k N. ✓ The half-life, the time for N to fall to half, is t½ = ln 2/k ≈ 0.693/k. If k = 0.05 per minute, the half-life is 13.9 minutes. With a plus sign instead of a minus the same equation describes growth with a doubling time. The cooling of a cup of tea follows the same pattern with one change: the rate depends on the difference from room temperature, dT/dt = −k(T − Troom). Its solution is T = Troom + (T0 − Troom) e−kt: the tea relaxes exponentially toward room temperature. A computer can also get the answer without any formula by taking small steps: new T = old T + (rate) × (time step). That is exactly how simulations work, and you will build one in Your first simulation."},{"n":19,"l":"Start here 2","t":"Rates of change: calculus from scratch","h":"6. More than one variable","u":"computational/rates-of-change-calculus.html#several","x":"Often a quantity depends on several things: the height of a hilly landscape depends on x and y; the energy of a system depends on the positions of many atoms. A partial derivative is the slope in one direction while the other variables are held fixed, written ∂h/∂x. For h(x, y) = 4 − x2 − y2 (a smooth hill), ∂h/∂x = −2x and ∂h/∂y = −2y. Putting the slopes together gives the gradient, an arrow written ∇h = (∂h/∂x, ∂h/∂y) that points in the direction of steepest uphill, with length equal to the steepness. For the hill, ∇h = (−2x, −2y), pointing back toward the summit at the origin. This leads to the most important formula in atomistic modelling. A ball on a slope is pushed downhill, opposite to the gradient. In the same way, the force on an object is minus the gradient of its potential energy: F = −∇U For U(x, y) = x2 + 3y2 the force is (−2x, −6y), which at (1, 2) is (−2, −12): it points toward the minimum and is stronger in the steeper direction. Computing forces on atoms from an energy is exactly what molecular dynamics does at every step. Key takeaways A derivative is the slope of a curve at a point: the instantaneous rate of change. Where the slope of an energy curve is zero there is an equilibrium; a positive curvature there means it is stable. An integral adds up many small pieces and equals the area under a curve. Many laws say 'the rate of change depends on the amount', and their solutions are exponential decay or growth. Force is minus the slope of the energy: the arrow that points downhill."},{"n":19,"l":"Start here 2","t":"Rates of change: calculus from scratch","h":"7. Check your understanding","u":"computational/rates-of-change-calculus.html#check","x":"A runner covers 150 m in 10 s. What is the average speed? Distance divided by time: 150/10 = 15 m/s. This is the slope of the line joining the start and finish of the distance–time graph. Differentiate 4x3. Bring down the power and subtract one from it, keeping the constant: 4 × 3x2 = 12x2. Where is the minimum of U(x) = 3(x − 1)2 + 2, and how stiff is it? U′ = 6(x − 1) = 0 gives x = 1, where U = 2. The second derivative is U″ = 6, positive, so it is a minimum with stiffness 6. What is the area under the constant function f(x) = 3 from x = 0 to x = 5? It is a rectangle of height 3 and width 5, so the area (the integral) is 15. A quantity decays as dN/dt = −0.1N (time in hours). What is its half-life? ln 2/k = 0.693/0.1 = 6.93 hours. A potential is U(x, y) = x2 + y2. What is the force at (1, −2)? F = −∇U = (−2x, −2y) = (−2, 4) at that point. It points toward the origin, the minimum."},{"n":19,"l":"Start here 2","t":"Rates of change: calculus from scratch","h":"8. Where to go for more","u":"computational/rates-of-change-calculus.html#refs","x":"3Blue1Brown, “Essence of calculus” (video series, free): the best pictorial introduction. Khan Academy, Differential calculus and Integral calculus (free). Strang, G., Calculus, Wellesley–Cambridge Press (free online from MIT OpenCourseWare). Next: Mathematics for simulation in the Background level, which adds vectors, eigenvalues, Fourier series and statistics. Previous topicNumbers, units and graphs Next topicAtoms, bonds and materials from scratch"},{"n":20,"l":"Start here 3","t":"Atoms, bonds and materials from scratch","h":"Atoms, bonds and materials from scratch","u":"computational/atoms-bonds-and-materials.html","x":"Topics/Start hereStart here 3 Atoms, bonds and materials from scratch Why is a diamond hard and graphite soft when both are pure carbon? Why does a metal bend instead of shatter? The answers lie in how atoms are bonded and arranged. This page builds that picture from the beginning, with two interactive tools to explore it. Reading timeabout 10 min Builds onS0. Start here Leads toB3. Materials essentials CategoryStart here After reading this page you should be able to: Describe an atom, a bond and a crystal, with the sizes involved. Explain why atoms settle at a particular distance, and what bond stiffness and strength mean. Name the main types of bond and the four classes of material. Say what a vacancy, a solute atom and a dislocation are, and why defects matter. You need: nothing beyond the previous pages, mainly powers of ten from Numbers, units and graphs."},{"n":20,"l":"Start here 3","t":"Atoms, bonds and materials from scratch","h":"1. Everything is atoms","u":"computational/atoms-bonds-and-materials.html#atoms","x":"Every material, whether steel, wood, water or air, is built from atoms. There are about a hundred different kinds, each a different element: iron, carbon, oxygen, silicon and so on. An atom is incredibly small, roughly 0.1 to 0.3 nanometres across, which is a few tenths of a billionth of a metre. A single pinch of iron weighing one gram contains about 1022 of them, that is 10 000 000 000 000 000 000 000. To count such numbers chemists use the mole: one mole is 6.022 × 1023 items (the Avogadro number). The mass of one mole of an element in grams, its molar mass, is a number you can read off the periodic table: iron 55.85 g/mol, copper 63.55, aluminium 26.98, carbon 12.01. So the number of atoms in 1 g of iron is (1/55.85) × 6.022 × 1023 ≈ 1.08 × 1022. What a material is like depends on two things: which atoms it contains, and how they are arranged and held together. Diamond and graphite are both pure carbon; the only difference is the arrangement, and the properties are completely different."},{"n":20,"l":"Start here 3","t":"Atoms, bonds and materials from scratch","h":"2. Inside an atom","u":"computational/atoms-bonds-and-materials.html#inside","x":"An atom has a tiny, dense nucleus at the centre, made of protons (positive electric charge) and neutrons (no charge). Around it is a cloud of electrons (negative charge, much lighter than protons). The nucleus is only about 10−15 m across, 100 000 times smaller than the atom: almost all of an atom’s size is the electron cloud, and almost all of its mass is the nucleus. The number of protons, the atomic number, decides which element it is: 6 for carbon, 8 for oxygen, 26 for iron. A neutral atom has equal numbers of protons and electrons. Electrons are arranged in shells at different distances. The electrons in the outermost shell, the valence electrons, are the ones that take part in bonding, and the number of them decides the atom’s chemistry. The periodic table lists elements by atomic number and puts atoms with similar valence electrons in the same column. That is why sodium and potassium behave alike, and why the metals fill the left and middle of the table. The rules governing electrons come from quantum mechanics. You do not need them yet: it is enough to know that electrons are what hold atoms together."},{"n":20,"l":"Start here 3","t":"Atoms, bonds and materials from scratch","h":"3. Why atoms stick together","u":"computational/atoms-bonds-and-materials.html#bonds","x":"Two atoms attract each other because the positive nucleus of one pulls on the negative electrons of the other. But if they get too close their electron clouds and nuclei repel strongly. The balance of attraction and repulsion produces a preferred distance, the bond length, where the energy is lowest, just as a ball settles at the bottom of a valley. The bond energy (or well depth) is the energy needed to pull the atoms apart; typical values are 0.5 to 8 eV per atom. The picture below shows this as an energy curve. Drag the slider to change the separation and watch the force arrows: too close, they push apart; too far, they pull together; at the bond length the force is zero. The force is minus the slope of the curve, exactly the idea from the calculus page. This interactive demo needs JavaScript to run. Two numbers describe a bond. The strength is the depth of the valley: stronger bonds mean materials that melt at higher temperatures and are harder to break. The stiffness is how steep the sides of the valley are: stiff bonds resist being stretched or squeezed, so a material with stiff bonds is hard to bend elastically. Make the bond stronger with the second slider and notice that the valley deepens and gets steeper; in general strong bonds are stiff bonds."},{"n":20,"l":"Start here 3","t":"Atoms, bonds and materials from scratch","h":"4. Types of bond","u":"computational/atoms-bonds-and-materials.html#types","x":"Bond How it works Picture Examples Typical result Metallic Outer electrons are shared by all the atoms and move freely Positive ions in a sea of electrons Iron, copper, aluminium Conduct electricity and heat, shiny, can be bent (ductile) Covalent Neighbouring atoms share pairs of electrons in definite directions Atoms holding hands at fixed angles Diamond, silicon, plastics Hard and brittle (solids) or flexible chains (polymers); often poor conductors Ionic One atom hands electrons to another; the opposite charges attract Alternating positive and negative balls Table salt (NaCl), alumina, magnesia Hard, brittle, high melting points, insulating van der Waals Weak attraction between neutral atoms from fleeting charge shifts A faint stickiness Argon solid, layers of graphite, between polymer chains Soft, low melting points; layers slide Hydrogen bond A hydrogen atom attracted to a neighbouring electronegative atom A weak bridge Water, many polymers Responsible for the structure of water and for strong plastics like nylon Materials are commonly grouped into four classes by their bonding and behaviour, plus combinations: Metals (steel, aluminium, copper): strong, tough, conduct electricity and heat. Ceramics and glasses (alumina, concrete, window glass): hard, brittle, resist heat and corrosion, insulate. Polymers (polyethylene, nylon, rubber): long chain molecules; light, flexible, easy to shape, insulating. Semiconductors (silicon, gallium arsenide): conduct only a little, in a way that can be controlled, which is the basis of electronics. Composites (carbon fibre in resin): combine two or more to get properties that none has alone."},{"n":20,"l":"Start here 3","t":"Atoms, bonds and materials from scratch","h":"5. How atoms arrange in solids","u":"computational/atoms-bonds-and-materials.html#solids","x":"In a crystal the atoms sit in a regular pattern that repeats in all directions, like oranges stacked in a crate. The smallest repeating block is the unit cell. Many common metals take one of three simple patterns: face-centred cubic (aluminium, copper, and iron at high temperature), body-centred cubic (iron at room temperature, tungsten) and hexagonal close-packed (magnesium, titanium). Each has a different density of packing and different ways of deforming, which is why iron and copper behave differently. A glass has no repeating pattern: the atoms are jumbled like a frozen liquid. Many polymers are partly crystalline and partly jumbled. And most real metals are polycrystals: they are made of millions of tiny crystals called grains, each with the atoms tilted differently, meeting along surfaces called grain boundaries. The pattern of grains and other features, visible in a microscope at micrometre scale, is the microstructure, and it is one of the most important things engineers control."},{"n":20,"l":"Start here 3","t":"Atoms, bonds and materials from scratch","h":"6. Imperfections","u":"computational/atoms-bonds-and-materials.html#defects","x":"A perfect crystal would be a poor guide to a real material. Real crystals always contain defects, and defects control many useful properties. A vacancy is an empty site where an atom is missing. At any temperature a few sites are always empty, and vacancies are what allow atoms to move around inside a solid by hopping into the gaps. An interstitial atom is an atom squeezed into the space between the normal sites; small atoms like carbon sit this way in iron. A solute atom (or substitutional atom) replaces a normal atom with a different element. Mixing elements in this way gives an alloy. A dislocation is a line defect: imagine an extra half-sheet of atoms pushed into the crystal, like an extra card in a deck. When a metal bends permanently, dislocations glide through the crystal one row at a time, a bit like moving a ruck across a carpet instead of dragging the whole carpet. This is why metals deform at far smaller forces than you would calculate by pulling whole planes of atoms apart. A grain boundary, as described above, is a surface defect between grains. Try the crystal below: click atoms to create vacancies and solute atoms, and see how many there are. This interactive demo needs JavaScript to run. How many vacancies are there? The fraction of empty sites in equilibrium is e−E/(kT), where E is the energy cost of making one (the exponential from the graphs page, with k and T to be explained soon). For a typical cost of 1 eV at 1000 K that fraction is about 10−5: one site in a hundred thousand. It is tiny but vital, and it rises very steeply with temperature."},{"n":20,"l":"Start here 3","t":"Atoms, bonds and materials from scratch","h":"7. From atoms to properties","u":"computational/atoms-bonds-and-materials.html#properties","x":"Properties that you can measure in the lab come from the atomic picture: Property What controls it at the atomic level Stiffness (how much it stretches under a load) Bond stiffness: steep valleys in the energy curve Melting point Bond strength compared with the thermal jiggling of atoms Electrical conductivity Whether electrons are free to move (metals) or bound (ceramics) Strength and ductility How easily dislocations move and how many obstacles there are Density Atomic mass and how tightly the atoms pack Corrosion resistance Chemical reactions at the surface and the films that form there Take steel, the world’s most important engineering material. Pure iron is soft because dislocations glide easily. Adding a little carbon, which sits between the iron atoms, puts obstacles in the way of dislocations, so the material becomes much stronger. Heat treatment then controls the size of the grains and the tiny particles that form, and the strength can change by a factor of several. Understanding and predicting all this from atoms upward is what computational materials science is about."},{"n":20,"l":"Start here 3","t":"Atoms, bonds and materials from scratch","h":"8. Why simulate","u":"computational/atoms-bonds-and-materials.html#why","x":"We simulate because we cannot see inside a material at the atomic scale in real time, because experiments are slow and costly, and because the number of possible compositions and treatments is far too large to test one by one. A simulation can show a dislocation moving, or tell us whether an alloy will be stable, before anyone makes it. It does not replace experiments; it helps decide which ones to do and explains what they find. Key takeaways Everything is made of atoms; properties come from which atoms there are and how they are arranged and bonded. Bonds balance attraction and repulsion, giving a preferred spacing and a stiffness; deeper bonds mean higher melting points. Crystals are repeating patterns; real crystals contain defects such as vacancies, solute atoms and dislocations. Defects, not perfect order, explain why metals bend, conduct and can be strengthened by alloying. We simulate because we cannot see atoms directly and cannot afford to test every composition."},{"n":20,"l":"Start here 3","t":"Atoms, bonds and materials from scratch","h":"9. Check your understanding","u":"computational/atoms-bonds-and-materials.html#check","x":"How many atoms are in 1 g of aluminium (molar mass 26.98 g/mol)? (1/26.98) × 6.022 × 1023 ≈ 2.2 × 1022 atoms. Why does pulling two bonded atoms slightly apart make them pull back? Beyond the bond length the attraction between the nucleus of one atom and the electrons of the other is stronger than the repulsion, so the net force points back toward the bond length. The force is the slope of the energy curve and always points toward the bottom of the valley. Which type of bond involves atoms sharing pairs of electrons in definite directions? Covalent bonding. This directionality is why covalent solids such as diamond and silicon are hard and brittle. If a material’s bonds are made stronger, what generally happens to its stiffness and melting point? Both tend to increase: a deeper, steeper energy valley resists stretching (more stiffness) and takes more thermal jiggling to overcome (higher melting point). Why are alloys often stronger than the pure metal? The foreign atoms distort the crystal and act as obstacles that dislocations have to push past, so more force is needed to deform the metal permanently. What fraction of atomic sites is vacant at 1000 K if a vacancy costs 1 eV? (Use kT = 0.0862 eV at 1000 K.) The fraction is e−1/0.0862 = e−11.6 ≈ 9 × 10−6, about one site in a hundred thousand."},{"n":20,"l":"Start here 3","t":"Atoms, bonds and materials from scratch","h":"10. Where to go for more","u":"computational/atoms-bonds-and-materials.html#refs","x":"Callister, W. D. and Rethwisch, D. G., Materials Science and Engineering: An Introduction, Wiley (chapters on atomic structure and bonding, crystal structures and imperfections). Ashby, M. F. and Jones, D. R. H., Engineering Materials 1, Butterworth-Heinemann. MIT OpenCourseWare, 3.091 Introduction to Solid State Chemistry (free). Next in the Background level: Materials science essentials, which covers the same ground at university level. Previous topicRates of change: calculus from scratch Next topicForces, energy, heat and motion"},{"n":21,"l":"Start here 4","t":"Forces, energy, heat and motion","h":"Forces, energy, heat and motion","u":"computational/forces-energy-and-heat.html","x":"Topics/Start hereStart here 4 Forces, energy, heat and motion This page covers the physics that molecular simulations are built on: how forces change motion, how energy is stored and exchanged, and why temperature is really a measure of how much atoms are jiggling. Along the way you will see why atoms behave like little masses on springs. Reading timeabout 10 min Builds onS1. Numbers, units, graphs Leads toB2. Physics CategoryStart here After reading this page you should be able to: Use F = ma and the idea of energy conservation in simple problems. Read an energy curve: find equilibrium, stability, and the force. Explain why atoms vibrate, and estimate how fast. Say what temperature and heat are in terms of atoms, and calculate stress and strain. You need: Numbers, units and graphs; Rates of change helps (slope as a rate) but each idea is explained again here."},{"n":21,"l":"Start here 4","t":"Forces, energy, heat and motion","h":"1. Describing motion","u":"computational/forces-energy-and-heat.html#motion","x":"To describe how something moves we use three linked quantities. Position is where it is. Velocity is how fast its position is changing (a speed together with a direction), in metres per second. Acceleration is how fast its velocity is changing, in metres per second per second (m/s2). Each one is the rate of change of the one before, so they are linked by the derivatives of the calculus page. If an object starts with velocity v0 and accelerates steadily at a, its velocity after time t is v = v0 + a t. Near the Earth’s surface, falling objects accelerate downward at g = 9.8 m/s2."},{"n":21,"l":"Start here 4","t":"Forces, energy, heat and motion","h":"2. Forces and Newton’s laws","u":"computational/forces-energy-and-heat.html#forces","x":"A force is a push or a pull. It is measured in newtons (N), where one newton is the force that gives a one-kilogram mass an acceleration of one metre per second per second. Forces have a size and a direction, so they are vectors (arrows). Isaac Newton summarised how forces change motion in three laws: Inertia. An object with no net force on it keeps moving at constant velocity (or stays still). It takes a force to change motion. F = ma. A net force F on a mass m produces an acceleration a = F/m. The same force accelerates a light object more than a heavy one. Action and reaction. When one object pushes on another, the second pushes back with an equal and opposite force. Worked example. A force of 10 N acts on a 2 kg block. Its acceleration is 10/2 = 5 m/s2. If it starts from rest, after 3 s its speed is 5 × 3 = 15 m/s. The second law is the heart of molecular dynamics: if you know the force on every atom you can calculate its acceleration, update its velocity and position a tiny time later, and repeat. Everything depends on knowing the forces, and where the forces come from is the next section."},{"n":21,"l":"Start here 4","t":"Forces, energy, heat and motion","h":"3. Energy","u":"computational/forces-energy-and-heat.html#energy","x":"Energy is the capacity to cause change. It comes in many forms, and the key fact about it is that it can be converted from one form to another but is never created or destroyed: it is conserved. The unit is the joule (J). Two forms matter most here: Kinetic energy is energy of motion: K = ½ m v2. Doubling the speed quadruples the kinetic energy. Potential energy is energy stored because of position or arrangement. Lift a mass m a height h and it stores m g h. Stretch a spring and it stores ½ k x2. The chemical bond between two atoms stores energy depending on their separation. Energy conservation means kinetic plus potential energy stays constant when there is no friction: K + U = constant. Worked example. A ball is dropped from a height of 5 m. At the top it has potential energy m g(5) and no kinetic energy. At the bottom all of it has become kinetic: ½ m v2 = m g(5), so v = √(2 × 9.8 × 5) ≈ 9.9 m/s, whatever the mass. The same idea in mechanical form: when a force moves an object, it does work equal to force times distance, and that work changes the energy. Power is the rate of doing work, in watts (1 W = 1 J/s)."},{"n":21,"l":"Start here 4","t":"Forces, energy, heat and motion","h":"4. Energy landscapes","u":"computational/forces-energy-and-heat.html#landscape","x":"Plot the potential energy against position and you get a curve like a landscape of hills and valleys. A ball released on it rolls downhill. This picture is so useful that it is used for everything from rolling balls to chemical reactions to atoms. A valley bottom is a stable equilibrium: nudge the ball and it rolls back. A hilltop is an unstable equilibrium: the slightest push makes it roll away. A pass between two valleys is a barrier: to get from one valley to the next the ball needs enough energy to climb it. The height of the barrier above the valley decides how likely the crossing is, which is why chemical reactions and atomic jumps need energy to start. The force on the ball is the downhill slope: F = −dU/dx. Steep slope, big force; flat bottom, no force. For atoms the same applies, with many dimensions instead of one: the energy depends on where all the atoms are, and they move toward valleys of that energy landscape. This is the idea behind the bond curve on the atoms page."},{"n":21,"l":"Start here 4","t":"Forces, energy, heat and motion","h":"5. Springs and vibrations","u":"computational/forces-energy-and-heat.html#springs","x":"Stretch a spring and it pulls back; compress it and it pushes out. For many springs, and for atoms displaced a small distance from equilibrium, the force is proportional to the displacement: Hooke’s law, F = −k x. The constant k is the stiffness, in newtons per metre. Its potential energy is ½ k x2, a parabola, matching the bottom of any smooth energy valley. A mass on a spring, once displaced, oscillates back and forth. How fast depends on the stiffness and the mass: a stiffer spring is quicker, a heavier mass is slower. The time for one full cycle, the period, is T = 2π √(m / k), frequency f = 1/T Worked example. For k = 10 N/m and m = 1 kg, T = 2π√(1/10) ≈ 2.0 s, so the frequency is 0.5 hertz (cycles per second). This interactive demo needs JavaScript to run. Now the key observation: atoms bonded together behave like masses joined by springs, because near the bond length the energy curve is a parabola. A typical bond has stiffness about 20 N/m, and a copper atom has a mass of 63.5 atomic mass units (1.05 × 10−25 kg). The same formula then gives a frequency of about 2 trillion vibrations per second (2 THz), a period under a picosecond. Atoms really do shake this fast, which is why simulations of atoms must take time steps of a femtosecond."},{"n":21,"l":"Start here 4","t":"Forces, energy, heat and motion","h":"6. Heat and temperature","u":"computational/forces-energy-and-heat.html#heat","x":"What is temperature, really? Atoms in a solid, liquid or gas are always moving. In a solid they vibrate about fixed positions; in a gas they fly about. Temperature is a measure of the average kinetic energy of that random atomic motion: the hotter something is, the faster its atoms jiggle. Heat is the transfer of that energy from a hotter body to a colder one, as the hot jiggling atoms nudge their cooler neighbours. The link is precise. The average kinetic energy per atom is (3/2) kB T, where T is the temperature in kelvin and kB = 8.617 × 10−5 eV/K is the Boltzmann constant, the conversion between temperature and energy. At room temperature (300 K) the thermal energy kBT is only 0.0259 eV, much smaller than a bond energy of a few eV, which is why solids stay together at room temperature. At absolute zero (0 K, or −273.15 °C) the motion is at its minimum. This explains many everyday facts. Solids expand when heated because bigger vibrations push the average spacing out (a bond curve is steeper on the squeezed side than on the stretched side). They melt when the jiggling becomes so large that atoms break out of their places; a useful rule of thumb is that this happens when atoms move by about a tenth of the atomic spacing. Try it below: raise the temperature until the crystal melts, and move the melting point of the material. This interactive demo needs JavaScript to run."},{"n":21,"l":"Start here 4","t":"Forces, energy, heat and motion","h":"7. States of matter","u":"computational/forces-energy-and-heat.html#phases","x":"At low temperature atoms are held in place (solid); with more thermal energy they slide past each other but stay in contact (liquid); with still more they fly apart (gas). Changing from one state to another takes energy, called latent heat, even though the temperature does not change while it happens: melting ice at 0 °C absorbs 334 kJ per kilogram before the temperature rises again. Materials scientists use the word phase more broadly for any region with uniform structure and composition. Solid iron, for example, has different crystal structures (phases) at different temperatures: body-centred cubic at room temperature and face-centred cubic above 912 °C. Which phases are present, and how fast they change, controls the properties of steel, and predicting this is a major use of simulation."},{"n":21,"l":"Start here 4","t":"Forces, energy, heat and motion","h":"8. Stress and strain","u":"computational/forces-energy-and-heat.html#stress","x":"To describe how a solid object responds to a load, engineers use quantities that do not depend on its size. Stress is the force per unit area, σ = F/A, in pascals (1 Pa = 1 N/m2). Strain is the stretch as a fraction of the original length, ε = ΔL/L, with no unit. For small loads most solids follow a linear law, the material version of Hooke’s law: σ = E ε The constant E is Young’s modulus, the stiffness of the material: about 210 GPa for steel, 70 GPa for aluminium, 3 GPa for nylon. It comes directly from the bond stiffness. If the load goes beyond the yield stress, the deformation becomes permanent (dislocations have started to move); eventually the material fractures. Worked example. A steel rod 1 m long with a cross-section of 100 mm2 (1.0 × 10−4 m2) carries a load of 10 kN. Stress = 10 000 N/1.0 × 10−4 m2 = 1.0 × 108 Pa = 100 MPa. Strain = σ/E = 108/(210 × 109) = 4.8 × 10−4. The rod stretches by 4.8 × 10−4 × 1 m = 0.48 mm, which is invisible to the eye but very real. Key takeaways A force changes motion according to F = ma, and forces on atoms come from the slope of an energy curve. Energy is conserved: it moves between kinetic (motion) and potential (position) forms. Atoms joined by bonds behave like masses on springs, and they vibrate at around ten trillion times a second. Temperature measures the average kinetic energy of atoms; heating a solid makes the atoms jiggle more until it melts. Stress is force per area, strain is relative stretch, and their ratio for a given material is its stiffness."},{"n":21,"l":"Start here 4","t":"Forces, energy, heat and motion","h":"9. Check your understanding","u":"computational/forces-energy-and-heat.html#check","x":"A force of 10 N acts on a 2 kg mass. What is its acceleration? a = F/m = 10/2 = 5 m/s2. A ball is dropped from 5 m. How fast is it going just before it lands (ignoring air resistance)? Energy conservation: ½v2 = g h, so v = √(2 × 9.8 × 5) = 9.9 m/s. What is the period of a 1 kg mass on a spring with stiffness 10 N/m? T = 2π√(m/k) = 2π√0.1 ≈ 1.99 s. How does the average thermal energy per atom at 600 K compare with that at 300 K? It is proportional to temperature (3/2 kBT), so at 600 K it is exactly twice as large. A steel rod (E = 210 GPa) carries a stress of 100 MPa. What is the strain? ε = σ/E = 108/(2.1 × 1011) = 4.8 × 10−4, or 0.048%. Why do solids expand when they are heated? Heating makes atoms vibrate with larger amplitude. Because the bond energy curve is steeper on the squeezed side than on the stretched side, larger vibrations push the average spacing to larger values, so the whole solid grows."},{"n":21,"l":"Start here 4","t":"Forces, energy, heat and motion","h":"10. Where to go for more","u":"computational/forces-energy-and-heat.html#refs","x":"Khan Academy, Physics: one-dimensional motion, forces and Newton’s laws, work and energy (free). Feynman, R. P., Leighton, R. B. and Sands, M., The Feynman Lectures on Physics, volume I (free online): a famous, intuitive treatment of mechanics and heat. Halliday, D., Resnick, R. and Walker, J., Fundamentals of Physics, Wiley (a standard textbook with many exercises). Next in the Background level: Physics for simulation. Previous topicAtoms, bonds and materials from scratch Next topicChance, averages and error bars"},{"n":22,"l":"Start here 5","t":"Chance, averages and error bars","h":"Chance, averages and error bars","u":"computational/chance-averages-and-error-bars.html","x":"Topics/Start hereStart here 5 Chance, averages and error bars Nothing in science is measured exactly, and nothing in a simulation is calculated from a single run. Atoms move randomly, instruments have noise, and simulations use random numbers. This page teaches how to handle chance sensibly: how to average, how to state how sure you are, and how much data you need. Reading timeabout 8 min Builds onS1. Numbers, units, graphs Leads toS6. Your first simulation CategoryStart here After reading this page you should be able to: Calculate simple probabilities and combine them. Find the mean and standard deviation of a set of numbers and explain what they tell you. Explain why the error of an average shrinks as one over the square root of the number of samples. Report a result with an honest uncertainty, and tell random from systematic error. You need: Numbers, units and graphs (square roots and proportionality)."},{"n":22,"l":"Start here 5","t":"Chance, averages and error bars","h":"1. Why chance matters here","u":"computational/chance-averages-and-error-bars.html#why","x":"There are three reasons you will meet chance throughout the series. First, measurements are noisy: measure the same length twice and the numbers differ slightly. Second, atoms move randomly: at any temperature each atom gets knocked about by its neighbours in an unpredictable way, so properties like temperature and pressure are averages over enormous numbers of atoms. Third, many simulation methods deliberately use random numbers (the name Monte Carlo comes from the casino), and any result computed that way carries a statistical error that you must estimate. Knowing how to do this separates a result from a guess."},{"n":22,"l":"Start here 5","t":"Chance, averages and error bars","h":"2. Probability","u":"computational/chance-averages-and-error-bars.html#probability","x":"The probability of an event is a number from 0 (impossible) to 1 (certain) saying how likely it is. When all outcomes are equally likely, the probability is the number of outcomes you want divided by the total number of possible outcomes. A fair coin: the probability of heads is 1/2 = 0.5. A fair die: the probability of a six is 1/6 ≈ 0.17. Independent events (one does not affect the other) multiply. Two heads in two flips: 1/2 × 1/2 = 1/4. Three heads in a row: 1/8. The chance that an event does not happen is 1 minus the chance that it does. At least one head in three flips: 1 − (chance of no heads) = 1 − 1/8 = 7/8. Mutually exclusive outcomes (they cannot both happen) add. The chance of a one or a six on a die: 1/6 + 1/6 = 1/3. The expected value is the average you would get over many repeats. For a fair die it is (1 + 2 + 3 + 4 + 5 + 6)/6 = 3.5, a value you can never roll, but the long-run average of many rolls. Probability does not say what one trial will do, only what happens in the long run, and that is exactly how it is used."},{"n":22,"l":"Start here 5","t":"Chance, averages and error bars","h":"3. Averages and spread","u":"computational/chance-averages-and-error-bars.html#average","x":"Given a set of numbers, two summaries do most of the work. The mean (average) is the sum divided by how many there are. The standard deviation, written σ (sigma) or s, tells you how spread out the numbers are around the mean. Roughly, it is the typical distance of a value from the mean. To calculate it: subtract the mean from each value, square the differences (so negatives do not cancel), add them, divide by one less than the number of values, and take the square root. Worked example. The values 4, 5, 6, 7, 8 have mean (4+5+6+7+8)/5 = 6. The differences are −2, −1, 0, 1, 2; squared: 4, 1, 0, 1, 4; their sum is 10. Dividing by 5 − 1 = 4 gives 2.5, and the square root is σ = 1.58. (We divide by n − 1 instead of n because the mean itself was estimated from the same data, which makes the spread look slightly smaller than it is.) A histogram shows the whole picture: divide the range into bins and count how many values fall into each. A tall narrow histogram means small spread; a low wide one means large spread. The median is the middle value when the data are sorted, which is less affected by a single wild value than the mean is."},{"n":22,"l":"Start here 5","t":"Chance, averages and error bars","h":"4. More samples, smaller error","u":"computational/chance-averages-and-error-bars.html#samples","x":"Suppose each measurement has a random error with spread σ. If you average N independent measurements, the average is a more reliable estimate, because random highs and lows partly cancel. How much better? The uncertainty of the average, called the standard error, is standard error = σ / √N So with σ = 10 and N = 25 measurements, the standard error is 10/5 = 2; with N = 100 it is 1. This square-root law has an important consequence: to cut the error in half you need four times as many measurements, and to cut it by ten you need a hundred times as many. Gains get expensive, so decide how accurate you need to be before starting. The experiment below makes this visible. Each “experiment” flips a fair coin n times and records the fraction of heads; the plot shows 1000 such experiments. With a few flips the results are widely spread; with many flips they crowd close to 0.5, and the width follows the 1/√n prediction. This interactive demo needs JavaScript to run."},{"n":22,"l":"Start here 5","t":"Chance, averages and error bars","h":"5. The bell curve","u":"computational/chance-averages-and-error-bars.html#bell","x":"When you plot the histogram of many random errors, or of averages of many samples, the shape is almost always the same: a symmetric hump called the normal distribution, or bell curve. There is a reason, known as the central limit theorem: when a result is the sum of many small, independent random effects, the total tends to a bell shape whatever the individual effects look like. That is why the bell curve is everywhere. The bell curve has well-known proportions, in terms of the mean and the standard deviation σ: about 68% of values fall within ±1σ of the mean, about 95% within ±1.96σ (often rounded to 2σ), about 99.7% within ±3σ. For an average, use the standard error in place of σ. If you measure a length 100 times and find a mean of 12.34 mm with a standard error of 0.05 mm, then you can say it is 12.34 ± 0.05 mm (68% confidence) or 12.34 ± 0.10 mm (about 95% confidence). Always say which one you mean."},{"n":22,"l":"Start here 5","t":"Chance, averages and error bars","h":"6. Random walks","u":"computational/chance-averages-and-error-bars.html#walk","x":"Here is a chance process that will return in the diffusion topics. A walker stands on a line and at each step moves one step left or right, decided by a coin flip. After N steps, where is the walker? On average, exactly where it started, because left and right are equally likely. But it is not likely to be at the start: the typical distance away grows as the square root of the number of steps, √N. After 10 000 steps the walker is typically about 100 steps from home, not 10 000. This is the same square-root law as for the standard error, and it is no coincidence: the position is the sum of many independent random steps. Many atoms taking random hops produce diffusion, the spreading of one substance into another, and that is why diffusion distance is proportional to the square root of time. You will see a crowd of random walkers produce diffusion in Your first simulation."},{"n":22,"l":"Start here 5","t":"Chance, averages and error bars","h":"7. Reporting honestly","u":"computational/chance-averages-and-error-bars.html#honest","x":"Always give an uncertainty. A number without an error bar cannot be compared with anything. State what it represents (standard deviation, standard error, or a 95% range). Random versus systematic error. Averaging reduces random error but cannot remove a systematic one: a thermometer that reads 3 degrees too high gives a wrong answer however many times you average. In simulations the systematic error might be a poor model; more runs shrink the noise but not the model error, so the answer converges to the wrong number with a very small error bar. Beware of small samples. Three measurements tell you very little about the spread. Be wary of conclusions from a handful of data points. Outliers. A single wildly different value can drag the mean. Investigate it; do not silently throw it away or silently keep it. Correlation is not causation. Two quantities rising together does not mean one causes the other; both may depend on a third. Do not cherry-pick. Report all the runs, not only the one that looked best. Key takeaways Probability measures how likely an outcome is, from 0 (never) to 1 (always); independent chances multiply. The mean says where data centre; the standard deviation says how spread out they are. The error of an average falls as one over the square root of the number of samples: four times as many for half the error. Sums of many small random effects form a bell curve, which is why it appears everywhere. Always report an uncertainty: a number without an error bar is a guess."},{"n":22,"l":"Start here 5","t":"Chance, averages and error bars","h":"8. Check your understanding","u":"computational/chance-averages-and-error-bars.html#check","x":"What is the chance of flipping heads three times in a row with a fair coin? The flips are independent, so multiply: 1/2 × 1/2 × 1/2 = 1/8 = 0.125. Find the mean and standard deviation of 4, 5, 6, 7, 8. Mean 6. Squared deviations sum to 10; 10/(5 − 1) = 2.5; σ = √2.5 = 1.58. Individual measurements have σ = 10. What is the standard error of the average of 25? σ/√N = 10/√25 = 10/5 = 2. You want to cut a standard error from 2 down to 0.5. How many times more data do you need? The error must fall by a factor of 4, and since it falls as 1/√N, you need 42 = 16 times as many measurements. A random walker takes 10 000 steps of one unit, left or right at random. About how far from the start is it likely to be? Typically about √10 000 = 100 units. An instrument reads 3 units too high. You average 1000 readings. What is wrong with the result? The random noise is reduced, but the average is still 3 units too high. That is a systematic error, which averaging cannot remove; the instrument must be calibrated."},{"n":22,"l":"Start here 5","t":"Chance, averages and error bars","h":"9. Where to go for more","u":"computational/chance-averages-and-error-bars.html#refs","x":"Khan Academy, Statistics and probability (free). Taylor, J. R., An Introduction to Error Analysis, University Science Books. Hughes, I. and Hase, T., Measurements and their Uncertainties, Oxford University Press. Next: Your first simulation, and later Mathematics for simulation (probability and statistics at university level). Previous topicForces, energy, heat and motion Next topicYour first simulation"},{"n":23,"l":"Start here 6","t":"Your first simulation","h":"Your first simulation","u":"computational/your-first-simulation.html","x":"Topics/Start hereStart here 6 Your first simulation Time to build one. This page shows that every simulation in the series is made from the same three ingredients, lets you watch diffusion appear from nothing but coin flips, and then writes the same thing in a few lines of code that you can run yourself. Reading timeabout 12 min Builds onS2. Rates of changeS5. Chance and averages Leads toB4. Numerical methodsB5. Programming and workflow CategoryStart here After reading this page you should be able to: Describe the three ingredients of any simulation: state, rule and loop. Explain why smaller time steps are more accurate but cost more, and how to test it. Explain how random hopping produces diffusion, and use D = a²/(2τ). Run a short Python program and apply three basic checks to its result. You need: Rates of change (the idea of a rate) and Chance, averages and error bars (averages, error bars and random walks). No programming experience is assumed."},{"n":23,"l":"Start here 6","t":"Your first simulation","h":"1. The recipe","u":"computational/your-first-simulation.html#recipe","x":"Strip any simulation down and you find three ingredients: A state: the numbers that describe the system at one moment. For the tea it is a single number, the temperature. For a crystal it is the position and velocity of every atom. A rule for how the state changes: the physics. For the tea, “it cools in proportion to how much hotter than the room it is”. For atoms, Newton’s laws with forces from an energy. A loop that applies the rule again and again to advance the state a little at a time, recording what you want. That is all. The sophisticated methods later in the series differ in what the state is and how clever the rule is, but the skeleton is this one."},{"n":23,"l":"Start here 6","t":"Your first simulation","h":"2. Time steps: tea cooling","u":"computational/your-first-simulation.html#timestep","x":"Take the cooling cup of tea from the previous pages. The rule says how fast the temperature changes: dT/dt = −k(T − Troom). A computer does not know calculus; it takes small steps. In each step of length Δt it estimates the change as (rate) × (Δt) and adds it on: Tnew = Told + Δt × [ −k ( Told − Troom ) ] With k = 0.05 per minute, the room at 20 °C and tea starting at 90 °C, a step of 10 minutes gives, by hand: T = 90 − 0.5 × (90 − 20) = 55 °C after the first step, then 37.5, 28.8 and so on. The simulation is easy to do on paper, and the computer simply repeats it. This interactive demo needs JavaScript to run. The orange curve is the exact answer (from the calculus page); the blue dots are the simulation. A big step is clearly inaccurate: each step assumes the rate stays fixed over the whole step, but in reality it keeps changing. Shrinking the step improves it, as the table (from running exactly this simulation) shows. Time step Δt Temperature after 60 min Error (exact answer 23.49 °C) Number of steps 10 min 21.09 °C 2.39 °C 6 5 min 22.22 °C 1.27 °C 12 1 min 23.23 °C 0.26 °C 60 0.1 min 23.46 °C 0.026 °C 600 Look at the pattern: ten times smaller steps give about ten times smaller error, at ten times the cost. This trade-off between accuracy and cost appears in every simulation, and the lesson is always the same: never trust a result until you have checked that it does not change when you shrink the step."},{"n":23,"l":"Start here 6","t":"Your first simulation","h":"3. Random walkers","u":"computational/your-first-simulation.html#walkers","x":"Now a different kind of rule: chance. Imagine a long line of positions and a crowd of walkers. Each time step, every walker flips a coin and moves one step left or right. Nothing else. Orange walkers start on the left half and blue ones on the right, mixed at the boundary. Run the simulation and watch what happens to the boundary. This interactive demo needs JavaScript to run. The sharp edge between orange and blue blurs out, because walkers on the crowded side randomly wander across more often than walkers from the other side wander back. Nobody told the crowd to mix; it follows from nothing but coin flips. This spreading is diffusion, the same process by which a drop of ink spreads in water and carbon atoms spread in steel. The simulation gives you numbers too. The mean squared distance (MSD), the average over walkers of (distance from start)2, grows in proportion to time: after t steps it is about t. In general, with a step length a and a time τ per step, the MSD is (a2/τ) t, and the diffusion coefficient D is defined from it in one dimension by MSD = 2Dt, so D = a² / (2 τ) In the demo a = 1 and τ = 1, so D = 1/2: check the readout."},{"n":23,"l":"Start here 6","t":"Your first simulation","h":"4. Walkers become diffusion","u":"computational/your-first-simulation.html#fick","x":"The dashed curve in the demo is the exact mathematical solution of the diffusion equation (the error function you meet in the diffusion topic), and the walkers follow it closely. Two lessons follow. First, a macroscopic law (concentration smoothing out according to D) can be derived from a microscopic random rule; this is the big idea of statistical physics. Second, the spreading distance grows only as the square root of time, √(2Dt): to spread twice as far you need four times as long. Worked example with real numbers. Suppose an atom in a solid hops to a neighbouring site about once every microsecond (τ = 10−6 s), with a hop length a = 0.25 nm = 2.5 × 10−10 m. Then D = (2.5 × 10−10)2/(2 × 10−6) = 3.1 × 10−14 m2/s. In one second the typical spread is √(2Dt) = √(6.2 × 10−14) = 2.5 × 10−7 m, a quarter of a micrometre. A million hops a second sounds like a lot, but the atom ends up only about a thousand atom-spacings away. That is why diffusion in solids is slow, and why heat treatments take hours."},{"n":23,"l":"Start here 6","t":"Your first simulation","h":"5. The same thing in Python","u":"computational/your-first-simulation.html#python","x":"Time to run code yourself. Python is a programming language, a way of writing instructions that a computer can follow. It is widely used in science because it is readable and has excellent free libraries. How to run it Easiest, nothing to install: open Google Colab (colab.research.google.com) in your browser with a Google account. It gives you a page where you type code into a cell and press the play button. On your own computer: install Miniforge or Anaconda (free), which provides Python and the science libraries; then open JupyterLab or VS Code. Either way, you type code into a cell, run it, and see the result beneath. Mistakes are harmless: read the error message (the last line says what went wrong), fix it and run again. Program 1: the cooling tea T_room = 20.0 # room temperature, deg C k = 0.05 # cooling rate, per minute dt = 10.0 # time step, minutes T = 90.0 # starting temperature t = 0.0 # starting time while t < 60: # the loop: repeat until 60 minutes T = T + dt * (-k * (T - T_room)) # the rule: apply one step t = t + dt print(f\"t = {t:4.0f} min T = {T:5.1f} C\") Lines starting with # are comments for humans. T = T + ... means “calculate the right side, then store it in T”. The output is: t = 10 min T = 55.0 C t = 20 min T = 37.5 C t = 30 min T = 28.8 C t = 40 min T = 24.4 C t = 50 min T = 22.2 C t = 60 min T = 21.1 C The three ingredients are all there: the state (T and t), the rule (the line inside the loop) and the loop itself. Try changing dt to 1.0 and see the last line become about 23.2. Program 2: random walkers import numpy as np # numerical library for arrays rng = np.random.default_rng(1) # random number generator (seed 1) n_walkers, n_steps = 2000, 400 steps = rng.choice([-1, 1], size=(n_walkers, n_steps)) # coin flips: -1 or +1 x = steps.cumsum(axis=1) # position after each step, per walker msd = (x**2).mean(axis=0) # mean squared displacement at each time print(\"MSD after 400 steps:\", msd[-1]) print(\"D estimate:\", msd[-1] / (2 * 400)) This builds a table of 2000 × 400 random ±1 steps in one line, adds them up along each row to get the positions, squares and averages over walkers. With the seed fixed at 1 the output is: MSD after 400 steps: 408.726 D estimate: 0.5109075 The MSD is close to the 400 steps taken, and D comes out near the expected 0.5. To see it, add a plot: import matplotlib.pyplot as plt t = np.arange(1, n_steps + 1) plt.pl"},{"n":23,"l":"Start here 6","t":"Your first simulation","h":"6. Checking a simulation","u":"computational/your-first-simulation.html#checking","x":"A program that runs and prints numbers is not necessarily right. Three habits will catch most problems; use them on everything you write. Compare with a known answer. Here the theory says MSD = t, and the simulation gave 408.7 for t = 400. When the answer is unknown, test a simpler case where it is known (no cooling when the tea is at room temperature; no spreading with one walker per site). Change the random seed and the sample size. A result based on chance varies from run to run. Repeat with different seeds to see how much, and use more walkers to shrink the scatter, just as the standard-error law predicts. The table below is from repeating the walker simulation with 60 different seeds for each size. Change the numerical settings. Halve the time step; use a larger system. If the answer changes substantially, you have not converged (as with the tea above). Number of walkers Average D from 60 runs Run-to-run scatter Prediction 0.5√(2/N) 50 0.53 0.080 0.100 200 0.505 0.052 0.050 800 0.496 0.028 0.025 3200 0.499 0.012 0.013 Each fourfold increase in walkers halves the scatter: the square-root law at work. With few walkers a single run can mislead you by 15% or more, and you could not know without repeating it. Always quote a result with its scatter, as in D = 0.50 ± 0.03."},{"n":23,"l":"Start here 6","t":"Your first simulation","h":"7. Try it yourself","u":"computational/your-first-simulation.html#try","x":"The best way to learn is to change things and see what happens. Suggestions, roughly in order of difficulty: Change n_steps to 100 and 1600. How does the MSD change? (It should equal the number of steps.) Make the walk two-dimensional: each step is up, down, left or right at random. In two dimensions the MSD is still the number of steps, but D is defined by MSD = 4Dt, so D should come out near 0.25. (A version with 2000 walkers and 400 steps gives D = 0.240.) Make the walk biased: choose +1 with probability 0.6 and −1 with probability 0.4 (use rng.choice([-1, 1], p=[0.4, 0.6], size=...)). Now the walkers drift. Plot the mean position against time and find its slope: it should be 0.2 steps per step. Write the tea simulation as a function cool(dt) that returns the final temperature, and call it for several values of dt to draw your own convergence table."},{"n":23,"l":"Start here 6","t":"Your first simulation","h":"8. Where this leads","u":"computational/your-first-simulation.html#leads","x":"The rest of the series is these same ideas applied to real materials. In this page Becomes See State, rule, loop The state is atomic positions and velocities, the rule is Newton’s laws with forces from an energy Molecular dynamics Random rule with averaging Random moves accepted or rejected with probabilities from the energy Monte Carlo Walkers and D = a²/2τ Diffusion coefficients, Arrhenius behaviour, solving the diffusion equation Diffusion and kinetics Time-step accuracy Integrators, stability and convergence Numerical methods Averages and error bars Ensembles, sampling and uncertainty Statistical mechanics Programs and checks Command line, clusters, version control, testing Programming and workflow You are ready for the next level. Mathematics for simulation and Physics for simulation take the ideas of the Start here pages to university level, and Programming and workflow develops what you started here. Key takeaways A simulation needs a description of the state, a rule for how it changes, and a loop that applies the rule step by step. Smaller time steps give more accurate answers at more cost; always check by changing the step. Diffusion emerges from random walkers: the spread grows as the square root of time, and D = a squared over 2 tau in one dimension. Checking against a known answer, changing the random seed and increasing the sample size are the first tests of any simulation. Everything in the Core topics is this same idea with better rules: atoms, energies, grids and probabilities."},{"n":23,"l":"Start here 6","t":"Your first simulation","h":"9. Check your understanding","u":"computational/your-first-simulation.html#check","x":"Name the three ingredients of a simulation and give them for the cooling tea. State: the temperature (and the time). Rule: the temperature changes at a rate proportional to its excess over room temperature. Loop: repeatedly add (rate × time step) to the temperature until the end time. You reduce the time step from 1 min to 0.5 min and the result changes noticeably. What does that tell you? The simulation has not converged: the step is still too large to give an accurate answer. Keep reducing it until the result stops changing (to the accuracy you need). A random walker takes steps of 1 nm every 1 ns. What is its diffusion coefficient in one dimension? D = a2/(2τ) = (1 nm)2/(2 ns) = 0.5 nm2/ns = 0.5 × 10−18/10−9 = 5 × 10−10 m2/s. Why does diffusion spread things out only as the square root of time? Positions are the sum of many independent random steps, and the typical size of such a sum grows only as the square root of the number of steps (as for the standard error of an average). So to double the spread distance you need four times as many steps, that is, four times the time. Your walker simulation gives D = 0.58 with 50 walkers. Should you worry? Not yet. With 50 walkers the run-to-run scatter is about 0.08, so 0.58 is within one scatter of the expected 0.5. Repeat with other seeds and more walkers to see whether the average settles near 0.5 and the scatter shrinks as expected."},{"n":23,"l":"Start here 6","t":"Your first simulation","h":"10. Where to go for more","u":"computational/your-first-simulation.html#refs","x":"Sweigart, A., Automate the Boring Stuff with Python (free online): the friendliest start to programming. Software Carpentry lessons (free online): Python for scientists, the command line and version control. The official Python tutorial (python.org). Berg, H. C., Random Walks in Biology, Princeton University Press (a short, readable book on exactly this idea). Previous topicChance, averages and error bars Next topicMathematics for simulation"},{"n":12,"l":"Background 1","t":"Mathematics for simulation","h":"Mathematics for simulation","u":"computational/mathematics-for-simulation.html","x":"Topics/BackgroundBackground 1 Mathematics for simulation Simulation looks like a wall of equations, but a small set of mathematical ideas does most of the work. This page collects them with one aim: so that when a later topic writes a gradient, an eigenvalue problem or a standard error, you know what it means and why it appears. Reading timeabout 15 min Builds onS1. Numbers, units, graphsS2. Rates of change Leads toB2. PhysicsB4. Numerical methods CategoryBackground After reading this page you should be able to: Read gradients, divergences and Laplacians and connect them to forces, fluxes and conservation laws. Describe what an eigenvalue problem asks and name three places it appears in materials modelling. Classify a differential equation, state what conditions it needs, and estimate its characteristic time. Use Fourier modes, standard errors, least squares and dimensionless groups for quick, honest estimates."},{"n":12,"l":"Background 1","t":"Mathematics for simulation","h":"1. How much maths is needed","u":"computational/mathematics-for-simulation.html#scope","x":"First-year university mathematics (calculus, linear algebra and introductory statistics) is enough to follow every topic in this series. What matters is fluency with a few ideas rather than a long list of techniques. If you are starting from school-level mathematics, read Numbers, units and graphs and Rates of change first: they build the same ideas from scratch. The table maps each idea to where it returns. Idea Where it appears Gradient, divergence, Laplacian Forces from potentials (molecular dynamics), fluxes and diffusion (diffusion), interfaces (phase-field) Eigenvalues and eigenvectors Electronic structure (DFT), vibrations, principal stresses, stability, principal components in data analysis Differential equations Every dynamical and continuum model; the finite element and phase-field methods solve them numerically Fourier modes Reciprocal space and plane waves in DFT, spectral phase-field solvers, diffraction Probability and least squares Sampling and error bars (statistical mechanics, Monte Carlo), fitting, machine learning"},{"n":12,"l":"Background 1","t":"Mathematics for simulation","h":"Derivatives and Taylor series","u":"computational/mathematics-for-simulation.html#taylor","x":"The derivative f′(x) is the slope of a function, and a function near a point can be approximated by its Taylor series: f(x + h) = f(x) + h f′(x) + ½ h² f″(x) + ⅙ h³ f′′′(x) + … Truncating this series is the source of two of the most used approximations in simulation. Near an energy minimum the first derivative vanishes, leaving the harmonic approximation U(x) ≈ U0 + ½k(x − x0)² with stiffness k = U″(x0); and replacing derivatives by differences of nearby values gives the finite-difference methods of the numerical methods page, whose error is set by the first neglected Taylor term. For functions of several variables, partial derivatives ∂f/∂x hold the other variables fixed, and the chain rule links them: df = (∂f/∂x) dx + (∂f/∂y) dy. Thermodynamics is full of partial derivatives (for example μi = (∂G/∂ni) at fixed T and p), and the subscripts say what is held fixed."},{"n":12,"l":"Background 1","t":"Mathematics for simulation","h":"Gradient, divergence and Laplacian","u":"computational/mathematics-for-simulation.html#vector","x":"Three operators built from ∇ = (∂/∂x, ∂/∂y, ∂/∂z) cover most of the continuum physics used here. Operator Acts on Meaning Where it is used Gradient ∇f scalar field Vector pointing uphill with the steepest slope Force F = −∇U; diffusion flux J = −D∇c; chemical-potential gradients Divergence ∇·J vector field Net outflow per unit volume Conservation laws: ∂c/∂t + ∇·J = 0 Laplacian ∇²f = ∇·∇f scalar field How much a value differs from the average of its neighbours (curvature) Diffusion, heat flow, Poisson and Schrödinger equations, gradient-energy terms Curl ∇×v vector field Local rotation Electromagnetism, dislocation theory (less common here) Combining the first two gives Fick’s second law in one line: substituting J = −D∇c into the continuity equation yields ∂c/∂t = ∇·(D∇c). The divergence theorem, ∫V ∇·F dV = ∮S F·n dS, says that what flows out through a surface equals the sum of sources inside. It is the reason conservation laws can be written for a small cell, and the reason the finite-volume and finite-element methods work."},{"n":12,"l":"Background 1","t":"Mathematics for simulation","h":"Integrals worth remembering","u":"computational/mathematics-for-simulation.html#integrals","x":"The Gaussian integral ∫−∞∞ e−x² dx = √π underlies the Maxwell–Boltzmann distribution and the diffusion kernel. Averages over a distribution are integrals, ⟨A⟩ = ∫ A(x) p(x) dx, the form that every ensemble average in statistical mechanics takes. The Dirac delta δ(x) represents an idealised point (a point source, a point charge, a sharp interface) with ∫ δ(x) dx = 1."},{"n":12,"l":"Background 1","t":"Mathematics for simulation","h":"Vectors, matrices and coordinate changes","u":"computational/mathematics-for-simulation.html#vectors","x":"A matrix is a linear map, and matrix multiplication is composition of maps (so, in general, AB ≠ BA). The determinant of a matrix whose columns are three lattice vectors is the cell volume, V = a1·(a2×a3) = det[a1 a2 a3]. Converting fractional coordinates f to Cartesian positions is a matrix product, r = Af, and the reciprocal lattice vectors are the columns of 2π(A−1)T (see crystal structures and periodic cells). A rotation of axes is an orthogonal matrix R with RTR = I and det R = +1, which preserves lengths and angles."},{"n":12,"l":"Background 1","t":"Mathematics for simulation","h":"Eigenvalue problems","u":"computational/mathematics-for-simulation.html#eigen","x":"An eigenvector of a matrix A is a direction that A only stretches, by a factor called the eigenvalue: A v = λ v For a real symmetric matrix the eigenvalues are real and the eigenvectors can be chosen orthogonal, so A = QΛQT with Q orthogonal and Λ diagonal: in the right axes the matrix is just a set of independent stretches. This one structure explains many results: Principal stresses. The stress tensor is a symmetric 3×3 matrix; its eigenvalues are the principal stresses and its eigenvectors the principal directions. The von Mises stress and the maximum shear stress follow from them. Vibrations. For a crystal near equilibrium, Newton’s equations reduce to an eigenvalue problem for the dynamical matrix, with eigenvalues ω² (the squared vibration frequencies) and eigenvectors giving the atomic motions of each normal mode. Electrons. The Schrödinger equation, once expanded in a basis, becomes Hc = Ec (or Hc = ESc with an overlap matrix S); the eigenvalues are the energy levels. Diagonalising this matrix is the main cost of DFT. Stability. At a stationary point of an energy landscape, the eigenvalues of the Hessian matrix of second derivatives decide stability: all positive means a minimum (stable); one negative means a saddle point (a transition state); a negative eigenvalue of the dynamical matrix is an imaginary frequency. Data. Principal component analysis diagonalises a covariance matrix and keeps the directions of largest variance."},{"n":12,"l":"Background 1","t":"Mathematics for simulation","h":"Tensors and index notation","u":"computational/mathematics-for-simulation.html#tensors","x":"A tensor is the generalisation of scalars (rank 0) and vectors (rank 1). Stress σij and strain εij are rank-2 tensors; the elastic stiffness Cijkl linking them is rank 4. With the Einstein convention, a repeated index is summed over: σij = Cijkl εkl (sum over k and l) A rank-4 tensor has 81 components, but symmetry of stress and strain and the existence of a strain energy reduce the number of independent constants to 21 in the most general (triclinic) crystal, 5 for hexagonal and 3 for cubic. The compact Voigt notation writes the symmetric stress and strain as six-component vectors and C as a 6×6 matrix."},{"n":12,"l":"Background 1","t":"Mathematics for simulation","h":"4. Differential equations","u":"computational/mathematics-for-simulation.html#de","x":"A differential equation relates a function to its derivatives. Solving one means finding the function; nearly every model in this series is one."},{"n":12,"l":"Background 1","t":"Mathematics for simulation","h":"Ordinary differential equations","u":"computational/mathematics-for-simulation.html#ode","x":"An ODE has one independent variable, usually time. Two cases cover most needs: First-order decay, dc/dt = −kc, has the solution c = c0e−kt: a process whose rate is proportional to what remains, such as a first-order reaction, relaxation to equilibrium or radioactive decay. The time constant is 1/k. The harmonic oscillator, m d²x/dt² = −kx, has oscillatory solutions x = A cos(ωt + φ) with ω = √(k/m). It describes every small vibration about equilibrium. Newton’s equations for N atoms are 3N coupled second-order ODEs, which is what molecular dynamics integrates in time."},{"n":12,"l":"Background 1","t":"Mathematics for simulation","h":"Partial differential equations","u":"computational/mathematics-for-simulation.html#pde","x":"A PDE has several independent variables (space and time). Three prototypes are enough to recognise the rest: Type Prototype Behaviour Materials example Parabolic ∂c/∂t = D∇²c Smooths and spreads; irreversible in time Diffusion, heat conduction, the Allen–Cahn equation Hyperbolic ∂²u/∂t² = v²∇²u Waves travel at finite speed, with no smoothing Elastic waves, impact and explosion problems Elliptic ∇²φ = −ρ/ε0 Steady state: the answer in one place depends on the whole boundary Electrostatics, steady heat flow, static equilibrium of a solid The Schrödinger equation is parabolic-like in time (with an imaginary coefficient, so it oscillates instead of decaying), and its time-independent form is an eigenvalue problem."},{"n":12,"l":"Background 1","t":"Mathematics for simulation","h":"Conditions that make the problem well posed","u":"computational/mathematics-for-simulation.html#conditions","x":"A PDE alone has infinitely many solutions. A unique one needs initial conditions (the state at t = 0) and boundary conditions on the edge of the domain: Dirichlet (the value is prescribed, such as a fixed temperature or a surface at fixed concentration), Neumann (the flux is prescribed, with zero flux meaning an insulated or symmetry boundary), Robin (flux proportional to the difference from the surroundings, as in convective cooling) and periodic (the domain wraps around, as in a simulation cell)."},{"n":12,"l":"Background 1","t":"Mathematics for simulation","h":"Characteristic times and modes","u":"computational/mathematics-for-simulation.html#timescale","x":"Dividing the equation by its natural scales gives a quick estimate: diffusion across a length L takes a time of order τ ≈ L²/D. More precisely, the diffusion equation has solutions of the form eik·re−Dk²t: a fluctuation of wavenumber k decays at the rate Dk², so short-wavelength features vanish fastest. The same mode analysis shows how a spinodal pattern selects its length scale (see phase-field modelling). Equations with several very different time scales are called stiff, and they strongly constrain numerical methods."},{"n":12,"l":"Background 1","t":"Mathematics for simulation","h":"5. Fourier analysis","u":"computational/mathematics-for-simulation.html#fourier","x":"Any reasonable periodic function can be written as a sum of sines and cosines, or equivalently of complex exponentials. For a function with period L: f(x) = Σn cn ei kn x, kn = 2πn / L, cn = (1/L) ∫0L f(x) e−i kn x dx The coefficient cn says how much of wavenumber kn is present. Three consequences matter later. Derivatives become multiplication. Differentiating eikx gives ikeikx, so ∂/∂x → ik and ∇² → −k². Spectral solvers (the semi-implicit scheme in the phase-field page) exploit this to handle the Laplacian exactly. A periodic simulation cell has a discrete set of wavenumbers, k = 2πn/L, and none smaller than 2π/L. That is the origin of the finite-size limit on correlations, and the same reasoning gives plane-wave basis sets and the Brillouin zone in solids. The fast Fourier transform (FFT) computes all coefficients of N samples in about N log N operations instead of N², which is why plane-wave DFT and spectral methods are practical. Sharp features need many terms: the Fourier series of a square wave overshoots by roughly 9% of the jump height near the discontinuity however many terms are kept (the Gibbs phenomenon), which is why discontinuities cause ringing in spectral and plane-wave methods. Drag the slider to see the partial sums build up. This interactive demo needs JavaScript to run. The Fourier transform is the limit for non-periodic functions, f̂(k) = ∫ f(x)e−ikx dx. Diffraction patterns are the Fourier transform of the electron-density or scattering distribution in the sample, which is how the structure factor links simulated and measured structures."},{"n":12,"l":"Background 1","t":"Mathematics for simulation","h":"Random variables and distributions","u":"computational/mathematics-for-simulation.html#rv","x":"A random variable X has a probability density p(x), a mean μ = ⟨X⟩ and a variance σ² = ⟨(X − μ)²⟩. Four distributions recur: Uniform: all values in a range equally likely; the basis of random-number generators. Gaussian (normal), p(x) = exp[−(x − μ)²/2σ²]/(σ√(2π)): measurement noise, velocities of atoms in equilibrium, and the limit of sums (below). Exponential: waiting times between independent random events with a constant rate R, p(t) = Re−Rt, the basis of kinetic Monte Carlo. Poisson: the number of independent rare events in a fixed interval, such as counted defects or detected photons, with variance equal to the mean."},{"n":12,"l":"Background 1","t":"Mathematics for simulation","h":"Averages and error bars","u":"computational/mathematics-for-simulation.html#clt","x":"The central limit theorem says that the average of N independent samples is approximately Gaussian with mean μ and standard deviation σ/√N. That last quantity is the standard error, and about 95% of the time the true mean lies within ±1.96 standard errors of the sample average. Two practical rules follow: to halve the error bar you need four times as many independent samples, and correlated samples (successive steps of a simulation) count as fewer than N independent ones (see statistical mechanics)."},{"n":12,"l":"Background 1","t":"Mathematics for simulation","h":"Least squares and propagation of error","u":"computational/mathematics-for-simulation.html#fit","x":"Fitting a model f(x; θ) to data (xi, yi) by least squares means choosing the parameters that minimise Σ(yi − f(xi; θ))². For a straight line this has a closed form (the normal equations). A very common use in materials work is the Arrhenius plot: since D = D0exp(−Q/RT), a straight-line fit of ln D against 1/T gives slope −Q/R and intercept ln D0. Always plot the residuals, and quote parameter uncertainties from the fit. If a result f depends on independent measurements xi with uncertainties σi, the uncertainty in f propagates as σf² ≈ Σi ( ∂f/∂xi )² σi² so a quantity that depends exponentially on a barrier amplifies the barrier error: a 0.1 eV error in Q changes exp(−Q/kBT) by a factor of about 3 at 1000 K."},{"n":12,"l":"Background 1","t":"Mathematics for simulation","h":"Bayes’ theorem and Markov chains","u":"computational/mathematics-for-simulation.html#bayes","x":"Bayes’ theorem, P(A|B) = P(B|A)P(A)/P(B), says how to update a belief about A after observing B. It is the logic behind Bayesian fitting and the surrogate models used in active learning. A Markov chain is a random sequence in which the next state depends only on the current one; under suitable conditions it settles into a stationary distribution, which is the idea behind Metropolis Monte Carlo."},{"n":12,"l":"Background 1","t":"Mathematics for simulation","h":"7. Scaling and estimation","u":"computational/mathematics-for-simulation.html#scaling","x":"Before running any calculation, estimate the answer. Dimensional analysis says that physical laws can only depend on dimensionless combinations of the variables. Two appear constantly: the Fourier number Fo = Dt/L² (whether diffusion has had time to act across a length L: values near or above 1 mean yes) and the Biot number Bi = hL/k (whether a body has uniform temperature, or the surface heat transfer limits cooling: Bi below about 0.1 means near-uniform temperature). Writing an equation in dimensionless form reduces the number of independent parameters and shows which effects matter. A small set of orders of magnitude makes estimates routine. Quantity Typical value Interatomic spacing in solids 0.25–0.30 nm Atomic number density of a metal about 6–9 × 1028 m−3 (so about 1011 atoms in 1 µm3) Atomic vibration frequency about 1013 Hz (period about 100 fs) Thermal energy kBT at 300 K 25.9 meV (about 2.5 kJ/mol) Longitudinal sound speed in steel about 5.9 km/s Avogadro number 6.022 × 1023 mol−1 Use them to check feasibility. A cube holding one million atoms of a metal has a side of about 23 nm; the time for diffusion to cross 1 µm with D = 10−14 m²/s is L²/D = 100 s, which already shows why that process needs a coarser model than atoms. Key takeaways Most simulation maths reduces to a few ideas: gradients and divergences, eigenvalue problems, differential equations, Fourier modes and averages over random variables. Forces are gradients of energy (F = -grad U), fluxes are gradients of potentials (J = -D grad c), and conservation laws are divergence equations. Many physical quantities are eigenvalues of matrices: principal stresses, vibration frequencies and electronic energies. A differential equation needs initial and boundary conditions, and its character (diffusive, wave-like, oscillatory) decides the numerical method. Plane waves and the Fourier transform connect real space and reciprocal space, and the standard error of an average falls as one over the square root of the number of independent samples."},{"n":12,"l":"Background 1","t":"Mathematics for simulation","h":"8. Check your understanding","u":"computational/mathematics-for-simulation.html#check","x":"A potential is U(x, y) = x² + 3y². What is the force at (1, 2)? F = −∇U = (−2x, −6y), which at (1, 2) is (−2, −12). The force points toward the minimum at the origin and is larger along y, where the potential is steeper. A diffusion problem has L = 100 µm and D = 10−12 m²/s. About how long does the profile take to relax? τ ≈ L²/D = (10−4)²/10−12 = 104 s, a little under three hours. You average 10,000 correlated samples with a correlation time of 50 steps. What is the standard error compared with σ? The number of independent samples is about N/(2τ) = 10 000/100 = 100, so the standard error is about σ/√100 = σ/10, not σ/100."},{"n":12,"l":"Background 1","t":"Mathematics for simulation","h":"9. References and further reading","u":"computational/mathematics-for-simulation.html#refs","x":"Boas, M. L., Mathematical Methods in the Physical Sciences, Wiley. Riley, K. F., Hobson, M. P. and Bence, S. J., Mathematical Methods for Physics and Engineering, Cambridge University Press. Strang, G., Introduction to Linear Algebra, Wellesley–Cambridge Press. Strang, G., Computational Science and Engineering, Wellesley–Cambridge Press. Kreyszig, E., Advanced Engineering Mathematics, Wiley. Nye, J. F., Physical Properties of Crystals, Oxford University Press (tensors in materials). Sivia, D. S. and Skilling, J., Data Analysis: A Bayesian Tutorial, Oxford University Press. Previous topicYour first simulation Next topicPhysics for simulation"},{"n":13,"l":"Background 2","t":"Physics for simulation","h":"Physics for simulation","u":"computational/physics-for-simulation.html","x":"Topics/BackgroundBackground 2 Physics for simulation Behind every method in this series is a handful of physical principles: Newton’s laws for atoms, electrostatics for charges, the Schrödinger equation for electrons and thermodynamics for what is stable. This page states them in the form the simulation methods use them, and gives the units and constants you need to move between them. Reading timeabout 13 min Builds onB1. MathematicsS4. Forces, energy, heat Leads to3. Statistical mechanics5. Density functional theory CategoryBackground After reading this page you should be able to: Turn a potential energy surface into forces, stiffness, equilibrium positions and vibration frequencies. Explain why Coulomb interactions are long-ranged and what charge neutrality has to do with simulation cells. State the Schrödinger equation, the Pauli principle, the variational principle and Bloch’s theorem, and what each is used for. Convert between eV, kJ/mol, kelvin and terahertz, and recognise the unit systems of common codes. Starting point: This page works at first-year university level. If forces, energy, heat or atoms are new to you, read Forces, energy, heat and motion first; it covers the same ground from scratch."},{"n":13,"l":"Background 2","t":"Physics for simulation","h":"1. The physics behind the methods","u":"computational/physics-for-simulation.html#scope","x":"Principle What it gives Used in Newton’s laws, energy conservation Motion of atoms from forces; vibrations Molecular dynamics Electrostatics Interactions between charges and dipoles Ionic and polar materials, interatomic potentials Quantum mechanics Electron energies, bonding, bands, magnetism Density functional theory Thermodynamics and statistical physics What is stable; averages at finite temperature CALPHAD, sampling methods Wave physics Diffraction and scattering Comparing simulated structures with experiment"},{"n":13,"l":"Background 2","t":"Physics for simulation","h":"Forces, energy and the potential energy surface","u":"computational/physics-for-simulation.html#newton","x":"Newton’s second law, F = ma, together with the fact that interatomic forces derive from a potential energy, F = −∇U, defines the whole of classical atomistic modelling. The function U(r1, …, rN) of all atomic positions is the potential energy surface. Its features have direct physical meaning: a point where the gradient vanishes is an equilibrium; a minimum (all second derivatives positive) is stable; a saddle point with one negative curvature is a transition state between two minima; the depth of a minimum is a binding or cohesive energy and the height of a saddle above it is an activation barrier. Total energy is conserved when only conservative forces act, E = K + U = constant, which is the check used to validate integrators in molecular dynamics. Linear and angular momentum are also conserved for an isolated system; these conservation laws follow from the symmetry of space under translation and rotation."},{"n":13,"l":"Background 2","t":"Physics for simulation","h":"Vibrations and phonons","u":"computational/physics-for-simulation.html#oscillator","x":"Near a minimum, any potential looks like a parabola, so atoms behave like masses on springs. A single oscillator of stiffness k and mass m vibrates with angular frequency ω = √(k/m). For a crystal of N atoms, the coupled equations have 3N independent normal modes, each oscillating at one frequency, found by diagonalising the matrix of second derivatives (the dynamical matrix, see eigenvalue problems). Quantised normal modes are called phonons. For a one-dimensional chain of identical atoms with spacing a and nearest-neighbour stiffness K, the frequency depends on the wavenumber as ω(k) = 2 √(K/m) |sin(ka/2)| which is linear for small k (sound waves, with speed √(K/m)a) and flattens at the edge of the Brillouin zone. Typical atomic vibration frequencies in solids are a few to tens of terahertz, periods of about 0.1 ps, which is why molecular dynamics needs femtosecond steps. Phonons carry heat and set the vibrational free energy."},{"n":13,"l":"Background 2","t":"Physics for simulation","h":"Hamiltonian mechanics","u":"computational/physics-for-simulation.html#hamilton","x":"The Hamiltonian H(q, p) = Σpi²/2mi + U(q) gives the equations of motion in first-order form: dqi/dt = ∂H/∂pi, dpi/dt = −∂H/∂qi The state of the system is a point in phase space of positions and momenta, and a key result (Liouville’s theorem) is that the flow preserves phase-space volume. Numerical integrators that respect this structure (symplectic integrators such as velocity-Verlet) keep the energy from drifting over millions of steps, which is why they are standard in molecular dynamics. The same Hamiltonian appears again in statistical mechanics, where the Boltzmann factor is exp(−H/kBT)."},{"n":13,"l":"Background 2","t":"Physics for simulation","h":"Coulomb interactions","u":"computational/physics-for-simulation.html#coulomb","x":"Two point charges q1 and q2 a distance r apart have potential energy U = q1q2/(4πε0r). In atomistic units, e²/(4πε0) = 14.40 eV·Å, so two unit charges 2 Å apart interact with about 7.2 eV, far more than typical bond energies, which is why ionic solids are strongly bound. The electrostatic potential φ of a charge density ρ obeys Poisson’s equation, ∇²φ = −ρ/ε0 (see partial differential equations)."},{"n":13,"l":"Background 2","t":"Physics for simulation","h":"Why long range matters","u":"computational/physics-for-simulation.html#longrange","x":"Coulomb interactions decay only as 1/r, while the number of charges at distance r grows as r². A direct sum over a periodic crystal therefore converges only conditionally, and truncating at a cutoff gives errors that do not vanish. The remedy is the Ewald summation (1921) and its mesh-based descendants (PPPM, particle-mesh Ewald), which split the interaction into a short-range part summed in real space and a smooth long-range part summed in reciprocal space. For a rock-salt crystal, the result is the Madelung constant, α ≈ 1.7476 for NaCl, which sets the electrostatic energy per ion pair. Practical consequences for simulation cells: they must be charge neutral, a charged defect in a periodic cell needs a compensating background and a correction, and dielectric screening reduces interactions in the real material."},{"n":13,"l":"Background 2","t":"Physics for simulation","h":"Magnetism","u":"computational/physics-for-simulation.html#magnetism","x":"Electrons carry a spin magnetic moment of about one Bohr magneton, μB = eℏ/2me ≈ 5.79 × 10−5 eV/T. Whether the moments of neighbouring atoms line up is governed by the quantum exchange interaction, often modelled by the Heisenberg form E = −JΣSi·Sj. A positive J favours parallel alignment (ferromagnetism, as in iron, cobalt and nickel), and a negative J favours antiparallel (antiferromagnetism). Ferromagnetic order disappears above the Curie temperature (1043 K for iron), and its energy contribution is large enough to change which crystal structure is stable, a point that matters for iron-based alloys."},{"n":13,"l":"Background 2","t":"Physics for simulation","h":"Wavefunctions and the Schrödinger equation","u":"computational/physics-for-simulation.html#schrodinger","x":"An electron is described by a wavefunction ψ(r), with |ψ|² the probability density of finding it near r, so that ∫|ψ|² dV = 1. Allowed energies and states come from the time-independent Schrödinger equation, an eigenvalue problem: Ĥ ψ = E ψ, Ĥ = − (ℏ² / 2m) ∇² + V(r) The first term is the kinetic energy and V the potential. Because the equation has acceptable solutions only for particular energies, energies are quantised. The standard solvable cases build intuition: Particle in a box of width L: En = n²h²/(8mL²), ψn = √(2/L) sin(nπx/L), n = 1, 2, … Confinement raises the energy: for an electron, E1 = 37.6 eV·Å²/L². Harmonic oscillator: En = (n + ½)ℏω, with a non-zero zero-point energy ½ℏω; the origin of quantum effects on light atoms such as hydrogen and of the failure of classical heat capacity at low temperature. Hydrogen atom: En = −13.606 eV/n², with orbitals labelled s, p, d, f by angular momentum. These shapes are the basis of the periodic table and of chemical bonding in molecules and solids. Try the particle in a box: vary the width and the level and watch how confinement sets the energy scale. This interactive demo needs JavaScript to run."},{"n":13,"l":"Background 2","t":"Physics for simulation","h":"Four principles used constantly","u":"computational/physics-for-simulation.html#rules","x":"Spin and the Pauli exclusion principle. Electrons have spin ½, and no two can occupy the same quantum state. Filling levels from the bottom with two electrons each (spin up and down) gives the electronic structure of atoms, the Fermi level of a metal and the occupied bands of a semiconductor. Exchange and correlation. Because electrons are identical fermions, their joint wavefunction changes sign when two are swapped (exchange), and their motion is correlated by Coulomb repulsion. Everything in many-electron theory beyond independent electrons is these two effects, which is the content hidden in the exchange–correlation functional of DFT. The variational principle. For any trial wavefunction, the expectation value of the energy is at least the true ground-state energy, E[ψtrial] ≥ E0. So the best approximation is the one with the lowest energy, which turns quantum mechanics into an optimisation problem; basis-set convergence in a calculation means the energy goes down toward a limit. Born–Oppenheimer approximation. Nuclei are thousands of times heavier than electrons, so the electrons settle instantly into the ground state for any arrangement of nuclei, which defines the potential energy surface of Section 2."},{"n":13,"l":"Background 2","t":"Physics for simulation","h":"Electrons in crystals","u":"computational/physics-for-simulation.html#bands","x":"In a periodic potential, Bloch’s theorem says each eigenstate has the form ψnk(r) = eik·runk(r), with u having the periodicity of the lattice and k a wavevector in the first Brillouin zone. The energies En(k) form bands separated by gaps. Whether the highest filled band is full or partly filled determines whether the material is an insulator or semiconductor (a gap at the Fermi level) or a metal (a partly filled band). The simplest model is free electrons, E = ℏ²k²/2m, filled up to the Fermi energy EF = (ℏ²/2m)(3π²n)2/3 for electron density n. For copper (n ≈ 8.5 × 1028 m−3) this gives about 7 eV, much larger than kBT at any realistic temperature, which is why only electrons near the Fermi level respond to heat and why metals have a small electronic heat capacity. The density of states g(E), the number of states per unit energy, is the quantity that links band structure to properties and to the occupation integrals of DFT. Tunnelling, the penetration of a particle into a classically forbidden region, with probability falling as e−2κa across a barrier of width a, explains scanning tunnelling microscopy and is the quantum correction to hydrogen hopping in metals."},{"n":13,"l":"Background 2","t":"Physics for simulation","h":"5. Thermodynamics","u":"computational/physics-for-simulation.html#thermo","x":"The first law, dU = δQ − δW, states conservation of energy; the second law says the entropy S of an isolated system never decreases, which is why equilibrium means a free-energy minimum rather than an energy minimum. The state functions that matter are the internal energy U, enthalpy H = U + pV, Helmholtz energy F = U − TS and Gibbs energy G = H − TS. In differential form: dG = −S dT + V dp + Σi μi dni so entropy and volume are slopes of G, and the chemical potential μi is the Gibbs energy cost of adding a particle. Response functions follow by differentiation: heat capacity CV = (∂U/∂T)V, thermal expansion α, bulk modulus B and the relation Cp − CV = TVα²B. The third law, S → 0 as T → 0, and the Dulong–Petit limit, C → 3R ≈ 24.9 J mol−1 K−1 for a classical solid, frame what simulations should reproduce at the extremes. The microscopic origin of these quantities (the Boltzmann factor, partition functions, entropy as the logarithm of the number of microstates, S = kB ln Ω) is developed in statistical mechanics for simulation, and the practical computation of equilibrium for alloys in thermodynamics and CALPHAD."},{"n":13,"l":"Background 2","t":"Physics for simulation","h":"6. Waves and diffraction","u":"computational/physics-for-simulation.html#waves","x":"A wave has wavelength λ, wavenumber k = 2π/λ and frequency ν; waves superpose, and the sum of waves scattered by different atoms is large only where they arrive in phase. For a crystal with plane spacing d, this gives Bragg’s law, nλ = 2d sinθ, and in reciprocal-space language the Laue condition that the change in wavevector equals a reciprocal lattice vector, Δk = G (see crystal structures). X-rays from a copper tube have λ = 1.54 Å (photon energy = 12.398 keV·Å/λ), comparable to atomic spacings, which is why they resolve crystal structure; neutrons and electrons work the same way with different sensitivities. For simulation this matters in two ways: the powder diffraction pattern or phonon dispersion of a computed structure can be calculated directly and compared with experiment, and the same wave mathematics (plane waves, Fourier transforms, Brillouin zones) underlies the electronic-structure methods."},{"n":13,"l":"Background 2","t":"Physics for simulation","h":"7. Units and constants","u":"computational/physics-for-simulation.html#units","x":"Simulation codes use different unit systems, and most errors in units are factors of 1.6 × 10−19 or 96 hiding in a conversion. The most useful equivalences are below. Quantity Value Boltzmann constant kB = 8.617 × 10−5 eV/K = 1.381 × 10−23 J/K 1 eV 1.602 × 10−19 J = 96.49 kJ/mol = 23.06 kcal/mol = 11 605 K (as E/kB) Thermal energy at 300 K kBT = 25.85 meV = 2.49 kJ/mol 1 THz 4.136 meV = 33.36 cm−1 = 47.9 K Planck constant ℏ = 6.582 × 10−16 eV·s; hc = 12 398 eV·Å Atomic units Bohr radius 0.5292 Å; Hartree 27.21 eV; Rydberg 13.606 eV Pressure and stress 1 eV/Å3 = 160.2 GPa Atomic mass unit 1.661 × 10−27 kg Common program conventions: DFT codes often use eV and Å (VASP) or Rydberg and Bohr (Quantum ESPRESSO); LAMMPS “metal” units use eV, Å, ps, g/mol and bar. Always write the units of an input and a result next to the number. Key takeaways A potential energy surface turns forces, equilibrium, stability and vibrations into calculus: forces are slopes, stiffness is curvature, and normal modes are eigenvectors. Electrostatics is long-ranged, which is why charged systems need special summation methods and why neutral cells matter. Quantum mechanics enters through the Schroedinger equation, the Pauli principle, the variational principle and Bloch's theorem for periodic solids. Thermodynamics supplies the quantities that simulations are compared with: free energies, chemical potentials and heat capacities. Know your units: 1 eV is about 96.5 kJ/mol, kT is about 0.0259 eV at 300 K, and 1 Hartree is about 27.2 eV."},{"n":13,"l":"Background 2","t":"Physics for simulation","h":"8. Check your understanding","u":"computational/physics-for-simulation.html#check","x":"An interatomic bond has stiffness 20 N/m between atoms of mass 63.5 u. What is the vibration frequency? ω = √(k/m) = √(20/(63.5 × 1.661 × 10−27)) ≈ 1.4 × 1013 rad/s, so ν = ω/2π ≈ 2.2 THz and the period is about 0.45 ps. A time step of 1 fs is a few hundred times shorter than this, which is why molecular dynamics needs such small steps. Why must a simulation cell of ions be charge neutral, and what does a truncated Coulomb sum do wrong? The Coulomb energy of a net-charged periodic system diverges (or depends on arbitrary conventions), so a cell is made neutral or a neutralising background is added. Truncating the 1/r sum at a cutoff ignores a contribution that does not vanish with distance, because the number of charges grows as r², so Ewald or particle-mesh methods are used instead. Estimate the Fermi energy of copper from its electron density of 8.5 × 1028 m−3. kF = (3π²n)1/3 ≈ 1.36 × 1010 m−1, and EF = ℏ²kF²/2m ≈ 1.13 × 10−18 J ≈ 7 eV, some 270 times kBT at room temperature."},{"n":13,"l":"Background 2","t":"Physics for simulation","h":"9. References and further reading","u":"computational/physics-for-simulation.html#refs","x":"Griffiths, D. J., Introduction to Quantum Mechanics, Cambridge University Press. Feynman, R. P., Leighton, R. B. and Sands, M., The Feynman Lectures on Physics, vols. I and III. Kittel, C., Introduction to Solid State Physics, Wiley. Ashcroft, N. W. and Mermin, N. D., Solid State Physics, Saunders. Goldstein, H., Poole, C. and Safko, J., Classical Mechanics, Addison-Wesley. Callen, H. B., Thermodynamics and an Introduction to Thermostatistics, Wiley. Jackson, J. D., Classical Electrodynamics, Wiley. Ewald, P. P., Die Berechnung optischer und elektrostatischer Gitterpotentiale, Ann. Phys. 369, 253 (1921). NIST, CODATA recommended values of the fundamental physical constants (physics.nist.gov/constants). Previous topicMathematics for simulation Next topicMaterials science essentials"},{"n":14,"l":"Background 3","t":"Materials science essentials","h":"Materials science essentials","u":"computational/materials-science-essentials.html","x":"Topics/BackgroundBackground 3 Materials science essentials Simulation models materials, so it helps to be fluent in what they are and how they are described. This page is a compact refresher of the core ideas (bonding, structure, defects, phase transformations, mechanical behaviour, processing and the experiments used to test materials), always pointing to where a simulation method connects. Reading timeabout 13 min Builds onS3. Atoms and materials Leads to1. Introduction CategoryBackground After reading this page you should be able to: Relate bonding type to typical properties and to the interatomic model or electronic method that suits it. Describe the hierarchy of structure from atoms to grains and say which defects control strength and diffusion. Distinguish equilibrium from transformation kinetics and name the main strengthening mechanisms. Read a stress–strain curve, and name the experiment that measures each quantity a simulation predicts. Starting point: This page is a refresher at first-year university level. If atoms, bonds and crystals are new to you, read Atoms, bonds and materials from scratch first."},{"n":14,"l":"Background 3","t":"Materials science essentials","h":"1. The framework","u":"computational/materials-science-essentials.html#framework","x":"Materials science is organised around one chain: processing (how a material is made and treated) determines its structure (arrangement of atoms, defects, grains and phases at every scale); structure determines properties (strength, conductivity, corrosion resistance and so on); and properties determine performance in service. A steel sheet is a good illustration: composition and the thermomechanical schedule (rolling temperature, cooling rate, coiling temperature) set the mix of ferrite, bainite and martensite; that microstructure sets the yield strength and formability; and those set how the part forms and how it absorbs energy in a crash. Link in the chain Typical question Simulation that addresses it Processing → structure Which phases and grain size result from this schedule? CALPHAD, diffusion models, phase-field, process finite elements Structure → properties How strong is this microstructure? How does a defect move? Crystal plasticity, molecular dynamics, DFT Properties → performance Will the component survive the load, heat or environment? Continuum finite elements All links, in reverse Which composition and process would give this property? Machine learning and optimisation"},{"n":14,"l":"Background 3","t":"Materials science essentials","h":"2. Bonding","u":"computational/materials-science-essentials.html#bonding","x":"Everything starts with how atoms hold together. The bond type largely decides the properties and, importantly for simulation, which interatomic model or electronic method is appropriate. Bond Nature Examples and cohesive energy Typical consequences Suitable models Metallic Valence electrons delocalised over all atoms, non-directional Cu 3.5, Fe 4.3, W 8.9 eV/atom Ductile, conducting, usually close-packed or body-centred cubic structures Embedded-atom potentials; DFT Covalent Electrons shared in directional bonds Si 4.6, diamond 7.4 eV/atom Hard, brittle, open structures, semiconducting Tersoff and Stillinger–Weber potentials; DFT Ionic Electron transfer; electrostatic attraction between ions NaCl, MgO, Al2O3 (lattice energy of NaCl about 8 eV per ion pair) Hard, brittle, insulating, high melting points Coulomb plus short-range potentials; DFT+U van der Waals Weak attraction from fluctuating dipoles Noble gases, graphite interlayers, polymer chain packing; 0.01–0.1 eV Low melting points, soft, layered sliding Lennard-Jones; dispersion-corrected DFT Hydrogen bond Attraction between H and an electronegative atom Water, proteins, polyamides; 0.1–0.4 eV Structure of water and many polymers Specialised force fields All bonds share one shape of energy curve: repulsion at short distance, attraction at long, and a minimum at the equilibrium spacing r0 with depth equal to the bond energy. The curvature at the minimum is the bond stiffness k, so macroscopic stiffness follows from it: Young’s modulus is roughly E ≈ k/r0, giving about 80 GPa for k = 20 N/m and r0 = 0.25 nm, the right order for metals. The asymmetry of the curve (it is steeper on the repulsive side) is the origin of thermal expansion, and deeper wells mean higher melting points and stiffer solids."},{"n":14,"l":"Background 3","t":"Materials science essentials","h":"3. Structure at many scales","u":"computational/materials-science-essentials.html#structure","x":"Properties depend on structure at every scale from the atomic to the component, so simulations are matched to the scale that controls the property. Scale Features Typical size Atomic Crystal lattice or amorphous arrangement; point defects; dislocation cores 0.1–2 nm Nano Precipitates, clusters, solute atmospheres, thin films 1–100 nm Micro Grains, phases, inclusions, pores, dislocation networks 0.1–100 µm (steel sheet grains: 5–20 µm) Macro Component, weld, casting, residual-stress field mm–m Most engineering materials are polycrystals: aggregates of crystalline grains with different orientations separated by grain boundaries. The distribution of grain orientations is the texture, which makes properties direction-dependent (anisotropy): rolled sheet forms differently along and across the rolling direction. Other classes differ in structure. Glasses (and metallic glasses) lack long-range order but keep a regular short-range arrangement; polymers are long chain molecules, with crystalline and amorphous regions and a glass transition temperature; ceramics are mostly ionic or covalent compounds, usually made by sintering powders into polycrystals; composites combine phases (fibres in a matrix) to get properties neither has alone. A central empirical result links scales: the yield strength of a polycrystal rises as the grain size d falls, as described by the Hall–Petch relation: σy = σ0 + k d−1/2 where σ0 is the friction stress for dislocation motion and k measures how hard it is to pass slip across a boundary. For steels k is of order 0.5–0.7 MPa m1/2."},{"n":14,"l":"Background 3","t":"Materials science essentials","h":"4. Defects and strengthening","u":"computational/materials-science-essentials.html#defects","x":"A perfect crystal is a poor model of a real material: the useful properties of metals come largely from their defects. Point defects (vacancies, interstitials, substitutional solutes) exist in equilibrium at a concentration that rises exponentially with temperature (see crystal structures); vacancies are what lets substitutional atoms diffuse. Dislocations are line defects, characterised by the Burgers vector b (the magnitude and direction of the lattice distortion), with edge, screw and mixed character. Plastic deformation is the glide of dislocations on close-packed planes under shear stress, at stresses far below the theoretical shear strength of a perfect crystal. The dislocation density ρ (line length per volume) is about 1010–1012 m−2 in annealed metals and 1014–1016 m−2 in heavily deformed ones. Planar defects: grain boundaries, stacking faults, twin boundaries, phase boundaries and free surfaces. Volume defects: pores, inclusions and second-phase particles. Strengthening a metal means hindering dislocation motion. The main mechanisms are summarised below. Mechanism How it works Scaling Solid-solution strengthening Solute atoms distort the lattice and interact with dislocations Increases with solute concentration (roughly as a power of 1/2 to 2/3) Grain-boundary strengthening Boundaries block slip Hall–Petch: ∝ d−1/2 Work (strain) hardening Dislocations multiply and obstruct each other Taylor: Δσ = αMGb√ρ, with α ≈ 0.2–0.5 and Taylor factor M ≈ 3 Precipitation (dispersion) strengthening Fine particles are cut by or bowed around by dislocations Orowan bowing: Δτ ≈ Gb/L, with L the particle spacing Transformation strengthening Hard, fine product phases such as martensite or bainite Depends on carbon content and transformation temperature Here G is the shear modulus. These mechanisms combine, which is the basis of alloy design, and each ties to a modelling scale: solute–dislocation binding by DFT, dislocation mobility by molecular dynamics, precipitate evolution by phase-field and grain-level plasticity by crystal plasticity."},{"n":14,"l":"Background 3","t":"Materials science essentials","h":"5. Phases and transformations","u":"computational/materials-science-essentials.html#transformations","x":"A phase is a region of uniform composition and structure. The equilibrium phases for a given composition and temperature are what a phase diagram shows (the calculation is in thermodynamics and CALPHAD), but real processing is rarely at equilibrium, so which microstructure forms depends on kinetics. The main transformation types are: Nucleation and growth (diffusional). A new phase forms as small nuclei that need to overcome an energy barrier, then grows by atoms moving across the interface; the rate is set by diffusion and by the driving force. Examples: precipitation, pearlite, ferrite growth from austenite. Diffusionless (displacive). The lattice changes by a coordinated shear faster than atoms can diffuse, so composition is unchanged. The classic example is martensite in steel, formed on rapid cooling, which is hard because carbon remains trapped. Spinodal decomposition. A solution inside the spinodal separates continuously, with no barrier (see phase-field modelling). Order–disorder and magnetic transitions. Changes in the arrangement of species or spins with no change of lattice. Because outcomes depend on the cooling path, engineers use time–temperature–transformation (TTT) and continuous-cooling transformation (CCT) diagrams, which show how long a transformation takes to start and finish at each temperature or cooling rate; a critical cooling rate must be exceeded to produce martensite. The overall kinetics are often described by the Johnson–Mehl–Avrami–Kolmogorov equation (see diffusion and kinetics). Heat treatment exploits all of this: annealing softens by recovery, recrystallisation and grain growth; normalising refines grains; quenching traps non-equilibrium phases; tempering relaxes quenched structures by precipitating carbides; ageing forms fine precipitates, as in the Al–Cu system, where the sequence of Guinier–Preston zones and metastable precipitates gives peak strength before the equilibrium phase coarsens."},{"n":14,"l":"Background 3","t":"Materials science essentials","h":"Stress and strain","u":"computational/materials-science-essentials.html#stress-strain","x":"Engineering stress is σ = F/A0 (force over the original area) and engineering strain ε = ΔL/L0. When strains are large, true quantities account for the changing size: σtrue = σ(1 + ε) and εtrue = ln(1 + ε), valid until necking. A tensile test produces the curve from which most properties are read. Elastic region. Reversible; σ = Eε (Hooke’s law). The Poisson ratio ν = −εtransverse/εaxial (about 0.3 for metals) links the moduli: G = E/[2(1 + ν)] and K = E/[3(1 − 2ν)]. Yield and plasticity. Permanent deformation starts at the yield strength. Afterwards the flow stress rises by strain hardening, often described by σ = Kεn (Hollomon), up to the ultimate tensile strength, where necking begins (Considère criterion: at a true strain equal to n for a power law). Ductility is the strain to fracture. Hardness, resistance to local indentation, is roughly three times the yield strength for most metals."},{"n":14,"l":"Background 3","t":"Materials science essentials","h":"Fracture, fatigue and creep","u":"computational/materials-science-essentials.html#failure","x":"Fracture. Brittle fracture occurs when a crack grows at stresses below yield; the Griffith/Irwin result is σf = KIc/(Y√(πa)) for a crack of length a, with fracture toughness KIc a material property and Y a geometry factor. BCC metals show a ductile–brittle transition with temperature. Fatigue. Failure under repeated loading at stresses well below yield; described by S–N curves and the Paris law for crack growth, da/dN = C(ΔK)m. Creep. Slow deformation under constant load at high temperature, controlled by diffusion, with steady-state rate ε̇ = Aσnexp(−Q/RT)."},{"n":14,"l":"Background 3","t":"Materials science essentials","h":"Typical values","u":"computational/materials-science-essentials.html#typical","x":"Approximate room-temperature values give a feel for the range; real values depend strongly on composition and processing. Material Young’s modulus (GPa) Yield strength (MPa) Density (g/cm3) Pure aluminium, annealed 70 20–35 2.70 Aluminium alloy 7075-T6 71 about 500 2.81 Mild steel about 210 about 250 7.85 Advanced high-strength steels about 210 700 to over 1000 7.85 Titanium alloy Ti–6Al–4V about 114 about 880 4.43 Copper, annealed about 120 about 70 8.96 Alumina ceramic about 370 brittle (bending strength 300–400) 3.9 Nylon (polymer) 2–3 about 50–80 1.14 Note that the modulus of a given material class barely changes with processing (it is set by bonding), whereas yield strength changes by an order of magnitude (it is set by microstructure). That contrast is why DFT is good at moduli and why strength needs multiscale modelling."},{"n":14,"l":"Background 3","t":"Materials science essentials","h":"7. Other properties","u":"computational/materials-science-essentials.html#functional","x":"Electrical. Set by the band structure (see physics for simulation): metals conduct (copper resistivity about 1.7 × 10−8 Ω·m), semiconductors have a gap of order 1 eV that doping controls, insulators have gaps above about 3 eV. Thermal. Heat is carried by electrons and phonons: conductivity is about 400 W/(m·K) for copper, 235 for aluminium, 50 for typical steels and above 1000 for diamond. Thermal expansion coefficients are about 12 × 10−6 K−1 for steel, 23 × 10−6 for aluminium and about 1.2 × 10−6 for Invar, an iron–nickel alloy whose magnetism cancels the expansion. Magnetic. Ferromagnets (Fe, Co, Ni) have aligned moments below their Curie temperatures; soft magnets are easy to magnetise and used in transformers, hard magnets resist demagnetisation. Corrosion and oxidation. Electrochemical or chemical reactions with the environment, governed by thermodynamic stability (Pourbaix and Ellingham diagrams) and by the kinetics of transport through any surface film."},{"n":14,"l":"Background 3","t":"Materials science essentials","h":"8. Processing routes","u":"computational/materials-science-essentials.html#processing","x":"Route What it controls Where simulation enters Casting and solidification Grain structure, segregation, porosity Heat flow and solidification path (FEM with CALPHAD); dendrite growth by phase-field Wrought processing (rolling, forging, extrusion) Shape, texture, dislocation density, recrystallisation Large-strain finite elements, crystal plasticity Heat treatment Phases, precipitates, hardness Diffusion and transformation models, thermal–mechanical FEM Powder metallurgy and sintering Density, grain size Diffusion, kinetic Monte Carlo, phase-field Additive manufacturing Rapid solidification, residual stress, defects Moving-heat-source FEM, solidification models Welding and joining Heat-affected zone, distortion, residual stress Thermal–metallurgical–mechanical FEM Coatings and thin films Thickness, texture, stress, interfaces Kinetic Monte Carlo, molecular dynamics"},{"n":14,"l":"Background 3","t":"Materials science essentials","h":"9. Characterisation","u":"computational/materials-science-essentials.html#characterisation","x":"Simulations earn trust by being compared with measurements, so know what each technique reports. Technique What it measures Scale Compare with X-ray and neutron diffraction Phases, lattice parameters, texture, strain Average over a volume Computed diffraction patterns, lattice constants from DFT SEM and EBSD Grain size, orientation, phase maps 0.1 µm–mm Phase-field and crystal-plasticity microstructures TEM and STEM Dislocations, precipitates, interfaces at atomic resolution 0.1–100 nm Atomistic defect and interface structures Atom probe tomography Three-dimensional composition, segregation, clusters atomic to 100 nm Monte Carlo segregation, precipitation models Tensile and compression tests Modulus, yield, hardening, ductility Specimen Finite elements, crystal plasticity Nanoindentation and hardness Local modulus and hardness nm–µm Molecular dynamics, finite elements Calorimetry and dilatometry Transformation temperatures, latent heat, expansion Specimen CALPHAD and transformation kinetics Tracer and couple diffusion experiments Diffusion coefficients, activation energies µm DFT with transition-state theory, kinetic Monte Carlo Key takeaways Processing sets structure, structure sets properties, and properties set performance; simulation can enter at every link. Bonding type (metallic, covalent, ionic, van der Waals) decides which interatomic model or electronic method is appropriate. Defects control most useful properties: dislocations govern strength, vacancies govern diffusion, and boundaries govern grain-size effects. Phase diagrams give the equilibrium; transformation kinetics (nucleation, growth, diffusion) decide which microstructure actually forms. Know what each experiment measures (diffraction, microscopy, tensile tests, calorimetry), because that is what a simulation must be compared with."},{"n":14,"l":"Background 3","t":"Materials science essentials","h":"10. Check your understanding","u":"computational/materials-science-essentials.html#check","x":"Why are metals usually ductile and covalent solids such as silicon brittle at room temperature? Metallic bonding is non-directional, so atoms can slip past each other and dislocations glide easily. Covalent bonds are directional, so moving a dislocation requires breaking strong bonds and the material cracks first. A steel obeys Hall–Petch with k = 0.6 MPa m1/2. How much stronger does refining the grain size from 20 µm to 5 µm make it? Δσ = k(5 × 10−6)−1/2 − k(20 × 10−6)−1/2 = 0.6 × (447 − 224) ≈ 134 MPa, roughly 130 MPa stronger. Which simulation method would you use first to estimate the driving force for precipitating a carbide at 500 °C, and why? CALPHAD: it gives the Gibbs energy change for forming the phase from the current matrix composition at that temperature, which is the thermodynamic input to nucleation and growth models, at a tiny cost compared with atomistic methods."},{"n":14,"l":"Background 3","t":"Materials science essentials","h":"11. References and further reading","u":"computational/materials-science-essentials.html#refs","x":"Callister, W. D. and Rethwisch, D. G., Materials Science and Engineering: An Introduction, Wiley. Ashby, M. F. and Jones, D. R. H., Engineering Materials 1: An Introduction to Properties, Applications and Design, Butterworth-Heinemann. Porter, D. A., Easterling, K. E. and Sherif, M. Y., Phase Transformations in Metals and Alloys, CRC Press. Bhadeshia, H. K. D. H. and Honeycombe, R., Steels: Microstructure and Properties, Butterworth-Heinemann. Hull, D. and Bacon, D. J., Introduction to Dislocations, Butterworth-Heinemann. Courtney, T. H., Mechanical Behavior of Materials, Waveland Press. Dieter, G. E., Mechanical Metallurgy, McGraw-Hill. Hall, E. O., The deformation and ageing of mild steel, Proc. Phys. Soc. B 64, 747 (1951); Petch, N. J., The cleavage strength of polycrystals, J. Iron Steel Inst. 174, 25 (1953). Taylor, G. I., The mechanism of plastic deformation of crystals, Proc. R. Soc. A 145, 362 (1934). Previous topicPhysics for simulation Next topicNumerical methods essentials"},{"n":15,"l":"Background 4","t":"Numerical methods essentials","h":"Numerical methods essentials","u":"computational/numerical-methods-essentials.html","x":"Topics/BackgroundBackground 4 Numerical methods essentials Every simulation is a numerical approximation to a mathematical model. This page covers the ideas that decide whether the numbers a program prints can be trusted: how computers do arithmetic, how errors arise and grow, how to choose steps and grids, and what scales badly. Reading timeabout 12 min Builds onB1. MathematicsS6. Your first simulation Leads to6. Molecular dynamics7. Monte Carlo and kMC8. Diffusion and kinetics10. Finite elements CategoryBackground After reading this page you should be able to: Explain machine precision, round-off and cancellation, and when they matter. Distinguish discretisation, round-off and iteration error, and measure the order of convergence of a method. Choose among root-finding, optimisation, linear-solver and time-integration methods for a given problem. Estimate cost and parallel speed-up before launching a large run. Starting point: This page assumes the basic idea of a simulation and some calculus. If you have not met them, read Your first simulation and Rates of change first."},{"n":15,"l":"Background 4","t":"Numerical methods essentials","h":"1. Finite-precision arithmetic","u":"computational/numerical-methods-essentials.html#floating","x":"Computers store real numbers as floating-point values: a sign, an exponent and a fixed number of significant bits. Most scientific code uses IEEE 754 double precision: 64 bits, of which 52 are the stored fraction (53 significant bits with the implicit leading one), giving about 16 decimal digits and a range of roughly 10−308 to 10308. The spacing of numbers near 1 is the machine epsilon, εmach = 2−52 ≈ 2.2 × 10−16; single precision has about 7 digits (ε ≈ 1.2 × 10−7), which is much faster on GPUs but needs care. Three consequences appear constantly: Most decimals are not exact. 0.1 has no finite binary expansion, so 0.1 + 0.2 evaluates to 0.30000000000000004 and equality tests between floating-point numbers should use a tolerance (for example numpy.isclose). Catastrophic cancellation. Subtracting nearly equal numbers loses leading digits: (1 + 10−16) − 1 evaluates to 0. Formulas that subtract close quantities (small energy differences, variances computed as ⟨x²⟩ − ⟨x⟩²) should be rearranged. Order of summation matters. Adding a tiny number to a large one drops it; summing millions of terms accumulates error, so parallel and serial runs can differ in the last digits. Algorithms such as pairwise or compensated (Kahan) summation reduce the error. A result should therefore never be trusted beyond about 16 digits, and usually far fewer after many operations. Overflow and underflow (exponents leaving the representable range) are rare in physical units but common in exponentials such as exp(−E/kBT) at large arguments, where working with logarithms avoids them."},{"n":15,"l":"Background 4","t":"Numerical methods essentials","h":"2. Errors, order and stability","u":"computational/numerical-methods-essentials.html#errors","x":"Four kinds of error separate the model from the number on the screen: Error Source How to reduce or test it Modelling error The equations or potential do not describe reality exactly Validation against experiment Discretisation (truncation) error Replacing derivatives, integrals and continuous fields by finite steps and grids Refine the step or mesh and watch the result converge Round-off error Finite-precision arithmetic Reformulate; use higher precision; check sensitivity Iteration error Stopping an iterative solver before it has converged Tighten tolerances; monitor residuals A method has order of accuracy p if its discretisation error scales as hp with the step h. Halving h then cuts the error by 2p, and the order can be measured from two runs: p ≈ log(eh/eh/2)/log 2. If the measured order is lower than the theory says, there is usually a bug or a non-smooth feature. Richardson extrapolation combines runs at two step sizes to estimate the converged value and the remaining error. Accuracy is not enough: two further properties matter. Stability means errors do not grow without bound as the calculation proceeds (an explicit scheme with too large a time step does not converge to anything). Conditioning is a property of the problem itself: a problem is ill-conditioned if small changes in the input cause large changes in the answer. For a linear system Ax = b, the condition number κ(A) = ‖A‖‖A−1‖ bounds the amplification of relative errors, and one loses roughly log10κ digits. A well-posed problem needs a solution that exists, is unique and depends continuously on the data."},{"n":15,"l":"Background 4","t":"Numerical methods essentials","h":"Finite differences","u":"computational/numerical-methods-essentials.html#diff","x":"Taylor series (see mathematics for simulation) give difference formulas for derivatives with known error: forward: f′(x) ≈ [f(x+h) − f(x)]/h, error ≈ ½ h f″ (first order) central: f′(x) ≈ [f(x+h) − f(x−h)]/2h, error ≈ ⅙ h² f′′′ (second order) Reducing h shrinks this truncation error, but the numerator involves subtracting nearly equal numbers, so round-off error grows as εmach/h. The total error therefore has a minimum at an intermediate step: about √εmach ≈ 10−8 for forward differences and εmach1/3 ≈ 6 × 10−6 for central differences in double precision. The plot shows the characteristic V shape; switch to single precision to see the whole curve shift. This interactive demo needs JavaScript to run."},{"n":15,"l":"Background 4","t":"Numerical methods essentials","h":"Integration","u":"computational/numerical-methods-essentials.html#quad","x":"Numerical quadrature replaces an integral by a weighted sum. The trapezoid rule is second order, Simpson’s rule fourth order, and Gauss–Legendre quadrature with n points integrates polynomials up to degree 2n − 1 exactly; the latter is what finite-element codes use inside each element. For high-dimensional integrals (such as ensemble averages over 3N coordinates) grids fail, but Monte Carlo integration converges as σ/√N regardless of dimension (see Monte Carlo methods)."},{"n":15,"l":"Background 4","t":"Numerical methods essentials","h":"Solving f(x) = 0","u":"computational/numerical-methods-essentials.html#rootfind","x":"Bisection halves a bracketing interval each step; slow (linear convergence) but guaranteed if the function changes sign. Newton’s method, xn+1 = xn − f(xn)/f′(xn), roughly squares the error at each step near a simple root (quadratic convergence: 3 digits, then 6, then 12), but needs a good starting guess and fails where f′ is near zero. In many dimensions the derivative becomes the Jacobian matrix and each step solves a linear system; this is the Newton–Raphson iteration of nonlinear finite elements. Secant and Brent methods avoid derivatives; Brent combines bisection’s safety with fast convergence and is the usual library choice."},{"n":15,"l":"Background 4","t":"Numerical methods essentials","h":"Minimising a function","u":"computational/numerical-methods-essentials.html#optimise","x":"Finding structures (relaxation to a minimum of the energy), fitting parameters and training models are all optimisation. Gradient descent steps along −∇f; for an ill-conditioned landscape (a long narrow valley) it zigzags and converges slowly at a rate set by the condition number. Conjugate gradients and quasi-Newton methods (BFGS, and its limited-memory form L-BFGS) use information from previous steps and converge much faster; for atomic relaxation, the damped-dynamics method FIRE is popular because it is robust and cheap. All of them find a local minimum. Global searches (basin hopping, simulated annealing, evolutionary algorithms, random structure search) run many local optimisations from different starting points."},{"n":15,"l":"Background 4","t":"Numerical methods essentials","h":"5. Linear algebra in practice","u":"computational/numerical-methods-essentials.html#linear","x":"Most numerical methods eventually reduce to a linear system or an eigenvalue problem, so the cost of linear algebra decides what is feasible. Task Method Cost for n unknowns Notes Solve Ax = b, dense LU factorisation (Cholesky if symmetric positive definite) about (2/3)n3 operations (half for Cholesky) Use library routines (LAPACK), never invert a matrix explicitly Solve, sparse Sparse direct solvers Depends on structure; much less than n3 Finite-element and finite-difference matrices are sparse Solve, large sparse Conjugate gradients (symmetric positive definite), GMRES (general), with preconditioning, or multigrid Per iteration, about the cost of one matrix–vector product Convergence depends on the condition number; a good preconditioner is crucial All eigenpairs, dense QR algorithm order n3 Limits dense diagonalisation to a few thousand dimensions A few eigenpairs, large Lanczos, Davidson, LOBPCG Many matrix–vector products What plane-wave DFT codes use for the lowest bands The n3 growth of dense algorithms is why conventional DFT is limited to hundreds or a few thousand atoms: doubling the system multiplies the cost of the dense steps by eight."},{"n":15,"l":"Background 4","t":"Numerical methods essentials","h":"6. Integrating ODEs","u":"computational/numerical-methods-essentials.html#ode","x":"To advance dy/dt = f(y, t) in time: Explicit Euler, yn+1 = yn + h f(yn), is first order and unstable for oscillations (it adds energy to a harmonic oscillator at every step), so it is a teaching tool, not a production one. Runge–Kutta methods, in particular the classic fourth-order RK4 and adaptive embedded pairs (such as Dormand–Prince, used by scipy.integrate.solve_ivp), give high accuracy for smooth problems. Verlet and leapfrog integrators are second order and symplectic: they preserve the structure of Hamiltonian dynamics, so the energy error stays bounded over very long runs. They are the standard for molecular dynamics. Implicit methods (backward Euler, BDF) solve an equation at every step but are stable for much larger steps, which is essential for stiff problems with widely separated time scales, such as carbon diffusing millions of times faster than manganese. For the test equation dy/dt = −λy, explicit Euler is stable only if hλ ≤ 2, so the largest stable step is set by the fastest process in the system, however little that process matters to the answer."},{"n":15,"l":"Background 4","t":"Numerical methods essentials","h":"7. Discretising PDEs","u":"computational/numerical-methods-essentials.html#pde","x":"A PDE is turned into algebra by sampling space on a grid or mesh (see diffusion and finite elements). The main families: Finite differences replace derivatives by differences on a regular grid: simple and fast, best for simple geometry. Finite volumes balance fluxes across the faces of cells, so conserved quantities are conserved exactly; the standard for fluid flow and transport. Finite elements use a weak form and piecewise polynomial functions on unstructured meshes; the standard for solid mechanics and complex geometry. Spectral methods expand in Fourier or polynomial modes; extremely accurate for smooth periodic problems (see Fourier analysis). Time stepping adds stability limits. For explicit diffusion the dimensionless number r = DΔt/Δx² must not exceed 1/2 in one dimension (1/4 in two, 1/6 in three); for wave and advection problems the Courant–Friedrichs–Lewy (CFL) condition requires that the numerical domain of dependence contains the physical one, cΔt/Δx ≤ 1 with c the wave speed. Implicit schemes remove these limits at the price of solving a linear system each step, and the Crank–Nicolson scheme is second order in time. Always run a mesh-convergence and a time-step convergence study; and test against a problem with a known analytic solution (the method of manufactured solutions constructs one for any equation)."},{"n":15,"l":"Background 4","t":"Numerical methods essentials","h":"8. Random numbers","u":"computational/numerical-methods-essentials.html#random","x":"Computers produce pseudo-random numbers with deterministic algorithms: the Mersenne Twister (period 219937 − 1) and the PCG family (NumPy’s default) are good for scientific use. Fixing the seed makes a run reproducible, which is essential for debugging and for reporting results; different seeds provide the independent replicas used for error bars. For parallel runs, give each process its own independent stream (for example with NumPy’s SeedSequence.spawn), never the same seed. Other distributions come from uniform numbers. The inverse-transform method maps a uniform value u through the inverse of the cumulative distribution (an exponential waiting time is −ln(u)/R, the step of kinetic Monte Carlo); the Box–Muller transform produces Gaussian pairs from two uniform values; rejection sampling draws from a simple envelope and keeps points under the target curve."},{"n":15,"l":"Background 4","t":"Numerical methods essentials","h":"9. Cost, parallelism and scaling","u":"computational/numerical-methods-essentials.html#performance","x":"Before a large calculation, estimate its cost from how it scales with size: a pair-force loop without neighbour lists scales as N2, with cell lists as N; the FFT as N log N; dense diagonalisation as N3. A code may be limited by arithmetic (compute-bound) or by moving data from memory (memory-bound, typical of sparse and grid-based codes), and optimising the wrong one wastes effort. Vectorised library calls (NumPy, BLAS and LAPACK) are far faster than explicit loops in interpreted languages. Parallel machines combine threads within a node (OpenMP), message passing between nodes (MPI) and GPUs. Two scaling tests describe how well a code uses them: strong scaling (fixed problem, more processors) and weak scaling (problem grows with the processor count). Amdahl’s law limits strong scaling: if a fraction p of the work parallelises perfectly, S(N) = 1 / [ (1 − p) + p/N ] → 1/(1 − p) as N → ∞ so a code that is 95% parallel can never run more than 20 times faster, however many processors are used. Measure with a profiler before optimising, test on a small case first, and note that communication and input/output often dominate at large processor counts. Key takeaways Computers use finite-precision arithmetic: double precision keeps about 16 digits, and subtracting nearly equal numbers destroys most of them. Every numerical answer has modelling, discretisation, round-off and iteration error; convergence tests separate them. Stability and conditioning matter as much as accuracy: an explicit scheme can blow up, and an ill-conditioned problem amplifies small errors. Newton's method converges quadratically near a root; optimisers find local, not global, minima. Cost scaling decides what is feasible: dense linear algebra grows as N cubed, and Amdahl's law limits parallel speed-up."},{"n":15,"l":"Background 4","t":"Numerical methods essentials","h":"10. Check your understanding","u":"computational/numerical-methods-essentials.html#check","x":"Why does making the step h ever smaller eventually make a finite-difference derivative worse? The truncation error falls as a power of h, but the numerator subtracts nearly equal values, so round-off error grows like εmach/h. Their sum has a minimum at an intermediate h, and shrinking h beyond it increases the total error. A solver gives an error of 10−3 at h = 0.1 and 2.5 × 10−4 at h = 0.05. What is its order? The error fell by a factor of 4 when h halved, so p = log24 = 2: second order. A code is 95% parallelisable. What is the best possible speed-up, and what speed-up on 64 processors? The limit is 1/(1 − 0.95) = 20. On 64 processors, S = 1/(0.05 + 0.95/64) ≈ 15.4."},{"n":15,"l":"Background 4","t":"Numerical methods essentials","h":"11. References and further reading","u":"computational/numerical-methods-essentials.html#refs","x":"Heath, M. T., Scientific Computing: An Introductory Survey, SIAM. Press, W. H., Teukolsky, S. A., Vetterling, W. T. and Flannery, B. P., Numerical Recipes, Cambridge University Press. Trefethen, L. N. and Bau, D., Numerical Linear Algebra, SIAM. Golub, G. H. and Van Loan, C. F., Matrix Computations, Johns Hopkins University Press. Higham, N. J., Accuracy and Stability of Numerical Algorithms, SIAM. LeVeque, R. J., Finite Difference Methods for Ordinary and Partial Differential Equations, SIAM. Hairer, E., Lubich, C. and Wanner, G., Geometric Numerical Integration, Springer. Nocedal, J. and Wright, S. J., Numerical Optimization, Springer. Goldberg, D., What every computer scientist should know about floating-point arithmetic, ACM Comput. Surv. 23, 5 (1991). Bitzek, E. et al., Structural relaxation made simple, Phys. Rev. Lett. 97, 170201 (2006). Matsumoto, M. and Nishimura, T., Mersenne Twister, ACM Trans. Model. Comput. Simul. 8, 3 (1998). Previous topicMaterials science essentials Next topicProgramming and workflow"},{"n":16,"l":"Background 5","t":"Programming and workflow","h":"Programming and workflow","u":"computational/programming-and-workflow.html","x":"Topics/BackgroundBackground 5 Programming and workflow Computational work is as much about organisation as about equations: running many calculations, keeping track of what produced which result, and being able to repeat it months later. This page covers the practical skills that make that routine: the command line, a scripting language, version control, clusters and testing. Reading timeabout 11 min Builds onS6. Your first simulation Leads to11. Machine learning CategoryBackground After reading this page you should be able to: Move around and automate tasks from the command line, including simple loops and pipes. Read data, fit and plot it in Python with NumPy, SciPy and Matplotlib. Keep a project under version control and describe a reproducible folder layout. Submit and manage jobs on a cluster scheduler, and test a piece of your own code. Starting point: If you have never programmed, start with Your first simulation, which introduces Python step by step and runs in a browser, then come back here for the working habits."},{"n":16,"l":"Background 5","t":"Programming and workflow","h":"1. What you need","u":"computational/programming-and-workflow.html#scope","x":"You do not need to be a software engineer. A modest, well-chosen set of tools covers nearly all work in this series. Skill Why Typical tools Command line Simulation codes and clusters are driven from a text terminal bash or zsh, ssh, rsync A scripting language Build inputs, run sweeps, analyse and plot output Python with NumPy, SciPy, Matplotlib Version control History, collaboration, recovery from mistakes git with GitHub or GitLab Cluster use Large runs need many cores Slurm or PBS, MPI, modules Reproducibility habits Results must be repeatable and traceable Environments, containers, notes, seeds"},{"n":16,"l":"Background 5","t":"Programming and workflow","h":"2. The command line","u":"computational/programming-and-workflow.html#shell","x":"A shell reads commands, runs programs and glues them together. On Linux and macOS it is built in; on Windows, the Windows Subsystem for Linux (WSL) provides one. Everything is addressed by paths: absolute paths start at the root (/home/you/project), relative ones from the current directory, with . for the current and .. for the parent directory. The most useful commands: Task Commands Navigate and list pwd, cd, ls -l Create, copy, move, delete mkdir, cp, mv, rm (deletion is permanent: there is no recycle bin) Look at files cat, less, head, tail, wc -l Search grep (text inside files), find (files by name or date) Compare diff, sort, uniq Remote machines ssh to log in; scp and rsync to copy files Processes top, ps, kill Two ideas give the shell its power. A pipe sends the output of one command into the next, and > redirects output to a file. A loop repeats a command for many cases. Together they turn a morning of clicking into a line: # last total energy printed in a VASP OUTCAR file grep \"free energy TOTEN\" OUTCAR | tail -1 # make one folder per temperature for T in 300 600 900; do mkdir T$T; done Environment variables (such as PATH, the list of places searched for programs) and modules (module load lammps on most clusters) control which software versions you get. Python and other packages are managed in isolated environments (python -m venv, or conda / mamba), so that one project’s libraries do not break another’s."},{"n":16,"l":"Background 5","t":"Programming and workflow","h":"3. Python for science","u":"computational/programming-and-workflow.html#python","x":"Python is the usual glue language: readable, with a large scientific ecosystem. The core is small. Lists and dictionaries hold data, loops and functions structure the work, and f-strings format output. The scientific stack adds: NumPy: fast arrays with vectorised operations (apply a formula to a million numbers without writing a loop), slicing and broadcasting (arrays of different shapes combine by stretching the smaller one). SciPy: linear algebra, optimisation, integration, interpolation, statistics and ODE solvers (see numerical methods). Matplotlib: plots; pandas: tables of mixed data; Jupyter notebooks for exploring and documenting. Materials-specific libraries: ASE and pymatgen (structures and workflows), pycalphad (thermodynamics), matminer and scikit-learn (machine learning). A typical job reads data, computes, writes a result and plots it. This script fits the Arrhenius law (see diffusion and kinetics) to tabulated diffusivities and then evaluates it: import numpy as np kB = 8.617333e-5 # eV/K D0, Q = 2.0e-6, 0.87 # m^2/s, eV (illustrative values) T = np.linspace(500, 1000, 6) # K D = D0 * np.exp(-Q / (kB * T)) # vectorised: no loop needed for t, d in zip(T, D): print(f\"{t:6.0f} K D = {d:.2e} m^2/s\") # fit ln D against 1/T: the slope is -Q/kB slope, intercept = np.polyfit(1 / T, np.log(D), 1) print(\"recovered Q =\", -slope * kB, \"eV\") And turning a molecular-dynamics mean-squared displacement into a diffusion coefficient (see molecular dynamics) takes four lines. Conversions are a classic source of mistakes: 1 Å²/ps = 10−8 m²/s. import numpy as np data = np.loadtxt(\"msd.dat\") # columns: time (ps), MSD (A^2) t, msd = data[:, 0], data[:, 1] slope, intercept = np.polyfit(t, msd, 1) # MSD = 6 D t in three dimensions print(f\"D = {slope / 6 * 1e-8:.3e} m^2/s\")"},{"n":16,"l":"Background 5","t":"Programming and workflow","h":"4. Units and data formats","u":"computational/programming-and-workflow.html#data","x":"Unit systems differ between programs (see physics for simulation), so write the unit next to every number: in column headers, variable names and file comments. File formats matter for exchange and long-term use. Format Holds Notes CIF Crystal structures from databases and journals Text; the standard for experimental structures POSCAR, XYZ and extended XYZ Atomic positions (and cell, forces, energies in extended XYZ) Simple text; ASE converts between formats LAMMPS data and dump files Starting structures and trajectories for molecular dynamics Text; large trajectories use binary or compressed formats CSV and plain columns Tables of numbers Easy to read anywhere; add a header with units JSON and YAML Settings, parameters and metadata Human-readable, structured; ideal for configuration HDF5 and NetCDF Large numerical arrays with attributes Binary, self-describing, fast; used for big simulation output"},{"n":16,"l":"Background 5","t":"Programming and workflow","h":"5. Version control","u":"computational/programming-and-workflow.html#git","x":"Git records the history of a project as a series of snapshots (commits), each with a message saying why it changed. It lets you return to any earlier state, try ideas on separate branches, compare versions with git diff, and share work through a remote such as GitHub or GitLab. A basic session: git init # start tracking this folder git add run.py in.lammps # choose what goes in the next snapshot git commit -m \"Add vacancy-formation input and run script\" git status # what has changed git log --oneline # the history git switch -c larger-cell # try something on a new branch git push # share it What to track: scripts, input files, notes, small data and the instructions to rebuild the rest. What to leave out: large outputs (trajectories, wavefunctions), temporary files, binaries and anything secret such as passwords or access keys; list them in a .gitignore file and describe how to regenerate them in the README. Write commit messages that say why, make commits small and focused, and tag the commit that produced each published figure."},{"n":16,"l":"Background 5","t":"Programming and workflow","h":"6. Running on a cluster","u":"computational/programming-and-workflow.html#cluster","x":"You log in to a login node to prepare and submit work (never run heavy calculations there) and a scheduler, most often Slurm, places your job on compute nodes when resources are free. A job is described by a batch script whose #SBATCH lines request resources: #!/bin/bash #SBATCH --job-name=cu_vac #SBATCH --nodes=1 #SBATCH --ntasks-per-node=32 #SBATCH --time=02:00:00 #SBATCH --output=%x_%j.out module load lammps srun lmp -in in.vacancy Submit it with sbatch job.sh, watch it with squeue -u $USER, cancel with scancel <jobid> and review finished jobs with sacct. A job array runs the same script for many parameter values, for example ten temperatures: #SBATCH --array=0-9 T=$((300 + 100 * SLURM_ARRAY_TASK_ID)) # 300, 400, ... 1200 K srun lmp -in in.melt -var T $T Good practice on shared machines: Test small first, then measure how the run time scales with core count before requesting hundreds of cores (see parallel scaling). Ask for what you need. Requests far above the need wait longer in the queue; a time limit set too short kills the job. Allow a safety margin of 20–30% on measured run time. Checkpoint long runs (write restart files regularly) so a failure costs hours, not days. Mind the file systems. Fast scratch storage is for running jobs and is often purged; home or project storage is for keeping results. Copy what matters off scratch, and compress or delete bulky intermediate files. Read the error first. The usual failures are out of memory, exceeded time limit, a missing module or a wrong path, and the scheduler and program logs say which."},{"n":16,"l":"Background 5","t":"Programming and workflow","h":"7. Workflows and reproducibility","u":"computational/programming-and-workflow.html#workflow","x":"A reproducible result is one that you, or someone else, can regenerate from recorded inputs. That needs organisation more than clever tools. A predictable folder layout, one project per directory with separate places for inputs, scripts, raw output and results. A common structure: inputs/, scripts/, runs/<case>/, results/, notes.md. Scripts, not clicks. Generate input files and run sweeps from a script, so changing a parameter and re-running is one command. Notebooks are excellent for exploring; move stable steps into scripts. Record the environment: code versions, library versions (pip freeze, conda env export), the potential or pseudopotential files used, the random seeds, and the units. Containers (Docker, Apptainer) freeze a whole software stack. Name things consistently, with parameters in directory names (Cu_vac_4x4x4_T300) or, better, recorded in a metadata file next to the output. Workflow managers (Snakemake, AiiDA, signac, jobflow) automate dependencies between steps and store provenance; they pay off once a project has hundreds of calculations. Share and archive the inputs and scripts behind a paper in a repository that issues a persistent identifier (Zenodo, Materials Cloud). Data that are findable, accessible, interoperable and reusable (the FAIR principles) are worth more than data that are merely posted."},{"n":16,"l":"Background 5","t":"Programming and workflow","h":"8. Testing your code","u":"computational/programming-and-workflow.html#testing","x":"Research code is easy to get quietly wrong. Cheap checks catch most errors before they reach a paper: Test against known answers: analytic solutions, limiting cases, textbook numbers, a published benchmark. Check invariants: conserved energy or mass, symmetry, correct units, results that must be positive or monotonic. Automate it with unit tests that run in one command, so a later change that breaks something is noticed at once. With pytest, a test is a function whose name starts with test_: import numpy as np def arrhenius(T, D0, Q, kB=8.617333e-5): return D0 * np.exp(-Q / (kB * T)) def test_arrhenius_limits(): assert np.isclose(arrhenius(1e12, 2.0, 0.9), 2.0, rtol=1e-6) # very high T gives D0 assert arrhenius(300, 2.0, 0.9) < arrhenius(600, 2.0, 0.9) # rises with temperature Compare floating-point numbers with a tolerance, never with ==. Keep a small regression test that reruns a quick case and compares with a stored result, so changes in a library version or a refactor that alter results are flagged."},{"n":16,"l":"Background 5","t":"Programming and workflow","h":"9. Debugging and habits","u":"computational/programming-and-workflow.html#habits","x":"Read the error message from the bottom up. The last line says what failed, the lines above say where. Make a minimal example that reproduces the problem; half the time the bug is found while shrinking it. Print, log or plot intermediate values and look at them; use a debugger (pdb, or the one in your editor) for harder cases. Change one thing at a time, and keep the working version in version control. Profile before optimising (python -m cProfile): the slow part is rarely where you expect. Write for the next reader: clear names, short functions, a README that says how to run the project and what each file is. The next reader is usually you, six months later. Back up anything that cannot be regenerated, on more than one machine. For viewing results, common tools are VESTA and OVITO (structures and defects), ParaView (fields and meshes) and Matplotlib (plots), with VS Code, PyCharm or a terminal editor for writing code. Key takeaways A scripting language (Python with NumPy, SciPy and Matplotlib) plus the command line covers most day-to-day computational work. Keep inputs, scripts and notes under version control; keep large outputs elsewhere and record how they were made. On a cluster, test small, request only the resources you need, use job arrays for parameter sweeps and checkpoint long runs. Reproducibility means recorded versions, seeds, units and inputs so that someone else, or you in a year, can rerun the work. Test your own code against analytic solutions and simple limits before trusting it on real problems."},{"n":16,"l":"Background 5","t":"Programming and workflow","h":"10. Check your understanding","u":"computational/programming-and-workflow.html#check","x":"What does grep \"TOTEN\" OUTCAR | tail -1 do? grep prints every line of the file OUTCAR containing “TOTEN”; the pipe passes those lines to tail -1, which keeps only the last one, here the final total energy of a calculation. A calculation takes about 3 hours when measured. What time limit would you request, and why not 48 hours? About 4 hours: a margin of 20–30% protects against variation without wasting the allocation. A 48-hour request would wait much longer in the queue, block resources and can be rejected by queue limits, and it gives no benefit. Which files belong in a git repository for a simulation project, and which do not? Track scripts, input files, notes and small reference data, plus instructions to regenerate everything else. Leave out large outputs such as trajectories and wavefunctions, temporary files, binaries and secrets; record in the README how to recreate them."},{"n":16,"l":"Background 5","t":"Programming and workflow","h":"11. References and further reading","u":"computational/programming-and-workflow.html#refs","x":"Wilson, G. et al., Best practices for scientific computing, PLoS Biol. 12, e1001745 (2014). Wilson, G. et al., Good enough practices in scientific computing, PLoS Comput. Biol. 13, e1005510 (2017). Harris, C. R. et al., Array programming with NumPy, Nature 585, 357 (2020). Virtanen, P. et al., SciPy 1.0: fundamental algorithms for scientific computing in Python, Nat. Methods 17, 261 (2020). Hunter, J. D., Matplotlib: a 2D graphics environment, Comput. Sci. Eng. 9, 90 (2007). Larsen, A. H. et al., The Atomic Simulation Environment, J. Phys.: Condens. Matter 29, 273002 (2017). Wilkinson, M. D. et al., The FAIR guiding principles for scientific data management and stewardship, Sci. Data 3, 160018 (2016). Chacon, S. and Straub, B., Pro Git (free online). Shotts, W., The Linux Command Line, No Starch Press. Previous topicNumerical methods essentials Next topicIntroduction to computational materials science"},{"n":1,"l":"Topic 1","t":"Introduction to computational materials science","h":"Introduction to computational materials science","u":"computational/introduction.html","x":"Topics/FoundationsTopic 1 Introduction to computational materials science Computational materials science uses physical models and numerical methods to predict, explain and design the structure, processing and properties of materials. No single model covers every scale, so the field is a toolbox of methods, each valid over a limited range of length, time and chemistry. Reading timeabout 20 min Builds onB3. Materials essentials Leads to2. Crystals and cells3. Statistical mechanics CategoryFoundations After reading this page you should be able to: Place any simulation method on a length–time map and state what it does and does not resolve. Describe the physical basis, inputs, outputs and main approximations of each major method family. Explain how methods are linked into multiscale workflows (ICME). Distinguish verification, validation and uncertainty quantification, and apply basic convergence checks. Pick a sensible first method for a given materials question, and find software and data to start with."},{"n":1,"l":"Topic 1","t":"Introduction to computational materials science","h":"1. Scope and purpose","u":"computational/introduction.html#scope","x":"The central problem of materials science is the processing–structure–properties–performance chain: how a material is made determines its structure at every scale, structure determines properties, and properties determine how it performs in service. Computation can be applied to any link in this chain. In the forward direction it predicts properties from a given structure or composition. In the inverse direction it searches for compositions and processes that deliver a target property. What simulation adds to experiment Access to what cannot be measured: atomic-scale mechanisms, short-lived intermediates, internal fields during deformation, temperature or composition in places no probe can reach. Speed and cost: thousands of virtual compositions can be screened before a single melt is made. Isolation of variables: a simulation can change one parameter, such as one solute or one boundary type, while holding everything else fixed, which real samples rarely allow. Interpretation: models give a mechanism that explains why a measured trend occurs, which is what allows extrapolation beyond tested conditions. What simulation cannot do Every model contains approximations and needs inputs, and the output is no better than either. Models are usually most trustworthy for trends and for ranking candidates, and least trustworthy for absolute numbers on complex real material, which has defects, impurities, residual stress and a processing history the model may not represent. A prediction is a hypothesis to be tested, not a measurement."},{"n":1,"l":"Topic 1","t":"Introduction to computational materials science","h":"What to know first","u":"computational/introduction.html#background","x":"The series assumes undergraduate-level familiarity with a handful of subjects. If any feel rusty, the Background pages refresh them with the simulation use in mind: mathematics (vector calculus, eigenvalues, differential equations, Fourier analysis, statistics), physics (mechanics, electrostatics, quantum mechanics, thermodynamics, units), materials science essentials (bonding, structure, defects, transformations, mechanical behaviour), numerical methods (precision, stability, solvers, scaling) and programming and workflow (command line, Python, git, clusters). The topic map shows which core topics lean on which background page. Integrated computational materials engineering ICME is the practice of linking models across scales, together with experiments and databases, to shorten the path from composition and process to component performance. The US Materials Genome Initiative (launched 2011) pushed the related idea of combining computation, experiment and data to cut the time needed to bring new materials to use."},{"n":1,"l":"Topic 1","t":"Introduction to computational materials science","h":"2. Length and time scales","u":"computational/introduction.html#scales","x":"Material behaviour arises from structure at many scales at once: electrons bond atoms, atoms form defects, defects interact to form microstructure, and microstructure controls the bulk response. Cost grows steeply as a model resolves finer detail, so a method covers only a window of scales. The table summarises the windows; values are order-of-magnitude and depend on hardware and implementation. Level Length Time Representative methods Typical outputs Electronic 0.1–2 nm; up to a few hundred atoms routinely, thousands with effort Static, or picoseconds for ab initio molecular dynamics Density functional theory, quantum chemistry Energies, forces, elastic constants, bonding, defect formation energies, band structure Atomistic 1 nm to about 100 nm; millions to billions of atoms on large machines Picoseconds to microseconds Molecular dynamics, Metropolis Monte Carlo Diffusion coefficients, interface energies, dislocation and crack behaviour, thermal properties Thermodynamic No spatial scale (bulk, equilibrium) Equilibrium, or simple kinetics CALPHAD Phase diagrams, phase fractions and compositions, driving forces, transformation temperatures Mesoscale 10 nm to about 1 mm Microseconds to hours Phase-field, kinetic Monte Carlo, dislocation dynamics, crystal plasticity, cellular automata Microstructure evolution, grain and phase morphology, texture, grain-level stress and strain Continuum Micrometres to metres Seconds to years Finite element, finite volume, computational fluid dynamics Temperature, flow, stress, strain, distortion, residual stress, component response Data-driven Any Any Regression, neural networks, Gaussian processes Fast surrogate predictions, screening, inverse design Length windows of the main methods on a logarithmic axis, with the time each can reach. The windows overlap, which is what makes linking them possible. Two gaps matter in practice. First, atomistic time scales (up to microseconds) are many orders of magnitude shorter than the time scales of diffusion-controlled processing (seconds to hours). Second, atomistic strain rates are typically 107 s−1 or higher, against about 10−3 s−1 in a tensile test. Bridging these gaps is a main reason for coarse-grained, mesoscale and accelerated-dynamics methods."},{"n":1,"l":"Topic 1","t":"Introduction to computational materials science","h":"3.1 Density functional theory (DFT)","u":"computational/introduction.html#dft","x":"Basis. The Hohenberg–Kohn theorems (1964) show that the ground-state energy of an interacting electron system is a unique functional of the electron density, and the Kohn–Sham approach (1965) replaces the many-body problem by non-interacting electrons moving in an effective potential: [ −½∇² + vext(r) + vH(r) + vxc(r) ] ψi(r) = εi ψi(r), n(r) = Σi |ψi(r)|² Here vext is the nuclear potential, vH the classical electron–electron (Hartree) term, and vxc the exchange–correlation potential, which contains all the remaining many-body physics and is the one quantity that must be approximated. The equations are solved self-consistently because n(r) enters the potential that determines it. Approximations to choose. The local density approximation (LDA) uses the exchange–correlation energy of a uniform electron gas; generalised gradient approximations (GGA, for example PBE) add dependence on the density gradient; meta-GGAs (for example SCAN) add kinetic-energy density; hybrid functionals (for example HSE06) mix in part of exact exchange at higher cost. Other practical choices are the pseudopotential or PAW dataset, the plane-wave cutoff energy, the k-point mesh for Brillouin-zone sampling, smearing for metals, spin polarisation, and corrections such as DFT+U for localised d and f states or van der Waals terms for layered and molecular materials. Outputs. Total energies (hence formation energies, defect energies, surface and interface energies, migration barriers by nudged elastic band), forces and stresses (hence relaxed structures, elastic constants, phonons), electronic density of states and band structure, magnetic moments. Cost and limits. Conventional Kohn–Sham DFT scales roughly with the cube of the system size, which limits cells to hundreds or a few thousand atoms. Standard GGA tends to underestimate band gaps of semiconductors and insulators, and treats strongly correlated systems poorly. Results are normally at 0 K and for perfect periodic cells, so finite-temperature and long-time behaviour need additional steps. Magnetism can decide the answer: LDA famously predicts the wrong ground-state structure of iron, which GGA corrects."},{"n":1,"l":"Topic 1","t":"Introduction to computational materials science","h":"3.2 Molecular dynamics (MD)","u":"computational/introduction.html#md","x":"Basis. Atoms are treated as classical particles that move according to Newton’s equations under forces derived from an interatomic potential U: mi d²ri/dt² = Fi = −∇i U(r1, …, rN) The equations are integrated in small time steps (typically about 1 fs, shorter if light atoms such as hydrogen are involved; the step must resolve the fastest vibration). Statistical-mechanical ensembles are imposed with thermostats and barostats: NVE (constant energy), NVT (constant temperature) and NPT (constant temperature and pressure). Properties are time or ensemble averages over the trajectory. Interatomic potentials are the decisive input. Lennard–Jones and Morse are simple pair potentials. The embedded-atom method (EAM, Daw and Baskes, 1984) and modified EAM include an environment-dependent term and are standard for metals. Stillinger–Weber and Tersoff describe directional covalent bonding (silicon, carbon). ReaxFF allows bond breaking and forming. A growing class of machine-learned potentials (for example Gaussian approximation, moment tensor and neural-network potentials) is fitted to DFT data and approaches DFT accuracy at far lower cost, within the chemical and structural range they were trained on. Outputs. Diffusion coefficients from mean-squared displacement, thermal expansion, melting point, free-energy differences, elastic and thermal properties, dislocation and crack mechanisms, grain boundary structure and energy, radiation damage cascades. Limits. The result is only as good as the potential, which may be wrong outside the data it was fitted to. Time scales stop at microseconds and strain rates are extreme. Electrons are absent, so electronic and magnetic effects are missing unless built into the potential."},{"n":1,"l":"Topic 1","t":"Introduction to computational materials science","h":"3.3 Monte Carlo methods","u":"computational/introduction.html#mc","x":"Monte Carlo (MC) samples configurations randomly instead of following physical trajectories. In Metropolis MC (1953), a trial change from state a to state b is accepted with probability P(a→b) = min[ 1, exp( −(Eb − Ea) / kBT ) ] so that the configurations visited follow the Boltzmann distribution. Because moves need not be physical, MC can equilibrate quantities that MD reaches too slowly, such as chemical ordering, segregation of solutes to interfaces, or composition in multicomponent alloys (swap moves). Kinetic Monte Carlo (kMC) instead picks events, such as an atom hopping into a vacancy, with rates from transition-state theory, and advances a physically meaningful clock; it reaches seconds or longer but needs a catalogue of possible events and their barriers, often taken from DFT. Lattice models such as the Ising model and cluster expansions, fitted to DFT energies, provide the energy function for many alloy MC studies."},{"n":1,"l":"Topic 1","t":"Introduction to computational materials science","h":"3.4 CALPHAD","u":"computational/introduction.html#calphad","x":"Basis. The CALPHAD (CALculation of PHAse Diagrams) method models the molar Gibbs energy of every phase as a function of temperature, pressure and composition, and finds equilibrium by minimising the total Gibbs energy of the system. A typical solution-phase model has the form Gm = Gref + Gideal + Gexcess + Gmag, Gideal = RT Σ xi ln xi where the excess term is commonly written as a Redlich–Kister polynomial in composition, and phases with distinct sublattices (carbides, intermetallics, interstitial solutions) use the compound energy formalism. Model parameters are fitted simultaneously to phase-diagram data, thermochemical measurements and first-principles results, and collected into thermodynamic databases (often distributed as .tdb files). Binary and ternary descriptions are extrapolated to multicomponent systems. Outputs. Phase diagrams and isopleths, equilibrium phase amounts and compositions, solidus and liquidus, solvus lines, transformation temperatures, activities, driving forces for precipitation, and (with the Scheil–Gulliver model) solidification paths under limited diffusion. Limits. It is a bulk equilibrium method with no geometry, no interfaces and no time unless coupled to a kinetic model. Accuracy depends on how well the database covers the composition and temperature region of interest, which is a common source of error for unusual compositions. Coupling with diffusion mobility databases allows simulation of diffusion-controlled transformations in one dimension and supplies the thermodynamic input to phase-field models."},{"n":1,"l":"Topic 1","t":"Introduction to computational materials science","h":"3.5 Phase-field modelling","u":"computational/introduction.html#phasefield","x":"Basis. Interfaces are represented as diffuse transition layers, so the interface need not be tracked explicitly. One or more continuous order parameters φ(r, t) distinguish phases or grains, and the free energy of the whole microstructure is a functional F = ∫ [ f(φ, c, T) + ½ κφ|∇φ|² + ½ κc|∇c|² + fel ] dV Evolution follows by relaxation toward lower free energy. For a non-conserved order parameter (grain orientation, phase identity) this is the Allen–Cahn equation, ∂φ/∂t = −L δF/δφ. For a conserved quantity such as composition it is the Cahn–Hilliard equation, ∂c/∂t = ∇·(M∇δF/δc). Elastic, magnetic and electrostatic energies can be added to the functional. Typical applications. Dendritic and cellular solidification, grain growth, spinodal decomposition and precipitation, martensitic and diffusional solid-state transformations, ferroelectric domain evolution, fracture and corrosion fronts. Practical points. The numerical grid must resolve the interface width, which makes large domains and long times expensive; thin-interface formulations relax this. Inputs are free-energy densities (often from CALPHAD), mobilities, interface energies and anisotropies, many of which are poorly known and must be taken from atomistic work or calibrated."},{"n":1,"l":"Topic 1","t":"Introduction to computational materials science","h":"3.6 Dislocation dynamics and crystal plasticity","u":"computational/introduction.html#cp","x":"Discrete dislocation dynamics (DDD) follows individual dislocation lines, moving each under the Peach–Koehler force from applied stress and from other dislocations, with rules for reactions such as junction formation and cross-slip. It reaches micrometre-scale volumes and addresses hardening and size effects. Crystal plasticity treats each grain as a single crystal that deforms by slip on defined systems. Slip on system α begins when the resolved shear stress, τα = mα·σ·nα (Schmid’s law), reaches a critical value, and the shear rate follows a flow rule with hardening laws. Implemented in a finite-element or spectral solver (crystal plasticity finite element method, CPFEM; spectral solvers such as DAMASK), it predicts texture evolution, anisotropy, grain-level strain partitioning and the response of polycrystals and multiphase aggregates, using a representative volume element (RVE) built from measured or synthetic microstructure."},{"n":1,"l":"Topic 1","t":"Introduction to computational materials science","h":"3.7 Continuum methods","u":"computational/introduction.html#continuum","x":"Continuum models treat the material as a smooth medium and solve conservation laws as partial differential equations: heat conduction, mass and momentum balance (fluid flow), species transport, and mechanical equilibrium with a constitutive law linking stress to strain and strain rate. The finite element method (FEM) divides the domain into elements and solves a weak form of the equations; the finite volume method is common in computational fluid dynamics (CFD). They are used for casting and welding thermal fields, forging, rolling and forming, heat treatment distortion, residual stress, fatigue and fracture assessment, and component-level design. Accuracy rests on the constitutive law and on boundary conditions, such as heat-transfer coefficients and friction, which are often the dominant uncertainty. Mesh convergence and time-step control are essential."},{"n":1,"l":"Topic 1","t":"Introduction to computational materials science","h":"3.8 Machine learning","u":"computational/introduction.html#ml","x":"Machine learning (ML) fits flexible models to data instead of deriving them from physics. In materials work it appears in four roles: (i) property prediction from composition or structure descriptors, (ii) interatomic potentials fitted to DFT, (iii) surrogate models that replace expensive simulations, and (iv) inverse design and active learning, where the model proposes the next experiment or calculation (for example by Bayesian optimisation). Key issues are data quantity and quality (materials datasets are small and biased toward successful experiments), the choice of descriptors or learned representations, the reliability of predictions outside the training domain (extrapolation), uncertainty estimates, and interpretability. Proper cross-validation matters: random splits of related compounds leak information, and splitting by composition family or by time is a stricter and more honest test. Physics-informed models, which embed symmetries or known constraints, generally need less data."},{"n":1,"l":"Topic 1","t":"Introduction to computational materials science","h":"4. Linking scales","u":"computational/introduction.html#multiscale","x":"Multiscale modelling connects the methods above. Two coupling strategies are common. Hierarchical (sequential, parameter passing): a finer model computes a quantity that is passed as a parameter to a coarser model. Example: DFT gives defect and interface energies; MD or kMC gives diffusivities and mobilities; CALPHAD supplies free energies; a phase-field model then uses all of these to predict microstructure; the microstructure feeds a crystal-plasticity or continuum model for properties. Concurrent: different regions or levels run together and exchange information during the run, as in quantum-mechanics/molecular-mechanics (QM/MM) or atomistic/continuum coupling around a crack tip. These are harder to set up and less common. Information also flows downward: a continuum thermal model provides the temperature history that a microstructure model needs. Along every interface of the chain, errors propagate, so each link should be validated on its own before the chain is trusted."},{"n":1,"l":"Topic 1","t":"Introduction to computational materials science","h":"5. A typical workflow","u":"computational/introduction.html#workflow","x":"Define the question in measurable terms: which property, which condition, what accuracy is needed. Select the model from the smallest feature and longest time that matter (section 7). Prepare inputs: structure or geometry, composition, potential or database, boundary and initial conditions, material parameters. Test convergence of numerical parameters before production runs (section 10). Run on a workstation or cluster; script it so that it is repeatable. Post-process and visualise; compute uncertainties, not only a single number. Validate against independent experiment or a higher-level calculation (section 6). Document versions, input files and random seeds so the work can be reproduced."},{"n":1,"l":"Topic 1","t":"Introduction to computational materials science","h":"6. Verification, validation and uncertainty","u":"computational/introduction.html#vv","x":"These three terms are often confused. Verification Are the equations solved correctly? Checks against analytical solutions, benchmark problems, mesh and time-step refinement, and code tests. Validation Are the right equations being solved? Comparison of predictions with experiments that were not used to fit the model. Uncertainty quantification How confident should one be in the number? Propagates uncertainty in inputs, parameters and numerical error to the output, and distinguishes systematic error (a poor model or potential) from statistical error (finite sampling). Typical sources of error include the exchange–correlation functional in DFT, the potential in MD, database coverage in CALPHAD, the neglect of defects and impurities, finite-size and finite-time effects, discretisation error, and poorly known input parameters. A model that reproduces its own training data has been fitted, not validated."},{"n":1,"l":"Topic 1","t":"Introduction to computational materials science","h":"7. Choosing a method","u":"computational/introduction.html#choosing","x":"Start from the question, not the tool. Three questions usually narrow the choice quickly: What is the smallest feature that controls the answer? Bond energies and electronic effects point to DFT; defect cores and interfaces point to atomistics; grains and precipitates point to mesoscale; whole components point to continuum models. What is the longest time that matters? Microsecond limits rule out direct MD for diffusional processing; use kMC, phase-field or a continuum model. Is the answer an equilibrium state or a rate? Equilibrium is often settled by thermodynamics alone, which is cheap; rates need a kinetic model. Question Reasonable first method Which phases are stable at this composition and temperature? CALPHAD How strongly does a solute bind to a defect or interface? DFT What is the diffusion coefficient or boundary mobility of an element? MD, kMC or DFT-derived barriers; or experimental data How does a dislocation or crack behave in this lattice? MD (with a validated potential) How does the microstructure evolve during heat treatment or solidification? Phase-field, with CALPHAD input How does a polycrystal deform and develop texture? Crystal plasticity What temperature, stress and distortion arise in the process or part? FEM or CFD Which of thousands of candidates should be tried first? High-throughput DFT or machine learning Often the cheapest useful step is a thermodynamic calculation, because it reveals which phases can exist before heavier modelling is attempted."},{"n":1,"l":"Topic 1","t":"Introduction to computational materials science","h":"8. Software","u":"computational/introduction.html#software","x":"The list is a starting map, not an endorsement; availability and licences change. Category Open source Commercial Electronic structure Quantum ESPRESSO, ABINIT, GPAW, CP2K VASP, CASTEP, Gaussian Molecular dynamics LAMMPS, GROMACS, OpenMM Materials Studio, Schrödinger suite Thermodynamics and kinetics pycalphad, OpenCALPHAD Thermo-Calc and DICTRA, FactSage, Pandat, JMatPro Mesoscale MOOSE, OpenPhase, PRISMS-PF, DAMASK, MMSP MICRESS Continuum OpenFOAM, CalculiX, FEniCS, MOOSE Abaqus, Ansys, COMSOL, DEFORM, Simufact Workflow and analysis ASE, pymatgen, AiiDA, OVITO, VESTA, ParaView — Machine learning scikit-learn, PyTorch, JAX, matminer — Python is the usual glue language: scripting inputs, running jobs, parsing outputs and plotting. Large jobs run on high-performance computing (HPC) clusters through a scheduler such as Slurm, with parallelism by MPI, threads or GPUs."},{"n":1,"l":"Topic 1","t":"Introduction to computational materials science","h":"9. Databases and data","u":"computational/introduction.html#data","x":"Materials Project, AFLOW, OQMD and JARVIS: large collections of DFT-computed structures and properties. NOMAD: repository and archive for computational materials data from many codes. Crystallographic databases: Inorganic Crystal Structure Database (ICSD, licensed), Crystallography Open Database (COD, open), Pearson’s Crystal Data. Thermodynamic and phase-diagram data: CALPHAD databases (commercial and some open), the ASM Alloy Phase Diagram Database, and NIST resources. Interatomic potentials: the NIST Interatomic Potentials Repository and OpenKIM, which also provides tests for potentials. Computed values in public databases carry the approximations of their method (typically GGA), so treat them as consistent comparisons rather than exact numbers."},{"n":1,"l":"Topic 1","t":"Introduction to computational materials science","h":"10. Good practice","u":"computational/introduction.html#habits","x":"Converge before you compute. Vary the plane-wave cutoff and k-point mesh in DFT, the cell size and time step in MD, the grid spacing and time step in phase-field, and the mesh in FEM, until the quantity of interest stops changing within the accuracy you need. Check the size effects. Periodic cells that are too small interact with their own images; MD boxes must be large compared with the defect or correlation length. Equilibrate and sample enough. Discard the start-up transient; report averages with error bars from independent runs or block averaging. Mind units and conventions. Codes use different unit systems (for example LAMMPS “metal” units) and different sign and normalisation conventions. Start simple. Reproduce a known result, such as a lattice parameter, an elastic constant or a textbook phase boundary, before attempting a novel one. Keep it reproducible. Store inputs, versions and seeds under version control, and archive the data that supports a published figure. Key takeaways Methods are specialised by length and time scale; no single model covers every scale, so multiscale linking is the norm. Start from the question: the smallest feature that matters, the longest time that matters, and whether the answer is an equilibrium or a rate. A simulation is a prediction to be tested. Verification, validation and uncertainty quantification are three different checks. The cheapest useful step is often a thermodynamic (CALPHAD) calculation, before heavier modelling is attempted. Inputs and approximations bound accuracy: trends and rankings are usually more reliable than absolute numbers."},{"n":1,"l":"Topic 1","t":"Introduction to computational materials science","h":"11. Check your understanding","u":"computational/introduction.html#check","x":"Which method would you reach for first to estimate the vacancy formation energy of copper, and why? Density functional theory: the quantity is an energy difference between a small cell with and without the defect, which needs electronic accuracy rather than a long trajectory. If a validated embedded-atom potential exists, molecular dynamics or static relaxation with that potential gives a much cheaper first estimate that can be checked against a few DFT calculations. Why can plain molecular dynamics not follow the homogenisation of a segregated alloy? Homogenisation is diffusion over micrometres lasting seconds to hours, while molecular dynamics reaches tens of nanometres and nanoseconds to microseconds. A kinetic Monte Carlo, phase-field or continuum diffusion model covers the relevant scales, with diffusivities and thermodynamics supplied by finer-scale methods. What is the difference between verification and validation? Verification asks whether the equations are solved correctly (convergence tests, benchmarks, code checks). Validation asks whether the right equations and inputs are being used, by comparing predictions with independent experiments that were not used to fit the model."},{"n":1,"l":"Topic 1","t":"Introduction to computational materials science","h":"12. Glossary","u":"computational/introduction.html#glossary","x":"Ab initio “From first principles”: methods that use no fitted parameters for the material of interest, such as DFT. Ensemble The set of conditions held fixed in a statistical-mechanics simulation (NVE, NVT, NPT). Gibbs energy The thermodynamic potential that is minimised at equilibrium at constant temperature and pressure. Interatomic potential A function giving the energy of a set of atoms from their positions, used in classical simulations. Order parameter A field that distinguishes one phase or grain orientation from another in phase-field models. Pseudopotential An effective potential that replaces core electrons in DFT so that only valence electrons are treated explicitly. Representative volume element (RVE) A volume of microstructure large enough that its averaged response represents the bulk material. Surrogate model A fast approximate model, often machine-learned, fitted to the output of an expensive simulation. Transition-state theory A theory that gives the rate of a thermally activated event from the energy barrier between two states."},{"n":1,"l":"Topic 1","t":"Introduction to computational materials science","h":"13. References and further reading","u":"computational/introduction.html#refs","x":"Check each citation against the original before relying on it in your own work. Hohenberg, P. and Kohn, W., Inhomogeneous electron gas, Phys. Rev. 136, B864 (1964). Kohn, W. and Sham, L. J., Self-consistent equations including exchange and correlation effects, Phys. Rev. 140, A1133 (1965). Perdew, J. P., Burke, K. and Ernzerhof, M., Generalized gradient approximation made simple, Phys. Rev. Lett. 77, 3865 (1996). Metropolis, N. et al., Equation of state calculations by fast computing machines, J. Chem. Phys. 21, 1087 (1953). Daw, M. S. and Baskes, M. I., Embedded-atom method, Phys. Rev. B 29, 6443 (1984). Cahn, J. W. and Hilliard, J. E., Free energy of a nonuniform system. I. Interfacial free energy, J. Chem. Phys. 28, 258 (1958). Allen, S. M. and Cahn, J. W., A microscopic theory for antiphase boundary motion, Acta Metall. 27, 1085 (1979). Plimpton, S., Fast parallel algorithms for short-range molecular dynamics, J. Comput. Phys. 117, 1 (1995). Lukas, H. L., Fries, S. G. and Sundman, B., Computational Thermodynamics: The Calphad Method, Cambridge University Press (2007). Frenkel, D. and Smit, B., Understanding Molecular Simulation, Academic Press. Allen, M. P. and Tildesley, D. J., Computer Simulation of Liquids, Oxford University Press. Martin, R. M., Electronic Structure: Basic Theory and Practical Methods, Cambridge University Press. Raabe, D., Computational Materials Science: The Simulation of Materials, Microstructures and Properties, Wiley-VCH (1998). Jain, A. et al., Commentary: The Materials Project, APL Materials 1, 011002 (2013). Previous topicProgramming and workflow Next topicCrystal structures, supercells and periodic boundaries"},{"n":2,"l":"Topic 2","t":"Crystal structures, supercells and periodic boundaries","h":"Crystal structures, supercells and periodic boundaries","u":"computational/crystal-structure-and-periodic-cells.html","x":"Topics/FoundationsTopic 2 Crystal structures, supercells and periodic boundaries Almost every atomistic simulation starts by writing down where the atoms are. For crystals, a handful of numbers describes an infinite solid; for defects, the same description is stretched into a finite, repeating box that must be chosen with care. Reading timeabout 12 min Builds on1. Introduction Leads to5. Density functional theory6. Molecular dynamics CategoryFoundations After reading this page you should be able to: Describe a crystal as a lattice plus a basis and count atoms, neighbours and packing for common structures. Use Miller indices for directions and planes, and relate real and reciprocal lattices. Choose a supercell for a point defect, surface or interface, and estimate the defect concentration it implies. Apply the minimum-image convention and explain the size effects that periodic boundaries introduce."},{"n":2,"l":"Topic 2","t":"Crystal structures, supercells and periodic boundaries","h":"1. Lattice and basis","u":"computational/crystal-structure-and-periodic-cells.html#lattice","x":"An ideal crystal is invariant under translation by any vector of a Bravais lattice: R = n1a1 + n2a2 + n3a3, ni integers where a1,2,3 are the primitive lattice vectors. The parallelepiped they span is the primitive cell: the smallest volume that tiles space by translation and contains exactly one lattice point. The crystal is the lattice decorated with a basis, a set of atoms at positions rj inside each cell, usually given as fractional coordinates (x, y, z) between 0 and 1 along the lattice vectors: position of atom j in cell n: r = Rn + (xja1 + yja2 + zja3) A cell can be described by six numbers: three lengths a, b, c and three angles α, β, γ. Symmetry sorts all lattices into seven crystal systems (cubic, tetragonal, orthorhombic, hexagonal, trigonal, monoclinic, triclinic) and fourteen Bravais lattices once centring is included (primitive P, body-centred I, face-centred F, base-centred C). Adding the basis and its symmetry gives 230 space groups. Symmetry matters computationally: it reduces the number of distinct k-points in electronic-structure calculations and the number of independent elastic constants (3 for cubic, 5 for hexagonal, 21 for triclinic). Often a conventional cell larger than the primitive cell is used because it shows the symmetry more clearly. The face-centred cubic conventional cell holds 4 lattice points, the body-centred cubic one holds 2."},{"n":2,"l":"Topic 2","t":"Crystal structures, supercells and periodic boundaries","h":"2. Common structures","u":"computational/crystal-structure-and-periodic-cells.html#structures","x":"Structure Lattice Atoms per conventional cell Nearest neighbours Packing fraction Examples Face-centred cubic (fcc) cubic F 4 12 0.74 Al, Cu, Ni, Au, austenite (γ-Fe) Body-centred cubic (bcc) cubic I 2 8 (6 more at 15% larger distance) 0.68 α-Fe (ferrite), W, Cr, Mo Hexagonal close-packed (hcp) hexagonal P, 2-atom basis 2 (in the hexagonal cell) 12 0.74 at ideal c/a = √(8/3) ≈ 1.633 Mg, Ti, Zn, Co Diamond cubic fcc, 2-atom basis 8 4 0.34 Si, Ge, C (diamond) Rock salt (B1) fcc, 2-atom basis 8 (4 cation + 4 anion) 6 (unlike ions) depends on radii NaCl, MgO, TiC Perovskite (ABO3) simple cubic (ideal) 5 B: 6 O; A: 12 O — SrTiO3, BaTiO3 Close packing arises from stacking triangular layers. The sequence ABAB… gives hcp and ABCABC… gives fcc, which explains why the two structures have the same density and coordination but different symmetry, and why stacking faults in fcc metals locally look like hcp. Bcc is less densely packed and has no single close-packed slip plane; the mobility of its screw dislocations depends strongly on temperature, which is the origin of the ductile-to-brittle transition that bcc metals show and fcc metals do not. Lattice parameters at room temperature are the first numbers to check against any model: Cu fcc a ≈ 3.61 Å, Al fcc 4.05 Å, α-Fe bcc 2.87 Å, Si diamond 5.43 Å. If a calculation or potential reproduces these to within a percent or two, the basic bonding description is plausible; if not, nothing downstream can be trusted."},{"n":2,"l":"Topic 2","t":"Crystal structures, supercells and periodic boundaries","h":"3. Directions and planes","u":"computational/crystal-structure-and-periodic-cells.html#indices","x":"Directions are written [uvw]: the smallest integers proportional to the components of the vector in lattice coordinates, with negative values shown by a bar. A family of equivalent directions is written ⟨uvw⟩. Planes use Miller indices (hkl): take the intercepts of the plane with the axes in units of the lattice parameters, invert them, and clear fractions. A family of equivalent planes is written {hkl}. In cubic crystals, [hkl] is perpendicular to (hkl), but this is not true in general for other systems. The spacing of (hkl) planes in a cubic crystal is dhkl = a / √(h² + k² + l²) which is used directly in diffraction (Bragg’s law, nλ = 2d sinθ) and to compare simulated structures with X-ray or electron diffraction. Close-packed planes and directions control plasticity: slip occurs most readily on {111}⟨110⟩ in fcc and on {110}, {112} and {123} planes along ⟨111⟩ in bcc. Surface energy also depends on orientation, with close-packed surfaces such as fcc (111) typically lowest. Hexagonal crystals use four-index Miller–Bravais notation (hkil) with i = −(h + k), which makes equivalent planes look alike."},{"n":2,"l":"Topic 2","t":"Crystal structures, supercells and periodic boundaries","h":"4. Reciprocal lattice","u":"computational/crystal-structure-and-periodic-cells.html#reciprocal","x":"Waves in a periodic solid, whether electrons, phonons or X-rays, are best described in reciprocal space. The reciprocal lattice vectors satisfy ai · bj = 2π δij, i.e. b1 = 2π (a2 × a3) / [a1 · (a2 × a3)] and cyclic permutations The Wigner–Seitz cell of the reciprocal lattice is the first Brillouin zone. A key rule: the larger the real-space cell, the smaller its Brillouin zone, so a supercell needs fewer k-points. The reciprocal lattice vector Ghkl = hb1 + kb2 + lb3 is perpendicular to the (hkl) planes and has length 2π/dhkl, which links this section to Section 3."},{"n":2,"l":"Topic 2","t":"Crystal structures, supercells and periodic boundaries","h":"5. Defects","u":"computational/crystal-structure-and-periodic-cells.html#defects","x":"Real materials are defined as much by their defects as by their ideal structure, and most of the interesting simulation questions concern them. Defects are classified by dimension. Dimension Defect Typical simulation question 0D (point) Vacancy, self-interstitial, substitutional or interstitial solute, antisite Formation energy, migration barrier, binding to other defects, concentration at equilibrium 1D (line) Edge, screw and mixed dislocations; disclinations Core structure, Peierls stress, interaction with solutes and precipitates 2D (planar) Grain boundaries, stacking faults, twin boundaries, phase boundaries, free surfaces Interface energy, segregation, sliding and migration, fracture energy 3D (volume) Voids, precipitates, inclusions Nucleation barrier, coherency strain, growth rate A point defect’s equilibrium concentration follows from the competition between the energy it costs and the configurational entropy it gains: cv ≈ exp( −Ef / kBT ) (times a vibrational-entropy factor) Because the formation energy Ef of a vacancy is of order 1 eV in many metals, equilibrium vacancy fractions near the melting point are about 10−4 to 10−3 and fall steeply on cooling. Computing Ef from DFT or an interatomic potential is one of the most common first tasks in atomistic modelling."},{"n":2,"l":"Topic 2","t":"Crystal structures, supercells and periodic boundaries","h":"6. Building supercells","u":"computational/crystal-structure-and-periodic-cells.html#supercell","x":"A simulation cell cannot be infinite, so a supercell is built by repeating the unit cell n1×n2×n3 times and then modifying it, for example by removing one atom to create a vacancy. Because the supercell is itself periodically repeated, the defect interacts with its own periodic images. The artefact shrinks as the cell grows, and the cost grows too, so supercell size is a convergence parameter (see the convergence section of the density functional theory page). The defect concentration implied by a single vacancy in a n×n×n fcc supercell is 1/(4n³): fcc supercell Atoms (before removing one) Vacancy concentration 2×2×2 32 3.1% 3×3×3 108 0.93% 4×4×4 256 0.39% 6×6×6 864 0.12% These are far above the equilibrium concentration, so the vacancies are strongly interacting in small cells: elastic strain fields overlap and the computed formation energy drifts with size. The standard remedy is to repeat the calculation in several sizes and extrapolate, or to use a cell large enough that the result changes by less than the accuracy required. For charged defects in insulators, long-range Coulomb interactions converge very slowly and need explicit correction schemes. A supercell does not have to be a simple multiple. A general transformation matrix M (integer entries) gives new lattice vectors a′ = Ma, which allows a nearly cubic cell to be built from a hexagonal or primitive fcc cell, or a cell oriented along a chosen crystallographic direction. For dislocations and grain boundaries, the cell axes are chosen along specific directions so that the periodic boundary can accommodate the defect geometry. Here is how a 4×4×4 copper cell with one vacancy looks in the Atomic Simulation Environment (ASE), a Python library: from ase.build import bulk cell = bulk(\"Cu\", \"fcc\", a=3.61, cubic=True).repeat((4, 4, 4)) # 256 atoms del cell[0] # one vacancy: 255 atoms print(len(cell), cell.get_volume())"},{"n":2,"l":"Topic 2","t":"Crystal structures, supercells and periodic boundaries","h":"7. Periodic boundary conditions","u":"computational/crystal-structure-and-periodic-cells.html#pbc","x":"With periodic boundary conditions (PBC) the cell is surrounded by an infinite array of copies of itself. An atom that leaves through one face re-enters through the opposite face, so the simulation has no surfaces and a small number of atoms mimics bulk. Follow the highlighted atom in the figure: it leaves on the right and reappears on the left. The simulation cell (solid outline) and two of its periodic images (dashed). The same atoms appear in every image. Minimum image convention Each atom should interact with the nearest image of every other atom, not with a distant one. For an orthogonal box of side L, the separation component is wrapped back into the interval [−L/2, L/2]: Δx ← Δx − L · round(Δx / L) This is only correct if every interaction cutoff rc satisfies rc < L/2 (more generally, less than half the smallest perpendicular width of a triclinic cell). Otherwise an atom could interact with two images of the same neighbour. A typical metallic potential has a cutoff of 5 to 6 Å, so each box edge must exceed about 10 to 12 Å. What periodicity imposes Size limit on correlations. No fluctuation or structure can have a wavelength longer than the box. Near phase transitions, where correlation lengths diverge, results depend strongly on box size. Image interactions. Defects, charges and strain fields interact with their images, as in Section 6. Net momentum and drift. Total momentum is conserved in MD with PBC and should be set to zero; the centre of mass otherwise drifts. Diffusion bookkeeping. To compute diffusion from displacements, positions must be unwrapped (a running count of boundary crossings is kept), otherwise atoms appear to jump across the box. Compatibility of the defect with the box. A grain boundary must be compatible with the periodicity in the plane, and a single dislocation cannot be placed in a fully periodic cell because it would leave a net Burgers vector; dislocation dipoles or quadrupoles are used instead, so that opposite dislocations cancel."},{"n":2,"l":"Topic 2","t":"Crystal structures, supercells and periodic boundaries","h":"8. Surfaces and interfaces","u":"computational/crystal-structure-and-periodic-cells.html#surfaces","x":"To model a surface, periodicity is kept in two directions and broken in the third by adding a vacuum layer. This is called a slab model. The slab must be thick enough that the two surfaces do not interact (the middle layers should look like bulk), and the vacuum must be wide enough, often 10 Å or more, that neighbouring slabs do not interact through the vacuum. Surface energy follows from γ = ( Eslab − N Ebulk ) / (2A) where Ebulk is the energy per atom of the bulk crystal, N the number of atoms in the slab, A the surface area and the factor 2 counts both faces. Using the same computational settings for the slab and the bulk reference is essential, otherwise numerical differences swamp the small difference being measured. For grain boundaries, two crystals with relative misorientation are joined. Geometric descriptions such as the coincidence site lattice (Σ) count how many lattice points of the two grains coincide: low-Σ boundaries (for example the Σ3 coherent twin) have high symmetry and low energy. A fully periodic cell contains two boundaries (the second appears where the two grains meet across the periodic boundary), so the excess energy is divided by twice the area. Many initial atomic arrangements (rigid-body translations, atom deletion) should be relaxed and the lowest-energy result kept, because boundary structure is a global-optimisation problem."},{"n":2,"l":"Topic 2","t":"Crystal structures, supercells and periodic boundaries","h":"9. Tools and file formats","u":"computational/crystal-structure-and-periodic-cells.html#tools","x":"Building and manipulating: ASE and pymatgen (Python), Atomsk (command line, good for dislocations and polycrystals), VESTA (visual). Visualisation and analysis: OVITO (defect and structure identification such as common neighbour analysis and dislocation extraction), VESTA, VMD. Common file formats: CIF (crystallographic information file, from experiment databases), POSCAR (VASP), LAMMPS data files, XYZ (plain coordinates), and the formats of Quantum ESPRESSO inputs. ASE reads and converts between most of them. Symmetry analysis: spglib (used inside ASE and pymatgen) identifies space groups and primitive cells from coordinates. Key takeaways A crystal is a lattice plus a basis. Count atoms and neighbours to sanity-check a structure: fcc has 4 atoms per cubic cell and 12 neighbours, bcc has 2 and 8. Miller indices label planes and directions; the reciprocal lattice links real-space cells to diffraction and to k-point sampling. Defects are the subject of most simulations. Supercell size is a convergence parameter, and one vacancy in an N-atom cell is a concentration of 1/N. Periodic boundaries remove walls but make a defect interact with its own images; every cutoff must be smaller than half the box. Surfaces use slabs with vacuum; a grain boundary in a fully periodic cell always comes as a pair."},{"n":2,"l":"Topic 2","t":"Crystal structures, supercells and periodic boundaries","h":"10. Check your understanding","u":"computational/crystal-structure-and-periodic-cells.html#check","x":"How many atoms are in a 5×5×5 bcc supercell, and what is the concentration from one vacancy? The conventional bcc cell holds 2 atoms, so the supercell has 2 × 125 = 250 atoms. One vacancy gives 1/250 = 0.4%. Why must a box edge be larger than twice the cutoff radius? The minimum image convention picks only the nearest copy of each neighbour. If the cutoff exceeded half the box edge, an atom could be within range of two images of the same neighbour, and the force would be counted wrongly or only once. A surface-energy calculation gives a negative value. What should you check first? Whether the slab and the bulk reference were computed with identical settings (cutoff, k-point density, number of layers, smearing). Inconsistent settings are the usual cause, because the quantity being computed is a small difference of two large energies."},{"n":2,"l":"Topic 2","t":"Crystal structures, supercells and periodic boundaries","h":"11. References and further reading","u":"computational/crystal-structure-and-periodic-cells.html#refs","x":"Kittel, C., Introduction to Solid State Physics, Wiley. Ashcroft, N. W. and Mermin, N. D., Solid State Physics, Saunders. Hammond, C., The Basics of Crystallography and Diffraction, Oxford University Press. Hirth, J. P. and Lothe, J., Theory of Dislocations, Krieger. Larsen, A. H. et al., The Atomic Simulation Environment, J. Phys.: Condens. Matter 29, 273002 (2017). Hirel, P., Atomsk: a tool for manipulating and converting atomic data files, Comput. Phys. Commun. 197, 212 (2015). Stukowski, A., Visualization and analysis of atomistic simulation data with OVITO, Modelling Simul. Mater. Sci. Eng. 18, 015012 (2010). Previous topicIntroduction to computational materials science Next topicStatistical mechanics for simulation"},{"n":3,"l":"Topic 3","t":"Statistical mechanics for simulation","h":"Statistical mechanics for simulation","u":"computational/statistical-mechanics.html","x":"Topics/FoundationsTopic 3 Statistical mechanics for simulation A simulation follows a few thousand atoms; a material has 1023. Statistical mechanics is the bridge: it says which averages over the small system reproduce the temperature, pressure, free energy and transport coefficients of the large one. Reading timeabout 12 min Builds on1. IntroductionB2. Physics Leads to4. Thermodynamics and CALPHAD6. Molecular dynamics7. Monte Carlo and kMC CategoryFoundations After reading this page you should be able to: Distinguish microstates from macrostates and time averages from ensemble averages. State the main ensembles, their fixed variables and their thermodynamic potentials. Write and use the Boltzmann distribution and the canonical partition function. Explain why free-energy differences are harder to compute than energies, and name the main methods. Estimate the statistical uncertainty of a simulated average."},{"n":3,"l":"Topic 3","t":"Statistical mechanics for simulation","h":"1. From atoms to properties","u":"computational/statistical-mechanics.html#bridge","x":"A microstate is a complete specification of the system at the atomic level: all positions and momenta for a classical system, or a full quantum state. A macrostate is what an experiment sees: a few variables such as temperature T, pressure p, volume V, number of atoms N and composition. Enormously many microstates correspond to one macrostate, and the macroscopic property is the average of the microscopic quantity over those microstates, weighted by how probable each is. For N classical atoms the microstate is a point in phase space of 6N dimensions, (r1, …, rN, p1, …, pN). The total energy as a function of that point is the Hamiltonian, H(r, p) = Σi |pi|² / 2mi + U(r1, …, rN) Two kinds of average appear in simulation. An ensemble average ⟨A⟩ weights every phase-space point by its probability. A time average follows one trajectory and averages over time. Molecular dynamics produces time averages; Monte Carlo produces samples that estimate ensemble averages directly. The two agree if the system is ergodic (Section 8)."},{"n":3,"l":"Topic 3","t":"Statistical mechanics for simulation","h":"2. Ensembles","u":"computational/statistical-mechanics.html#ensembles","x":"An ensemble is the set of microstates consistent with a set of fixed macroscopic conditions. Which one to use depends on what the experiment holds constant and what the algorithm can impose. Ensemble Fixed Fluctuates Potential that is minimised at equilibrium Typical use Microcanonical (NVE) N, V, E T, p Entropy S is maximised (S = kB ln Ω) Plain MD without a thermostat; testing energy conservation Canonical (NVT) N, V, T E, p Helmholtz free energy F = U − TS Properties at fixed volume; Monte Carlo Isothermal–isobaric (NPT) N, p, T E, V Gibbs free energy G = H − TS Matching laboratory conditions; thermal expansion; phase stability Grand canonical (μVT) μ, V, T E, N Grand potential Ω = F − μN Adsorption, segregation, chemical equilibrium with a reservoir In the thermodynamic limit (large N) the ensembles give the same averages. Fluctuations of an extensive quantity scale as 1/√N relative to its mean, so they are tiny for a macroscopic sample but visible in a box of a few hundred atoms, and they differ between ensembles. A well-posed simulation states which ensemble it samples."},{"n":3,"l":"Topic 3","t":"Statistical mechanics for simulation","h":"3. The Boltzmann distribution","u":"computational/statistical-mechanics.html#boltzmann","x":"For a system in contact with a heat bath at temperature T (canonical ensemble), the probability of finding it in a microstate of energy Ei is pi = exp(−Ei / kBT) / Z, Z = Σi exp(−Ei / kBT) This single formula underlies Metropolis Monte Carlo, thermostatted molecular dynamics, transition-state theory and the equilibrium populations of defects and phases. Lower-energy states are more probable, but the number of states at each energy (the degeneracy) also counts: a high-energy level with many states can hold more total probability than a low-energy level with few. The balance between these two effects is the balance between energy and entropy. Populations of six equally spaced energy levels as temperature changes. The scale of “hot” and “cold” is set by the spacing ε compared with kBT. This interactive demo needs JavaScript to run."},{"n":3,"l":"Topic 3","t":"Statistical mechanics for simulation","h":"4. Partition function and examples","u":"computational/statistical-mechanics.html#partition","x":"The partition function Z is the sum above, and it generates the thermodynamics. With β = 1/kBT: F = −kBT ln Z, ⟨E⟩ = −∂(ln Z)/∂β, S = −∂F/∂T, p = −∂F/∂V If Z can be computed, everything follows. It can be computed exactly only for simple models, which are nevertheless the building blocks of larger ones. Two-level system A defect with ground state at 0 and an excited state at ε has Z = 1 + exp(−ε/kBT). The excited fraction is exp(−ε/kBT)/Z, which at low temperature gives the Arrhenius form used for equilibrium vacancy concentration. Harmonic oscillator and equipartition For one classical oscillator Z ∝ kBT/(ℏω), giving mean energy kBT: equipartition assigns ½kBT to every quadratic term in the Hamiltonian. A solid of N atoms has 3N oscillators and so heat capacity 3NkB (3R ≈ 24.9 J mol−1 K−1, the Dulong–Petit value). This is why classical simulations give a constant heat capacity at all temperatures, while real solids drop below it at low temperature because quantum oscillators with ℏω > kBT are frozen out. Classical MD is therefore reliable only when T is comparable to or above the Debye temperature of the material, or when quantum corrections are applied. Kinetic temperature Equipartition also defines the temperature seen in an MD run: ⟨K⟩ = ½ Nf kBT, Nf = 3N − 3 (for fixed total momentum; subtract further constraints) The instantaneous kinetic temperature fluctuates by a relative amount of order √(2/Nf) in a small box, so a reading of a few percent away from the target in a 200-atom cell is normal, not an error."},{"n":3,"l":"Topic 3","t":"Statistical mechanics for simulation","h":"5. Free energy and entropy","u":"computational/statistical-mechanics.html#free-energy","x":"Phase stability, defect concentrations, solubility, nucleation barriers and reaction equilibria are governed by free energies, not energies. Free energies are harder to compute because they depend on the volume of phase space accessible, not just on averages of observables. In a simulation, regions of high entropy are visited often and are easy to see, but the free energy needs the absolute probability, which is ln Z, and Z is not an average of anything that is directly sampled. Contributions to the entropy of a material include vibrational (the main finite-temperature term in most solids), configurational (arrangement of atoms or vacancies on a lattice), electronic (important in metals) and magnetic (important in iron and its alloys, where it changes the relative stability of bcc and fcc). Neglecting the right one is a common reason that a 0 K energy ranking fails at the temperature of interest. Methods for free-energy differences Harmonic and quasi-harmonic approximation. The vibrational free energy of a crystal from its phonon frequencies ωi: Fvib = Σi [½ℏωi + kBT ln(1 − exp(−ℏωi/kBT))]. The quasi-harmonic version lets frequencies depend on volume, capturing thermal expansion. It fails when anharmonicity is strong. Thermodynamic integration. Switch the Hamiltonian from a reference H0 to the target H1 through a coupling parameter λ, and integrate: ΔF = ∫01 ⟨∂H/∂λ⟩λ dλ. With an Einstein crystal as reference this is the Frenkel–Ladd method for absolute free energies of solids; melting points follow from equating solid and liquid free energies. Free-energy perturbation. ΔF = −kBT ln⟨exp(−ΔU/kBT)⟩0, accurate only when the two states overlap strongly. Umbrella sampling and metadynamics. To obtain a free-energy profile along a coordinate (for example the distance an atom moves, or a nucleation order parameter), bias the simulation so that it samples barriers and then remove the bias. Both rely on a good choice of coordinate. Coexistence methods. Simulate two phases in one box and watch the interface move or stay still; the temperature at which it is stationary is the transition temperature."},{"n":3,"l":"Topic 3","t":"Statistical mechanics for simulation","h":"6. Properties from fluctuations","u":"computational/statistical-mechanics.html#fluctuations","x":"Response functions are determined by fluctuations of the corresponding quantities at equilibrium. For example in the canonical ensemble, CV = ( ⟨E²⟩ − ⟨E⟩² ) / (kB T²) and in the isothermal–isobaric ensemble the isothermal compressibility follows from the variance of the volume, κT = (⟨V²⟩ − ⟨V⟩²)/(kBT⟨V⟩). Thermal expansion comes from the cross-fluctuation of enthalpy and volume, and elastic constants from stress fluctuations. These formulae let one run produce several properties, but they converge slowly because they are variances; direct perturbation (apply a small strain and measure the response) is often more efficient."},{"n":3,"l":"Topic 3","t":"Statistical mechanics for simulation","h":"7. Correlation functions and transport","u":"computational/statistical-mechanics.html#correlation","x":"Structure. The radial distribution function g(r) is the probability, relative to a uniform gas, of finding an atom a distance r from another. Crystals give sharp peaks, liquids give damped oscillations that decay to 1, and amorphous solids lie between. Its Fourier transform is the structure factor S(q), directly comparable with diffraction. The coordination number is the integral of 4πr²ρg(r) up to the first minimum. Dynamics. Time-correlation functions measure how quickly the system forgets a state. Transport coefficients follow from them. The diffusion coefficient has two equivalent forms: Einstein: D = limt→∞ ⟨|r(t) − r(0)|²⟩ / (6t) Green–Kubo: D = (1/3) ∫0∞ ⟨v(0)·v(t)⟩ dt The mean-squared displacement (MSD) must be computed from unwrapped coordinates, and the slope fitted only in the linear (diffusive) regime, not the early ballistic regime where MSD ∝ t². Viscosity and thermal conductivity have analogous Green–Kubo expressions (from stress and heat-current correlations), and these converge slowly."},{"n":3,"l":"Topic 3","t":"Statistical mechanics for simulation","h":"8. Ergodicity and sampling","u":"computational/statistical-mechanics.html#ergodicity","x":"A system is ergodic if, given enough time, a trajectory visits all accessible regions of phase space in proportion to their probability. Then the time average equals the ensemble average. Real simulations are finite, and several situations break this in practice: Rare events. A system with an energy barrier much larger than kBT can sit in one basin for longer than the run, so the average describes only that basin. With a typical attempt frequency of 1013 s−1, an atomic hop over a 1 eV barrier at 300 K occurs about once every 104 seconds (hours), compared with MD runs of nanoseconds, a gap of some thirteen orders of magnitude. Metastability and hysteresis. Heating and cooling runs through a phase transition give different answers because nucleation is slow. Glassy dynamics. Structural relaxation is much slower than the simulated time. Remedies are: run longer; start from several independent configurations and compare; use methods that sample differently (Monte Carlo with swap moves, replica exchange, which exchanges configurations between temperatures; metadynamics; accelerated dynamics); or use kinetic methods that model rare events explicitly (see the Monte Carlo page)."},{"n":3,"l":"Topic 3","t":"Statistical mechanics for simulation","h":"9. Statistical error","u":"computational/statistical-mechanics.html#errors","x":"A simulated average is an estimate with an uncertainty, and a number without an error bar is incomplete. Successive configurations in an MD or MC run are correlated, so the number of independent samples is much smaller than the number of recorded frames. For M frames with correlation time τ (in units of the sampling interval), the standard error of the mean is approximately σmean ≈ σ √( 2τ / M ) where σ is the standard deviation of the quantity. Practical ways to get it: Block averaging. Divide the series into blocks longer than the correlation time, average each block and compute the error from the block means. Increase the block length until the estimated error stops growing. Independent replicas. Repeat with different random seeds or initial velocities; the spread of results is the error. Discard the equilibration period. The start of a run depends on the initial configuration; plot the property against time and start averaging only after it has stopped drifting. Systematic errors (a poor potential, finite box, neglected quantum effects) are not reduced by longer runs and are usually larger than the statistical error. Report both kinds. Key takeaways The Boltzmann distribution, p proportional to exp(-E/kT), underlies Metropolis Monte Carlo, thermostats and equilibrium defect concentrations. Pick the ensemble that matches what is held fixed: NVE, NVT, NPT or the grand canonical. Free energies, not energies, decide stability, and they need dedicated methods: thermodynamic integration, perturbation, umbrella sampling, the quasi-harmonic approximation. Classical simulation assumes equipartition, so it overestimates heat capacity when quantum modes are frozen out at low temperature. Quote averages with error bars. The correlation time sets the number of independent samples, and ergodicity is never guaranteed."},{"n":3,"l":"Topic 3","t":"Statistical mechanics for simulation","h":"10. Check your understanding","u":"computational/statistical-mechanics.html#check","x":"At what temperature is the excited state of a two-level defect with ε = 1 eV populated at 1%? Solve exp(−ε/kBT)/(1 + exp(−ε/kBT)) = 0.01, which gives ε/kBT ≈ 4.6, so T ≈ 11 600 K / 4.6 ≈ 2 500 K (using 1 eV/kB ≈ 11 600 K). Even at high temperature the excited state stays rare for such a large gap. Why does classical MD overestimate the heat capacity of a solid at low temperature? Classical equipartition gives each oscillator energy kBT at every temperature, so CV = 3NkB. In a real solid, modes with ℏω larger than kBT are not excited, so CV falls below this value as the temperature drops. A run of 106 frames has a correlation time of 103 frames. About how many independent samples is that? Roughly M/(2τ) = 106/(2×103) = 500 independent samples, so the standard error is about σ/√500, not σ/√106."},{"n":3,"l":"Topic 3","t":"Statistical mechanics for simulation","h":"11. References and further reading","u":"computational/statistical-mechanics.html#refs","x":"Chandler, D., Introduction to Modern Statistical Mechanics, Oxford University Press. McQuarrie, D. A., Statistical Mechanics, University Science Books. Tuckerman, M. E., Statistical Mechanics: Theory and Molecular Simulation, Oxford University Press. Frenkel, D. and Smit, B., Understanding Molecular Simulation, Academic Press (free energies, ensembles, error analysis). Frenkel, D. and Ladd, A. J. C., New Monte Carlo method to compute the free energy of arbitrary solids, J. Chem. Phys. 81, 3188 (1984). Flyvbjerg, H. and Petersen, H. G., Error estimates on averages of correlated data, J. Chem. Phys. 91, 461 (1989). Laio, A. and Parrinello, M., Escaping free-energy minima, Proc. Natl. Acad. Sci. USA 99, 12562 (2002). Previous topicCrystal structures, supercells and periodic boundaries Next topicThermodynamics and CALPHAD"},{"n":4,"l":"Topic 4","t":"Thermodynamics and CALPHAD","h":"Thermodynamics and CALPHAD","u":"computational/thermodynamics-and-calphad.html","x":"Topics/ThermodynamicsTopic 4 Thermodynamics and CALPHAD Before asking how fast anything happens, ask what is stable. The CALPHAD method turns measured and computed thermodynamic data into models of the Gibbs energy of every phase, and from those models any equilibrium can be calculated for any composition. Reading timeabout 12 min Builds on3. Statistical mechanics Leads to8. Diffusion and kinetics9. Phase-field CategoryThermodynamics After reading this page you should be able to: State the condition for equilibrium in terms of Gibbs energy and chemical potentials. Write the Gibbs energy of ideal and regular solutions and predict a miscibility gap. Construct the common tangent and apply the lever rule. Describe how CALPHAD models phases, and list what the method can and cannot calculate."},{"n":4,"l":"Topic 4","t":"Thermodynamics and CALPHAD","h":"1. Equilibrium and Gibbs energy","u":"computational/thermodynamics-and-calphad.html#basics","x":"At constant temperature T and pressure p, a closed system is at equilibrium when its Gibbs energy is a minimum: G = H − TS = U + pV − TS The enthalpy H favours strong bonding and the entropy term −TS favours disorder, and rising temperature shifts the balance toward entropy. For a multicomponent system the contribution of each component is its chemical potential μi = (∂G/∂ni)T,p,nj≠i. Equilibrium between phases then requires that each component has the same chemical potential in every phase in which it is present: μiα = μiβ = … for every component i The Gibbs phase rule counts how many variables may be changed independently: F = C − P + 2 with pressure free, or F = C − P + 1 at fixed pressure, where C is the number of components and P the number of phases. In a binary alloy at constant pressure, three phases can coexist only at one temperature (F = 0), which is why eutectic, eutectoid and peritectic reactions occur at fixed temperatures."},{"n":4,"l":"Topic 4","t":"Thermodynamics and CALPHAD","h":"2. Mixing and solution models","u":"computational/thermodynamics-and-calphad.html#mixing","x":"The Gibbs energy of one mole of a binary solution of A and B is built from three parts: G = xA GA0 + xB GB0 + ΔGmix, ΔGmix = RT (xA ln xA + xB ln xB) + Gex The first two terms are the pure components in the same structure; the logarithmic term is the ideal entropy of mixing (−RΣx ln x per mole), always favouring mixing and with infinite slope at the pure ends, which is why no phase is perfectly pure; and Gex is the excess Gibbs energy that collects all non-ideal interactions. The simplest form is the regular solution Gex = ΩxAxB, where Ω > 0 means unlike neighbours are disfavoured (tendency to separate) and Ω < 0 means they are favoured (tendency to order or form compounds). The activity ai = exp[(μi − μi0)/RT] = γixi measures the effective concentration. The activity coefficient γi equals 1 for an ideal solution; Raoult’s law holds for the solvent and Henry’s law (ai ∝ xi) for a dilute solute, with γ constant but not 1."},{"n":4,"l":"Topic 4","t":"Thermodynamics and CALPHAD","h":"3. Common tangent and stability","u":"computational/thermodynamics-and-calphad.html#tangent","x":"For a regular solution with Ω > 0 the shape of the Gibbs energy curve changes with temperature: at high temperature the entropy term wins and the curve has a single minimum, while at low temperature the curve has two minima separated by a hump. Watch it happen in the animation: the cooling solution develops a miscibility gap. Regular-solution Gibbs energy of mixing as temperature falls (hot to cool, gauge on the right). Below the critical temperature, the dashed line is the common tangent and the composition range between its touching points is the miscibility gap. This interactive demo needs JavaScript to run. For a regular solution the critical (consolute) temperature, above which the gap closes, is Tc = Ω / (2R) Below Tc an alloy of intermediate composition lowers its Gibbs energy by splitting into two phases. The compositions of the two phases are found by the common tangent construction: draw the line that touches the curve at two points. The tangent condition is exactly equal chemical potentials in the two phases (the intercepts of the tangent with the pure-component axes are μA and μB). For a binary mixture of overall composition x0 splitting into phases of compositions xα and xβ, the lever rule gives the fractions: fβ = (x0 − xα) / (xβ − xα), fα = 1 − fβ Binodal and spinodal Within the gap there are two kinds of instability. Between the common-tangent points (the binodal) the two-phase state is stable, but a homogeneous solution may still be locally stable and decompose only after nucleation of a second phase. Inside the spinodal, where ∂²G/∂x² < 0, the solution is unstable to any small composition fluctuation and decomposes spontaneously. The distinction matters for kinetics and for simulation: the spinodal mechanism is the one modelled in the phase-field topic."},{"n":4,"l":"Topic 4","t":"Thermodynamics and CALPHAD","h":"4. Reading phase diagrams","u":"computational/thermodynamics-and-calphad.html#diagrams","x":"A binary phase diagram is a map of the stable phases against temperature and composition, produced by repeating the common-tangent construction at each temperature. Boundaries are loci of tangent points. A few conventions let you read any diagram: In a single-phase region, the phase composition equals the overall composition. In a two-phase region, draw a horizontal tie line at the temperature; its ends on the phase boundaries give the phase compositions, and the lever rule gives the amounts. Three-phase equilibria appear as horizontal lines (invariant reactions): eutectic L → α + β, eutectoid γ → α + β, peritectic L + α → β. Ternary systems are shown as isothermal sections or vertical sections (isopleths); tie lines in a ternary two-phase region do not generally lie along a fixed direction, which is why calculation is needed. The iron–carbon system illustrates the points. The eutectoid at about 727 °C and 0.76 wt% C converts austenite (fcc) into ferrite (bcc, almost no carbon) and cementite (Fe3C, 6.67 wt% C). A 0.40 wt% C steel just below 727 °C contains, by the lever rule, a cementite fraction of (0.40 − 0.02)/(6.67 − 0.02) ≈ 5.7%. Note that Fe–Fe3C is a metastable diagram: graphite is the true equilibrium phase, but cementite forms because its nucleation is much easier."},{"n":4,"l":"Topic 4","t":"Thermodynamics and CALPHAD","h":"5. The CALPHAD method","u":"computational/thermodynamics-and-calphad.html#calphad","x":"Phase diagrams were originally drawn from measurements alone. CALPHAD (CALculation of PHAse Diagrams, developed from the 1970s, building on Kaufman and Bernstein) replaces drawing with modelling: every phase gets an analytical expression for its Gibbs energy as a function of T, p and composition, with adjustable parameters. The workflow is: Collect data for the system: phase boundaries, invariant temperatures, heats of formation and mixing, activities, heat capacities, plus first-principles (DFT) formation energies for compounds that are hard to measure. Choose models for each phase that match its crystal structure and chemistry (Section 6). Optimise parameters so that the combined calculation reproduces all data within experimental uncertainty. Binary systems are assessed first, then ternaries, and the models are extrapolated to higher-order systems. Store the parameters in a thermodynamic database. The common exchange format is the TDB text file, readable by several programs. Calculate equilibria by minimising total Gibbs energy under the imposed conditions. The strength of the approach is that the same expression describes stable and metastable states and extends into regions that were never measured; its weakness is that the result is only as good as the assessment (Section 9)."},{"n":4,"l":"Topic 4","t":"Thermodynamics and CALPHAD","h":"6. Models inside a database","u":"computational/thermodynamics-and-calphad.html#models","x":"Pure elements Each element in each structure (even structures it never adopts, such as bcc for a metal that is hcp) has a Gibbs energy function written as a polynomial in T, relative to a reference state. The SGTE unary database, a community standard, supplies these lattice stabilities in the form G(T) = a + bT + cT ln T + dT² + eT−1 + fT³ + … Substitutional solutions Solution phases with atoms mixing on one set of sites are written as the sum of the three terms in Section 2, with the excess term expanded in a Redlich–Kister polynomial: Gex = xA xB Σk kLAB (xA − xB)k, kL = ak + bkT The term with k = 0 is the regular-solution parameter; k = 1 introduces asymmetry. The parameters ak and bk are the quantities fitted during assessment. Higher-order systems add ternary interaction terms, and binary data are extrapolated to ternaries with symmetric or asymmetric schemes (Muggianu, Kohler, Toop). Sublattice models Interstitial solutions and compounds with more than one type of site are written with the compound energy formalism (Hillert and Staffansson). Each sublattice has its own set of species, including vacancies (Va). For austenite or ferrite in the Fe–C system, carbon occupies interstitial sites that are mostly empty, giving the model (Fe)a(C, Va)c, with a = c = 1 for fcc and a = 1, c = 3 for bcc. Intermetallics such as Fe3C or sigma phase use sublattices with composition-dependent occupancy, and a stoichiometric compound is the limiting case with fixed site fractions. Magnetism and ordering Ferromagnetic transitions change the Gibbs energy by an amount large enough to move phase boundaries (it is why iron is bcc at low temperature and fcc between about 912 and 1394 °C). They are included with a separate magnetic term based on the Curie temperature and the Bohr magneton number (Inden–Hillert–Jarl model). Chemical ordering, for example B2 from bcc, is handled by using the same Gibbs energy for the ordered and disordered phases with an additional sublattice description for the ordered state."},{"n":4,"l":"Topic 4","t":"Thermodynamics and CALPHAD","h":"7. What can be calculated","u":"computational/thermodynamics-and-calphad.html#calc","x":"Calculation What it gives Typical use Equilibrium at fixed T, p, composition Stable phases, their amounts and compositions Heat treatment windows, solubility limits, second-phase amounts Property diagram (phase fraction versus T) Transformation temperatures, fraction curves Solvus, solidus and liquidus; Ae1 and Ae3 of steels Phase diagram and isopleth Phase boundaries in a plane of composition and T Alloy design; checking the effect of a new addition Scheil–Gulliver solidification Solidification path assuming no diffusion in the solid and complete mixing in the liquid Segregation, last-liquid composition, hot-cracking tendency Constrained equilibrium (for example paraequilibrium) Equilibrium with some elements held fixed, such as interstitial carbon moving while substitutional atoms do not Fast transformations, bainite and martensite thermodynamics Driving force Gibbs energy change for forming a new phase from the current state Nucleation models; precipitation and phase-field inputs Activities and chemical potentials Effective concentrations Slag–metal reactions, oxidation, diffusion couples"},{"n":4,"l":"Topic 4","t":"Thermodynamics and CALPHAD","h":"8. A short calculation","u":"computational/thermodynamics-and-calphad.html#python","x":"The open-source Python package pycalphad calculates equilibria from a TDB database. The sketch below finds the equilibrium phases of a 2 at.% carbon iron alloy over a temperature range, assuming you have a suitable Fe–C database file: from pycalphad import Database, equilibrium, variables as v dbf = Database(\"Fe-C.tdb\") # your thermodynamic database comps = [\"FE\", \"C\", \"VA\"] phases = [\"LIQUID\", \"BCC_A2\", \"FCC_A1\", \"CEMENTITE\"] eq = equilibrium( dbf, comps, phases, {v.X(\"C\"): 0.02, v.T: (600, 1800, 10), v.P: 101325, v.N: 1}, ) # eq.Phase, eq.NP (phase fractions) and eq.X (compositions) vs temperature Phase names depend on the database. A database is licensed data: some are free and published with the assessment, others are commercial. Always check which composition and temperature ranges a database was assessed for before trusting an answer outside them."},{"n":4,"l":"Topic 4","t":"Thermodynamics and CALPHAD","h":"9. Limits and coupling","u":"computational/thermodynamics-and-calphad.html#limits","x":"Equilibrium only. CALPHAD has no time, geometry or interfaces. It says where a system is heading, not how fast or in what shape. Database quality. Parameters are fitted to the data that existed; predictions outside the assessed range, or for a phase with sparse data, rest on extrapolation. A ternary or higher-order prediction made from binary data alone can be wrong when real ternary interactions are strong. Missing phases. If a phase is absent from the database (or from your list of candidate phases), it cannot be predicted. Metastable phases may be included deliberately. Local equilibrium assumption. Used in diffusion calculations, it holds when interfaces are fast compared with diffusion. Coupling extends what CALPHAD can do. Diffusion mobility databases assessed in the same way as thermodynamics (used in DICTRA and similar programs) enable one-dimensional simulation of diffusion-controlled transformations and homogenisation. Phase-field models take their free-energy densities, driving forces and chemical potentials from CALPHAD. DFT supplies formation energies of compounds and end-member energies. The thermodynamic input of larger models is therefore often a CALPHAD calculation. Key takeaways Equilibrium means minimum Gibbs energy, equivalently equal chemical potentials. The common tangent gives phase compositions and the lever rule gives amounts. For a regular solution the miscibility gap closes at Tc = Omega/2R; the spinodal lies inside the binodal. CALPHAD fits a Gibbs energy model to every phase (Redlich-Kister terms, sublattices, magnetism) and stores the parameters in a database. It delivers equilibria, solidification paths and driving forces. It does not deliver kinetics or geometry. Database coverage limits accuracy, and multicomponent extrapolation from binary data needs checking."},{"n":4,"l":"Topic 4","t":"Thermodynamics and CALPHAD","h":"10. Check your understanding","u":"computational/thermodynamics-and-calphad.html#check","x":"A regular solution has Ω = 20 kJ/mol. At what temperature does the miscibility gap close? Tc = Ω/(2R) = 20 000/(2 × 8.314) ≈ 1 200 K. An alloy with 30 at.% B splits into phases of 10 at.% and 70 at.% B. What fraction is the B-rich phase? Lever rule: (30 − 10)/(70 − 10) = 20/60 ≈ 33%. Why does the ideal entropy of mixing make perfectly pure phases impossible? The slope of RT(x ln x + (1 − x) ln(1 − x)) is infinite at x = 0 and x = 1, so adding even a tiny amount of a second component always lowers the Gibbs energy. Every real phase therefore dissolves some of every component, however small."},{"n":4,"l":"Topic 4","t":"Thermodynamics and CALPHAD","h":"11. References and further reading","u":"computational/thermodynamics-and-calphad.html#refs","x":"Lukas, H. L., Fries, S. G. and Sundman, B., Computational Thermodynamics: The Calphad Method, Cambridge University Press (2007). Saunders, N. and Miodownik, A. P., CALPHAD: Calculation of Phase Diagrams, Pergamon (1998). Hillert, M., Phase Equilibria, Phase Diagrams and Phase Transformations, Cambridge University Press. Gaskell, D. R. and Laughlin, D. E., Introduction to the Thermodynamics of Materials, CRC Press. Kaufman, L. and Bernstein, H., Computer Calculation of Phase Diagrams, Academic Press (1970). Dinsdale, A. T., SGTE data for pure elements, Calphad 15, 317 (1991). Hillert, M. and Staffansson, L.-I., The regular solution model for stoichiometric phases and ionic melts, Acta Chem. Scand. 24, 3618 (1970). Otis, R. and Liu, Z.-K., pycalphad: CALPHAD-based computational thermodynamics in Python, J. Open Res. Softw. 5, 1 (2017). Bocklund, B. et al., ESPEI for efficient thermodynamic database development, modification and uncertainty quantification, MRS Commun. 9, 618 (2019). Previous topicStatistical mechanics for simulation Next topicDensity functional theory in practice"},{"n":5,"l":"Topic 5","t":"Density functional theory in practice","h":"Density functional theory in practice","u":"computational/density-functional-theory.html","x":"Topics/ElectronsTopic 5 Density functional theory in practice Density functional theory (DFT) computes the energy and electronic structure of a solid from its atomic positions alone. It is the workhorse behind most first-principles materials data: formation energies, elastic constants, defect energies, band structures and the training sets for machine-learned potentials. Reading timeabout 13 min Builds on2. Crystals and cellsB2. Physics Leads to11. Machine learning CategoryElectrons After reading this page you should be able to: Explain the Kohn–Sham idea and the role of the exchange–correlation functional. Choose among common functionals and know the typical errors of each. List the numerical parameters that must be converged and how to test them. Describe how to obtain lattice parameters, elastic constants, defect energies, barriers and phonons from DFT."},{"n":5,"l":"Topic 5","t":"Density functional theory in practice","h":"1. The electronic structure problem","u":"computational/density-functional-theory.html#problem","x":"The nuclei of a solid are about 2000 times heavier than electrons, so electrons respond almost instantly to nuclear motion. In the Born–Oppenheimer approximation the nuclei are fixed and the electrons are solved for each arrangement, giving an energy surface E(R1, …, RN) on which the nuclei move; its negative gradients are the forces. This surface is what every classical interatomic potential tries to approximate. The electrons obey the many-body Schrödinger equation, whose wavefunction depends on 3 coordinates per electron. Exact solutions are impossible beyond a few electrons. DFT replaces the wavefunction with the electron density n(r), a function of only three coordinates no matter how many electrons there are."},{"n":5,"l":"Topic 5","t":"Density functional theory in practice","h":"2. The Kohn–Sham scheme","u":"computational/density-functional-theory.html#ks","x":"The Hohenberg–Kohn theorems (1964) prove that the ground-state energy is a functional of the density and is minimised by the true density. They do not say what the functional is. Kohn and Sham (1965) made it usable by mapping the interacting system onto fictitious non-interacting electrons that have the same density, and splitting the energy as E[n] = Ts[n] + ∫ vext(r) n(r) dr + EH[n] + Exc[n] The terms are, in order: the kinetic energy of the non-interacting electrons, the energy in the potential of the nuclei, the classical electrostatic (Hartree) energy of the density with itself, and the exchange–correlation energy Exc, which contains the quantum exchange of identical electrons, their correlated motion, and the correction to the kinetic energy. Minimising E[n] gives one-electron equations: [ −½∇² + veff(r) ] ψi(r) = εi ψi(r), veff = vext + vH[n] + vxc[n], n(r) = Σocc |ψi(r)|² (in atomic units). The orbitals ψi and eigenvalues εi are mathematical devices to build the density; only the total energy and the density have exact meaning. Reading band structures from the Kohn–Sham eigenvalues is a useful but approximate practice."},{"n":5,"l":"Topic 5","t":"Density functional theory in practice","h":"3. Exchange–correlation functionals","u":"computational/density-functional-theory.html#xc","x":"Everything not computed exactly is in Exc, which must be approximated. The choice of approximation is the main modelling decision in a DFT calculation, climbing a ladder of increasing ingredients and cost. Level Depends on Examples Typical behaviour Local density (LDA) Density at a point Ceperley–Alder / PZ Lattice parameters too small by 1–3%; bonds too strong; wrong ground state for iron Generalised gradient (GGA) Density and its gradient PBE, PBEsol, PW91 PBE lattice parameters 1–2% too large; PBEsol closer to experiment for solids; correct bcc ferromagnetic Fe; band gaps too small Meta-GGA Adds kinetic-energy density SCAN, r2SCAN, TPSS Better energetics and some correlated systems at modest extra cost Hybrid Mixes in a fraction of exact exchange PBE0, HSE06 Much better band gaps and localised-state description; 10 to 100 times the cost of GGA in plane-wave codes Beyond Many-body perturbation GW, random-phase approximation Quasiparticle band gaps, van der Waals; very expensive Common corrections Spin polarisation. Magnetic materials such as Fe, Co, Ni and many oxides need separate spin-up and spin-down densities. The starting magnetic moments and the magnetic configuration (ferromagnetic, antiferromagnetic) must be chosen deliberately, and different starting guesses can converge to different local solutions. DFT+U. An on-site Hubbard term corrects over-delocalisation of localised d and f electrons (transition-metal oxides, rare earths). U is a parameter and results can depend on it. Dispersion. Standard functionals miss van der Waals attraction. Empirical corrections (the “D3” scheme) or non-local functionals (vdW-DF) are used for layered materials, molecular crystals and adsorption. Practical rules: PBE is the default for metals and alloys. For energy differences between similar structures the errors often cancel, so DFT is far more reliable for relative energies than for absolute ones. Formation energies from GGA typically carry errors of order 0.1 eV per atom, which is large compared with the energy differences that separate competing phases, so conclusions about borderline stability need care. Band gaps from semi-local functionals are consistently underestimated."},{"n":5,"l":"Topic 5","t":"Density functional theory in practice","h":"4. Basis sets and pseudopotentials","u":"computational/density-functional-theory.html#basis","x":"The Kohn–Sham orbitals must be expanded in a finite basis. In a crystal the potential is periodic, and Bloch’s theorem says each orbital has the form ψnk(r) = eik·r unk(r), with u periodic and k a point in the Brillouin zone. Two families of basis dominate: Plane waves (VASP, Quantum ESPRESSO, ABINIT, CASTEP) expand u in Fourier components exp(iG·r) of the reciprocal lattice. The basis is controlled by one number, the cutoff energy: all plane waves with ½|k + G|² ≤ Ecut are included. It is systematic: raising Ecut always improves the basis. Localised orbitals (Gaussians in CP2K and Gaussian; numerical atomic orbitals in SIESTA and FHI-aims) are efficient for large and molecular systems and offer linear-scaling options, but need care over basis-set completeness. Core electrons are chemically inert but have rapidly oscillating wavefunctions that would need an enormous plane-wave basis. Pseudopotentials (and the closely related projector-augmented-wave, PAW, method) replace the nucleus and core electrons by an effective potential for the valence electrons, smooth inside a core radius. Choices include norm-conserving, ultrasoft and PAW datasets. Semi-core states (for example 3p in transition metals) should be included in the valence set when they influence the property. All-electron methods (such as FLAPW) avoid pseudopotentials at higher cost and are used as a reference."},{"n":5,"l":"Topic 5","t":"Density functional theory in practice","h":"5. Sampling reciprocal space","u":"computational/density-functional-theory.html#kpoints","x":"Many quantities (the density, the total energy) are integrals over the Brillouin zone, evaluated as a sum over a finite set of k-points. The standard choice is a uniform Monkhorst–Pack grid, reduced by symmetry. Insulators converge with a few points because their occupation is smooth. Metals have a sharp Fermi surface, so they need dense grids and a smearing function that smooths the occupation numbers: Gaussian, Methfessel–Paxton, Marzari–Vanderbilt (cold smearing) or Fermi–Dirac. Smearing introduces a small error that depends on the width; the tetrahedron method with Blöchl corrections is used for accurate densities of states. A useful rule: k-point density should scale inversely with cell size. A 4-atom cell may need a 12×12×12 grid where a 256-atom supercell of the same crystal converges with 2×2×2 or 3×3×3."},{"n":5,"l":"Topic 5","t":"Density functional theory in practice","h":"6. The self-consistency cycle","u":"computational/density-functional-theory.html#scf","x":"The Kohn–Sham equations are non-linear: the potential depends on the density, which depends on the orbitals, which depend on the potential. They are solved by iteration. Guess a density, build the potential, solve for orbitals, form a new density, and repeat until input and output densities agree. The self-consistent field cycle. The dot follows one pass around the loop; each pass costs a diagonalisation of the Hamiltonian. Simply replacing the old density with the new one oscillates or diverges, so a mixing scheme (Pulay/DIIS or Broyden) combines the history of densities. Convergence is declared when the change in energy or density drops below a tolerance (for example 10−6 eV in VASP for routine work, tighter for phonons). Slow or failed convergence usually has a physical cause: a metallic system with too little smearing, an incorrect magnetic starting guess, a nearly degenerate state, or a bad structure. Once the electronic problem is converged, forces and stress follow from the Hellmann–Feynman theorem, and the structure can be relaxed by moving atoms (and the cell) downhill in energy."},{"n":5,"l":"Topic 5","t":"Density functional theory in practice","h":"7. Convergence","u":"computational/density-functional-theory.html#convergence","x":"Every numerical parameter must be tested, because a result that has not converged is a result of the parameters, not of the material. The method is the same each time: vary one parameter while holding the others fixed and watch the quantity of interest (not just the total energy). Parameter What it controls Typical test Comment Plane-wave cutoff Basis completeness Raise until energy differences change by under about 1 meV per atom Stress converges more slowly than energy; relaxing a cell needs a higher cutoff or a fixed-basis re-run k-point grid Brillouin-zone integration Increase grid density until the property changes by under tolerance Metals need more than insulators Smearing width Fermi-surface treatment Reduce width while increasing k density Report the scheme and width used Supercell size Defect–image interaction Repeat in larger cells; extrapolate See supercells and periodic boundaries Vacuum thickness, slab thickness Surface and interface models Increase until the surface energy stops changing Both matter Force and SCF tolerances Relaxation accuracy Tighten until structure and energy stop changing Typical force tolerance 0.01 eV/Å; phonons need much smaller When comparing energies of different structures, use the same cutoff, pseudopotentials and equivalent k-point density in all of them, so that errors cancel. Test with differences, not absolute energies."},{"n":5,"l":"Topic 5","t":"Density functional theory in practice","h":"8. What to compute","u":"computational/density-functional-theory.html#calc","x":"Structure and equation of state Compute the total energy at several volumes (relaxing atoms at each), fit an equation of state, and read off the equilibrium volume V0, the bulk modulus B0 = −V(∂p/∂V) and its pressure derivative. The Birch–Murnaghan form is the standard fit: E(V) = E0 + (9V0B0/16) { [(V0/V)2/3 − 1]3 B′0 + [(V0/V)2/3 − 1]2 [6 − 4(V0/V)2/3] } Formation energies and phase stability The formation energy per atom of a compound AxBy relative to the elements in their ground-state structures is Ef = [ E(AxBy) − x E(A) − y E(B) ] / (x + y) A negative value means the compound is stable against decomposition into the elements; stability against all competing phases requires comparing with the lowest-energy combination, the convex hull. DFT formation energies are the input for many CALPHAD assessments. Elastic constants Apply small strains ε, relax atoms, and fit the stress σ = Cε. For a cubic crystal there are three independent constants C11, C12, C44, the bulk modulus is B = (C11 + 2C12)/3, mechanical stability requires C11 − C12 > 0, C11 + 2C12 > 0 and C44 > 0, and the Zener anisotropy ratio is A = 2C44/(C11 − C12). Polycrystalline moduli follow by averaging (Voigt, Reuss, Hill). Defects, interfaces and barriers The vacancy formation energy in a supercell of N atoms is Efvac = E(N−1) − [(N−1)/N] E(N), and solute, interstitial and surface energies follow the same logic of subtracting reference energies. Energy barriers for atomic hops come from the nudged elastic band method, which relaxes a chain of images between the initial and final states while keeping them evenly spaced; the climbing-image variant (Henkelman et al.) converges the highest image onto the saddle point. For vacancy diffusion the activation energy is Q = Ef + Em (formation plus migration), which compares directly with experiment. Vibrations and temperature Phonon frequencies are obtained by displacing atoms in a supercell and recording forces (finite displacements, programs such as Phonopy) or by perturbation theory. The phonon spectrum gives the vibrational free energy in the harmonic or quasi-harmonic approximation (see statistical mechanics), thermal expansion, heat capacity and the stability of a structure: an imaginary frequency means the structure is dynamically unstable. Ab initio molecular dynamics propagates the atoms using DFT forces directly, capturing anharmonicity but lim"},{"n":5,"l":"Topic 5","t":"Density functional theory in practice","h":"9. Cost and accuracy","u":"computational/density-functional-theory.html#cost","x":"With conventional diagonalisation, DFT cost grows roughly as the cube of the number of atoms. Practical limits are hundreds of atoms for routine work and a few thousand with large computing resources; hybrid functionals and large k-grids make the calculation heavier by an order of magnitude or more. Linear-scaling and GPU-accelerated implementations extend the range. An important finding for reproducibility: a large community comparison of about 40 code and pseudopotential combinations on 71 elemental crystals found that modern implementations agree closely when the same functional is used, so the largest source of disagreement in a published DFT result is usually the functional and the setup, not the code (Lejaeghere et al., 2016). Known weaknesses to keep in mind: semi-local functionals under-bind and over-delocalise in strongly correlated systems; energy barriers can be underestimated; and DFT at 0 K ignores finite-temperature entropy unless added separately."},{"n":5,"l":"Topic 5","t":"Density functional theory in practice","h":"10. Inputs in common codes","u":"computational/density-functional-theory.html#inputs","x":"The parameters above appear under different names in each program. The table maps a few widely used ones. Concept VASP Quantum ESPRESSO Cutoff energy ENCUT (eV) ecutwfc (Ry), ecutrho k-point grid KPOINTS file K_POINTS automatic Smearing ISMEAR, SIGMA occupations, smearing, degauss SCF tolerance EDIFF (eV) conv_thr (Ry) Spin polarisation ISPIN = 2, MAGMOM nspin = 2, starting_magnetization Relaxation IBRION, ISIF, NSW, EDIFFG calculation = 'relax' or 'vc-relax' Units differ (eV against Rydberg), as do conventions for the functional and pseudopotential: record these exactly, since they are part of the result. Key takeaways DFT solves for the electron density through the Kohn-Sham equations; only the exchange-correlation energy is approximated. The functional (LDA, GGA, meta-GGA, hybrid, plus U, van der Waals) sets the typical errors. Relative energies are more reliable than absolute ones. Converge the cutoff, k-points, smearing and cell size before trusting any number. Standard outputs are lattice constants, elastic constants, formation and defect energies, barriers by nudged elastic band, and phonons. Cost grows roughly as N cubed, which limits cells to hundreds or a few thousand atoms, and results are for 0 K unless extended."},{"n":5,"l":"Topic 5","t":"Density functional theory in practice","h":"11. Check your understanding","u":"computational/density-functional-theory.html#check","x":"Your computed vacancy formation energy changes from 1.1 eV to 1.5 eV when you double the supercell. What does that suggest? The small cell was not converged: the vacancy was interacting with its periodic images. Keep enlarging until the value stabilises, or check convergence against both cell size and k-points, since both are changing in practice. Why must two structures being compared be computed with the same cutoff and equivalent k-point density? Basis and sampling errors are systematic and partly cancel only when they are the same in both calculations. Using different settings leaves a difference in numerical error that can exceed the physical energy difference. A phonon calculation returns an imaginary frequency. What does it mean? The structure is at a saddle point rather than a minimum of the energy surface: it is dynamically unstable and would distort spontaneously. It can also indicate an unconverged calculation, so test the convergence before drawing a physical conclusion."},{"n":5,"l":"Topic 5","t":"Density functional theory in practice","h":"12. References and further reading","u":"computational/density-functional-theory.html#refs","x":"Sholl, D. S. and Steckel, J. A., Density Functional Theory: A Practical Introduction, Wiley (2009). Martin, R. M., Electronic Structure: Basic Theory and Practical Methods, Cambridge University Press (2004). Hohenberg, P. and Kohn, W., Phys. Rev. 136, B864 (1964); Kohn, W. and Sham, L. J., Phys. Rev. 140, A1133 (1965). Perdew, J. P., Burke, K. and Ernzerhof, M., Phys. Rev. Lett. 77, 3865 (1996). Sun, J., Ruzsinszky, A. and Perdew, J. P., Strongly constrained and appropriately normed semilocal density functional, Phys. Rev. Lett. 115, 036402 (2015). Heyd, J., Scuseria, G. E. and Ernzerhof, M., Hybrid functionals based on a screened Coulomb potential, J. Chem. Phys. 118, 8207 (2003). Blöchl, P. E., Projector augmented-wave method, Phys. Rev. B 50, 17953 (1994). Monkhorst, H. J. and Pack, J. D., Special points for Brillouin-zone integrations, Phys. Rev. B 13, 5188 (1976). Kresse, G. and Furthmüller, J., Efficient iterative schemes for ab initio total-energy calculations using a plane-wave basis set, Phys. Rev. B 54, 11169 (1996). Giannozzi, P. et al., QUANTUM ESPRESSO, J. Phys.: Condens. Matter 21, 395502 (2009). Henkelman, G., Uberuaga, B. P. and Jónsson, H., A climbing image nudged elastic band method, J. Chem. Phys. 113, 9901 (2000). Togo, A. and Tanaka, I., First principles phonon calculations in materials science, Scr. Mater. 108, 1 (2015). Lejaeghere, K. et al., Reproducibility in density functional theory calculations of solids, Science 351, aad3000 (2016). Previous topicThermodynamics and CALPHAD Next topicMolecular dynamics"},{"n":6,"l":"Topic 6","t":"Molecular dynamics","h":"Molecular dynamics","u":"computational/molecular-dynamics.html","x":"Topics/AtomsTopic 6 Molecular dynamics Molecular dynamics (MD) is the closest thing to a movie of matter: place atoms in a box, compute the force on each from an interatomic potential, move them a tiny step, and repeat a few million times. Averages over the trajectory give thermodynamic and transport properties, and the pictures show mechanisms. Reading timeabout 11 min Builds on2. Crystals and cells3. Statistical mechanicsB4. Numerical methods Leads to11. Machine learning CategoryAtoms After reading this page you should be able to: Write the velocity-Verlet algorithm and choose a sensible time step. Compare the main classes of interatomic potential and say how to validate one. Describe thermostats and barostats and which ensembles they produce. Plan a simulation from construction to analysis, and judge what MD can and cannot reach."},{"n":6,"l":"Topic 6","t":"Molecular dynamics","h":"1. The idea","u":"computational/molecular-dynamics.html#idea","x":"Classical MD treats atoms as point masses that obey Newton’s second law under forces derived from a potential energy function U of all the positions: mi d²ri/dt² = Fi = −∇i U(r1, …, rN) There are three ingredients: a potential that supplies the forces, an integrator that advances the positions and velocities, and the conditions (cell, boundaries, temperature, pressure). Starting from initial positions and random velocities, the trajectory samples the ensemble defined by those conditions (see statistical mechanics). The following simulation shows a two-dimensional crystal of Lennard-Jones atoms being heated: atoms start vibrating about lattice sites, then the lattice loses order and atoms begin to wander. A real molecular dynamics run: 144 atoms interacting through the Lennard-Jones potential in two dimensions, periodic boundaries, temperature ramped from low to high (reduced units). Colour shows how far each atom has moved: blue atoms stay near their sites, red atoms have travelled."},{"n":6,"l":"Topic 6","t":"Molecular dynamics","h":"2. Integrating the equations","u":"computational/molecular-dynamics.html#integration","x":"A good integrator should be cheap (one force evaluation per step, because forces dominate the cost), time-reversible and symplectic, so that the energy does not drift over long runs. The standard choice is the velocity-Verlet algorithm. With time step Δt: v(t + Δt/2) = v(t) + F(t) Δt / 2m r(t + Δt) = r(t) + v(t + Δt/2) Δt compute F(t + Δt) from the new positions v(t + Δt) = v(t + Δt/2) + F(t + Δt) Δt / 2m Its error per step is of order Δt3, but its structure preserves a nearby “shadow” energy, so the total energy fluctuates slightly without drifting if Δt is small enough. Trajectories themselves are chaotic: two runs that start a hair apart diverge exponentially, so individual trajectories are not reproducible over long times, but statistical averages are. Choosing the time step The step must resolve the fastest motion in the system, roughly one-tenth to one-twentieth of the shortest vibrational period. Metals with embedded-atom potentials commonly use 1 to 5 fs; systems with light atoms (hydrogen) or stiff bonds need 0.5 fs or less. The practical test is to run in the NVE ensemble and check that the total energy is conserved: a steady drift or large fluctuations mean the step is too long, the cutoff is too abrupt or the potential is not smooth. Each step is 10−15 s, so a nanosecond needs 106 steps and a microsecond 109."},{"n":6,"l":"Topic 6","t":"Molecular dynamics","h":"3. Interatomic potentials","u":"computational/molecular-dynamics.html#potentials","x":"The potential is a model of the Born–Oppenheimer energy surface (see DFT). The faster the potential, the simpler its functional form and the more physics it leaves out. The simplest, the Lennard-Jones pair potential, is a good model for noble gases and a useful teaching and testing tool: U(r) = 4ε [ (σ/r)12 − (σ/r)6 ], minimum at r = 21/6σ, depth ε This interactive explorer needs JavaScript to run. Class Idea Used for Cautions Pair (Lennard-Jones, Morse, Buckingham) Energy is a sum over pairs Noble gases, simple ionic and model systems Cannot reproduce metals’ elastic anisotropy (forces C12 = C44, the Cauchy relation) Embedded atom (EAM) and MEAM E = Σi Fi(ρi) + ½Σij φ(rij), where ρi is a sum of neighbour contributions; MEAM adds angular dependence fcc, bcc and hcp metals and alloys; defects, surfaces, plasticity Fitted to bulk properties; check stacking-fault energy, surface energy, melting point Bond-order and covalent (Tersoff, Stillinger–Weber, AIREBO) Bond strength depends on local coordination Si, Ge, C, SiC, BN Limited transferability between phases Reactive (ReaxFF, COMB) Bond order determined dynamically; charges equilibrated Oxidation, combustion, catalysis Slow; each parameter set is for a defined chemistry Charged systems (Coulomb + short-range) Long-range electrostatics (Ewald, PPPM) plus repulsion Oxides, salts, ceramics Long-range sums are expensive; need charge neutrality Machine-learned (GAP, MTP, ACE, SNAP, neural-network) Flexible functions of local atomic environments, fitted to DFT energies, forces and stresses Complex chemistry at near-DFT accuracy Valid only inside the training data; 10 to 1000 times slower than EAM Universal / foundation models (for example M3GNet, CHGNet, MACE-MP) Machine-learned potentials trained on very large DFT databases across the periodic table Quick screening, initial exploration Systematic softening of energy surfaces; validate before relying Validating a potential A potential is a model that must pass tests for the property you want. A minimal checklist, compared with experiment or DFT: lattice parameter and cohesive energy of the relevant structures; elastic constants and phonon spectrum; point-defect formation energies and migration barriers; surface energies, stacking-fault energies, grain-boundary energies; melting temperature, thermal expansion; relative energies of competing phases (is the ground stat"},{"n":6,"l":"Topic 6","t":"Molecular dynamics","h":"4. Neighbour lists and cost","u":"computational/molecular-dynamics.html#efficiency","x":"Short-range potentials are truncated at a cutoff rc, so each atom interacts with the neighbours within it. Checking every pair would scale as N2. Instead a neighbour list (Verlet list) records atoms within rc plus a skin distance and is rebuilt only every few steps, using spatial cell lists to find candidates quickly; the cost per step then scales as N. Large runs divide space into domains handled by different processors (domain decomposition, with MPI) and exchange atoms near the domain edges, so billions of atoms are feasible on supercomputers. Long-range Coulomb forces use Ewald summation or particle-mesh methods (PPPM, PME), scaling as N log N."},{"n":6,"l":"Topic 6","t":"Molecular dynamics","h":"5. Controlling temperature and pressure","u":"computational/molecular-dynamics.html#control","x":"With no control the simulation conserves energy (NVE). To model an experiment at constant temperature or pressure, a thermostat or barostat is added. The algorithms differ in whether they produce the correct ensemble. Method Controls Correct ensemble? Note Velocity rescaling T No Simple; used for quick equilibration only (the animation above uses it to ramp temperature) Berendsen T, p No (suppresses fluctuations) Robust and fast to equilibrate; not for production runs that need fluctuations Nosé–Hoover (chains) T Yes, canonical Standard choice; deterministic; the damping time sets coupling Stochastic velocity rescaling (CSVR / Bussi) T Yes, canonical Efficient equilibration and correct fluctuations Langevin T Yes, canonical Adds friction and random forces; perturbs dynamics, so not for diffusion Parrinello–Rahman, Martyna–Tobias–Klein p (cell shape and size) Yes, isothermal–isobaric Allows shear and shape change, needed for solid–solid transformations Thermostat strength matters: strong coupling distorts the natural dynamics, so for transport coefficients the system is equilibrated with a thermostat and then run in NVE."},{"n":6,"l":"Topic 6","t":"Molecular dynamics","h":"6. Workflow","u":"computational/molecular-dynamics.html#workflow","x":"Build the structure and cell (see crystal structures and periodic cells), with box edges at least twice the cutoff. Minimise the energy (conjugate gradient or FIRE) to remove overlaps and high forces before dynamics begin. Assign velocities from the Maxwell–Boltzmann distribution at the target temperature, remove net momentum. Equilibrate, usually NVT then NPT, until energy, temperature, pressure and density stop drifting. Produce the data run in the chosen ensemble, saving configurations and properties at intervals longer than a few vibrational periods. Analyse and estimate uncertainties from independent runs or block averages."},{"n":6,"l":"Topic 6","t":"Molecular dynamics","h":"7. Analysis","u":"computational/molecular-dynamics.html#analysis","x":"Structure. Radial distribution function g(r) and coordination numbers; common neighbour analysis or polyhedral template matching labels each atom as fcc, bcc, hcp or other, revealing stacking faults, twins and grain boundaries; the centrosymmetry parameter highlights defects. Defects. The dislocation extraction algorithm (DXA in OVITO) finds dislocation lines and Burgers vectors; Wigner–Seitz analysis counts vacancies and interstitials after radiation damage. Transport. Diffusion from the slope of the mean-squared displacement, MSD = 6Dt in three dimensions, using unwrapped coordinates (details in diffusion and kinetics); viscosity and thermal conductivity from Green–Kubo integrals or non-equilibrium set-ups. Mechanics. The stress tensor from the virial; stress–strain curves from imposed deformation; elastic constants from small strains or fluctuations. Thermodynamics. Melting temperature by two-phase coexistence (solid and liquid in one box: the interface advances toward melting or freezing depending on temperature); free energies by thermodynamic integration."},{"n":6,"l":"Topic 6","t":"Molecular dynamics","h":"8. Applications in materials","u":"computational/molecular-dynamics.html#applications","x":"MD is strongest where atomic mechanisms control behaviour on nanosecond time scales: tensile and compression tests of nanowires and nanopillars; nanoindentation; dislocation nucleation and motion at interfaces; grain-boundary sliding and migration; dislocation–precipitate and dislocation–solute interaction; crack propagation and brittle–ductile behaviour at a crack tip; shock compression; radiation-damage cascades; melting, solidification and nucleation of simple systems; diffusion in liquids and fast-diffusion solids; thermal conductivity of nanostructures."},{"n":6,"l":"Topic 6","t":"Molecular dynamics","h":"9. Limits","u":"computational/molecular-dynamics.html#limits","x":"Time scale. Nanoseconds to microseconds. Processes governed by rare events with barriers much above kBT are not reached directly. Strain rate. Deformation is typically driven at 107 to 1010 s−1, some ten orders of magnitude faster than a tensile test, and flow stresses are correspondingly high. Rate effects must be considered before comparing with experiment. Length scale. Millions to billions of atoms span tens to hundreds of nanometres, far below a grain in an engineering alloy, so surfaces and boundary effects are enormous. Potential accuracy. The result cannot be better than the energy surface supplied. Electronic effects (magnetism, charge transfer) are absent unless built in. Classical nuclei. Quantum effects (zero-point energy, tunnelling of hydrogen) are neglected, and classical heat capacity is wrong at low temperature."},{"n":6,"l":"Topic 6","t":"Molecular dynamics","h":"10. A minimal input","u":"computational/molecular-dynamics.html#lammps","x":"LAMMPS is the most widely used open-source MD code. This input equilibrates a 4000-atom copper crystal at 300 K and zero pressure with an EAM potential (the potential file ships with the program): units metal boundary p p p lattice fcc 3.615 region box block 0 10 0 10 0 10 create_box 1 box create_atoms 1 box mass 1 63.546 pair_style eam pair_coeff * * Cu_u3.eam velocity all create 300.0 12345 mom yes rot no fix 1 all npt temp 300.0 300.0 0.1 iso 0.0 0.0 1.0 timestep 0.002 # 2 fs in metal units (time in ps) thermo 100 run 10000 In LAMMPS “metal” units, distance is in Å, energy in eV, time in ps and pressure in bar. The numbers 0.1 and 1.0 on the npt line are the temperature and pressure damping times in ps. Key takeaways MD integrates Newton's equations with velocity-Verlet. Use a time step of about a tenth to a twentieth of the shortest vibrational period and check energy conservation in NVE. The potential defines the accuracy, so validate it on the property you need. Use canonical thermostats and proper barostats (Nose-Hoover, stochastic velocity rescaling, Parrinello-Rahman) for production runs. The workflow is build, minimise, equilibrate, produce and analyse, with error bars from independent runs. Limits: nanoseconds to microseconds, very high strain rates, classical nuclei and no electrons."},{"n":6,"l":"Topic 6","t":"Molecular dynamics","h":"11. Check your understanding","u":"computational/molecular-dynamics.html#check","x":"A NVE run shows the total energy rising steadily. List three things to check. (1) The time step may be too large for the fastest vibration. (2) The potential or its cutoff may be discontinuous, so the force jumps at rc (use a smoothly truncated or shifted form). (3) A neighbour-list skin that is too small can cause missed interactions when atoms move; also check for overlapping atoms in the starting structure. Why can one not use Berendsen coupling for a calculation of heat capacity from energy fluctuations? Berendsen coupling damps the energy fluctuations and does not generate the canonical distribution. The fluctuation formula for CV relies on the true canonical variance of the energy, so a canonical thermostat (Nosé–Hoover chains or stochastic velocity rescaling) is needed. A simulated tensile test gives a yield stress ten times the experimental value. Is the potential necessarily wrong? No. Strain rates in MD are far higher than in experiments, and nanoscale samples have few pre-existing dislocation sources, so high stresses are expected. Compare like with like (rate, size, defect content) and check whether the potential reproduces stacking-fault and surface energies, which control the mechanism."},{"n":6,"l":"Topic 6","t":"Molecular dynamics","h":"12. References and further reading","u":"computational/molecular-dynamics.html#refs","x":"Allen, M. P. and Tildesley, D. J., Computer Simulation of Liquids, Oxford University Press. Frenkel, D. and Smit, B., Understanding Molecular Simulation, Academic Press. Verlet, L., Computer “experiments” on classical fluids. I., Phys. Rev. 159, 98 (1967). Daw, M. S. and Baskes, M. I., Embedded-atom method, Phys. Rev. B 29, 6443 (1984). Hoover, W. G., Canonical dynamics: equilibrium phase-space distributions, Phys. Rev. A 31, 1695 (1985). Parrinello, M. and Rahman, A., Polymorphic transitions in single crystals, J. Appl. Phys. 52, 7182 (1981). Bussi, G., Donadio, D. and Parrinello, M., Canonical sampling through velocity rescaling, J. Chem. Phys. 126, 014101 (2007). Behler, J. and Parrinello, M., Generalized neural-network representation of high-dimensional potential-energy surfaces, Phys. Rev. Lett. 98, 146401 (2007). Bartók, A. P. et al., Gaussian approximation potentials, Phys. Rev. Lett. 104, 136403 (2010). Thompson, A. P. et al., LAMMPS, Comput. Phys. Commun. 271, 108171 (2022). Stukowski, A., Visualization and analysis of atomistic simulation data with OVITO, Modelling Simul. Mater. Sci. Eng. 18, 015012 (2010). Previous topicDensity functional theory in practice Next topicMonte Carlo and kinetic Monte Carlo"},{"n":7,"l":"Topic 7","t":"Monte Carlo and kinetic Monte Carlo","h":"Monte Carlo and kinetic Monte Carlo","u":"computational/monte-carlo-methods.html","x":"Topics/AtomsTopic 7 Monte Carlo and kinetic Monte Carlo Where molecular dynamics follows real motion, Monte Carlo (MC) takes a shortcut: it proposes random changes and keeps or rejects them with rules that guarantee the right statistics. Because moves need not be physical, MC reaches equilibrium states that dynamics would take far too long to find, and its kinetic variant reaches seconds and beyond. Reading timeabout 12 min Builds on3. Statistical mechanicsB4. Numerical methods Leads to— CategoryAtoms After reading this page you should be able to: Explain importance sampling, detailed balance and the Metropolis acceptance rule. Set up a lattice-model MC simulation and choose suitable moves. Describe cluster expansions and how they link DFT to alloy thermodynamics. Explain how kinetic Monte Carlo advances physical time, and what it needs as input."},{"n":7,"l":"Topic 7","t":"Monte Carlo and kinetic Monte Carlo","h":"1. Why sample randomly","u":"computational/monte-carlo-methods.html#why","x":"An equilibrium average in the canonical ensemble is an integral over all configurations: ⟨A⟩ = ∫ A(x) exp[−U(x)/kBT] dx / ∫ exp[−U(x)/kBT] dx The space is enormously high-dimensional (3N coordinates, or 2N states for N two-state sites), so grids are impossible. Sampling configurations uniformly at random fails as well: nearly all random configurations (atoms overlapping, or a random jumble of A and B) have very high energy and negligible Boltzmann weight, so the average is dominated by rare lucky samples. Importance sampling draws configurations with probability proportional to their Boltzmann weight, so that the average is simply the mean of the samples, ⟨A⟩ ≈ (1/M)Σm A(xm). The samples are generated by a Markov chain: each new configuration is produced from the current one by a random rule. If the rule satisfies detailed balance, so that the flow from state a to b equals the flow back at equilibrium, π(a) P(a→b) = π(b) P(b→a), π(a) ∝ exp[−U(a)/kBT] (and the chain can reach every state, which is ergodicity), then the chain converges to the Boltzmann distribution."},{"n":7,"l":"Topic 7","t":"Monte Carlo and kinetic Monte Carlo","h":"2. Metropolis algorithm","u":"computational/monte-carlo-methods.html#metropolis","x":"The simplest rule satisfying detailed balance is due to Metropolis and co-workers (1953). Given a symmetric proposal (the probability of proposing a→b equals that of b→a): Pick a trial move (for example, flip one spin). Compute the energy change ΔE = Enew − Eold. If ΔE ≤ 0, accept. Otherwise accept with probability exp(−ΔE/kBT). If rejected, keep the old configuration (and count it again in the averages). Pacc(a→b) = min[ 1, exp(−ΔE / kBT) ] Hastings (1970) generalised this to asymmetric proposals by multiplying by the ratio of proposal probabilities. Two practical points: moves that change energy locally can be evaluated cheaply, by recomputing only the affected neighbours, so each step costs O(1) on a lattice; and the acceptance rate should be tuned (often 20 to 50%) by adjusting step sizes, because very high acceptance means moves are too small and very low means they are too large. One sweep is N attempted moves, and sweeps are the natural unit of MC “time”."},{"n":7,"l":"Topic 7","t":"Monte Carlo and kinetic Monte Carlo","h":"3. The Ising model","u":"computational/monte-carlo-methods.html#ising","x":"The Ising model places a spin si = ±1 on each lattice site, with energy E = −J Σ⟨ij⟩ si sj − h Σi si summed over nearest-neighbour pairs. For J > 0 neighbours prefer to align. It is the minimal model of a phase transition: on the two-dimensional square lattice it has an exact solution (Onsager, 1944) with critical temperature kBTc = 2J/ln(1 + √2) ≈ 2.269 J. Below Tc a spontaneous magnetisation appears; above it, thermal disorder wins. The model maps directly to binary alloys and lattice gases, where the spin is the species on a site (A or B, or atom and vacancy) and J is built from pair energies; its ordering transition is the same mathematics as ordering in β-brass. Metropolis Monte Carlo on a 64×64 Ising lattice at T = 1.6 (critical temperature 2.27, in units of J/kB), starting from a random arrangement. Domains of each orientation form and coarsen; the sweep count is shown in the title. This interactive simulation needs JavaScript to run. Near Tc the correlation length diverges and Metropolis single-spin flips suffer critical slowing down: large domains change very slowly. Cluster algorithms (Swendsen–Wang, Wolff) flip whole correlated clusters and remove most of the problem for such models."},{"n":7,"l":"Topic 7","t":"Monte Carlo and kinetic Monte Carlo","h":"4. Moves for materials","u":"computational/monte-carlo-methods.html#moves","x":"The power of MC for materials is the freedom to design moves. Any set of moves that satisfies detailed balance and reaches the whole state space is allowed, whether or not it resembles real dynamics. Move Ensemble What it does Used for Displacement NVT Shifts one atom by a small random vector Off-lattice equilibrium; relaxation Exchange (swap) NVT with fixed composition Swaps the species of two atoms, even far apart Chemical ordering and segregation in alloys, where diffusion in MD is far too slow Volume change NPT Rescales the cell; acceptance includes the pΔV and −NkBT ln(V′/V) terms Thermal expansion, equations of state Insertion / deletion Grand canonical (μVT) Adds or removes an atom against a reservoir Adsorption, gas uptake, vacancy concentrations Species change Semi-grand canonical Changes the identity of a site at fixed chemical-potential difference Δμ Composition as a function of Δμ, phase boundaries of alloys Hybrid MC/MD NVT or NPT Alternates MD steps (relaxing local strain) with swap moves Segregation to grain boundaries, ordering in multicomponent alloys with realistic potentials"},{"n":7,"l":"Topic 7","t":"Monte Carlo and kinetic Monte Carlo","h":"5. Lattice models and cluster expansion","u":"computational/monte-carlo-methods.html#lattice","x":"Real alloys are not Ising models, but their configurational energy can be mapped onto one. A cluster expansion (Sanchez, Ducastelle and Gratias, 1984) writes the energy of any arrangement σ of species on a fixed parent lattice as a sum over clusters of sites (single sites, pairs, triplets, …): E(σ) = Σα mα Jα ⟨Φα(σ)⟩ where Φα are products of site variables over cluster α, mα is a multiplicity and Jα are the effective cluster interactions (ECIs). The ECIs are fitted to a set of DFT energies of ordered structures (typically tens to hundreds of them), and the fit is checked by cross-validation. The expansion is exact if all clusters are included; in practice it is truncated, and the truncation error determines accuracy. Once fitted, the energy of any configuration costs a few operations, so MC can simulate millions of atoms over many sweeps with ab initio energetics. Typical outputs are order–disorder transition temperatures, solvus lines, ground-state structures, short-range order and its temperature dependence, and (with vibrational and electronic free energies added) first-principles phase diagrams. Software packages include ATAT, CASM and icet. The same framework handles interstitial ordering (carbon in iron), vacancies and magnetic degrees of freedom by adding more site variables."},{"n":7,"l":"Topic 7","t":"Monte Carlo and kinetic Monte Carlo","h":"6. Better sampling","u":"computational/monte-carlo-methods.html#advanced","x":"Parallel tempering (replica exchange). Several copies run at different temperatures and periodically swap configurations with acceptance min[1, exp((βi − βj)(Ei − Ej))]. High-temperature replicas cross barriers, low-temperature replicas refine; the combination escapes metastable states. Flat-histogram methods (Wang–Landau, multicanonical). Estimate the density of states g(E) by a random walk that visits all energies equally. From g(E), all thermodynamic quantities at all temperatures follow from one run, including free energies and latent heats. Cluster moves for critical systems, as noted above. Biased moves that propose chemically sensible changes more often, corrected by Hastings ratios."},{"n":7,"l":"Topic 7","t":"Monte Carlo and kinetic Monte Carlo","h":"7. Kinetic Monte Carlo","u":"computational/monte-carlo-methods.html#kmc","x":"Metropolis MC samples equilibrium but its “time” is not physical. Kinetic Monte Carlo (kMC) instead chooses real events with real rates and advances a physical clock. It rests on the picture of a system that sits in a potential-energy basin, vibrates for a long time and then hops (a diffusive jump, a reaction, a dislocation depinning) to another basin; the hops are rare, independent, random events (a Poisson process). Rather than simulating the vibrations, kMC jumps straight from hop to hop. Each possible event i has a rate, typically from harmonic transition-state theory: ki = νi exp( −Ea,i / kBT ) where Ea,i is the activation barrier and νi the attempt frequency (about 1012–1013 s−1, from vibrational frequencies). The rejection-free algorithm (Bortz–Kalos–Lebowitz; also called the residence-time or Gillespie algorithm) is: List all possible events and compute the rates ki and the total rate R = Σi ki. Choose event i with probability ki/R, by drawing a uniform random number and finding where it falls on the cumulative sum of rates. Execute it (move the atom, change the state). Advance the clock by Δt = −ln(u)/R, with u a second uniform random number in (0, 1). Update the event list (only events near the change are affected) and repeat. Every step makes progress, so the method is efficient even when rates are very small, and the time scale can reach seconds, hours or longer; the speed is limited by the fastest events, since the clock moves in steps of 1/R. If some events are much faster than others (for example back-and-forth hops between two sites), they dominate the step count without producing net change, and special treatment (coarse-graining, superbasins) is needed. Input is the bottleneck kMC needs a catalogue of events and their barriers. On a rigid lattice the barriers can be tabulated from DFT nudged-elastic-band calculations, but they depend on the local atomic environment (neighbouring solutes, strain), which leads to either many barriers or a barrier model fitted as a function of environment. Off-lattice (adaptive) kMC finds events on the fly by searching the potential-energy surface from each new minimum, at much higher cost per step but without having to guess the events beforehand. Typical uses Vacancy-mediated diffusion and clustering of solutes in alloys; precipitate nucleation and coarsening; irradiation-damage evolution in reactor steels; "},{"n":7,"l":"Topic 7","t":"Monte Carlo and kinetic Monte Carlo","h":"8. Grain growth with the Potts model","u":"computational/monte-carlo-methods.html#potts","x":"The Potts model generalises Ising to Q states: each site carries an integer (a grain orientation) and neighbouring sites of different orientation cost energy J. Domains of equal integer represent grains, and the boundary between them has an energy proportional to its length. Metropolis dynamics reduces the boundary energy, so grains grow by boundary migration. Studies by Anderson, Srolovitz and co-workers (1984) used this to study normal grain growth, where the classical parabolic law predicts mean size ∝ t1/2, and to examine the grain-size distribution; fitted growth exponents in these models are usually somewhat below one half. The relationship to physical time must be calibrated, boundary mobility and energy are simple functions of misorientation in the basic model, and anisotropy or particle pinning require extensions; the model is nevertheless a popular tool for exploring grain growth and recrystallisation topologically."},{"n":7,"l":"Topic 7","t":"Monte Carlo and kinetic Monte Carlo","h":"9. MC compared with MD","u":"computational/monte-carlo-methods.html#compare","x":"Molecular dynamics Metropolis Monte Carlo Kinetic Monte Carlo Output Trajectory with real dynamics Equilibrium samples Evolution with physical time Time Femtosecond steps, up to microseconds Sweeps; not physical time Physical, up to seconds and beyond Moves Dictated by forces Any move satisfying detailed balance List of real events with rates Needs Forces (potential) Energies only Event catalogue and barriers Best for Mechanisms, short-time dynamics, transport Ordering, segregation, phase equilibrium Slow diffusion-controlled evolution"},{"n":7,"l":"Topic 7","t":"Monte Carlo and kinetic Monte Carlo","h":"10. A minimal code","u":"computational/monte-carlo-methods.html#code","x":"A complete Metropolis sweep for the 2D Ising model, in units where J = kB = 1, is only a few lines of Python: import numpy as np rng = np.random.default_rng(1) N, T = 32, 1.6 s = rng.choice([-1, 1], size=(N, N)) def sweep(s, T): for _ in range(N * N): i, j = rng.integers(N, size=2) nb = s[(i+1) % N, j] + s[(i-1) % N, j] + s[i, (j+1) % N] + s[i, (j-1) % N] dE = 2 * s[i, j] * nb if dE <= 0 or rng.random() < np.exp(-dE / T): s[i, j] *= -1 for _ in range(500): sweep(s, T) print(\"magnetisation per spin:\", s.mean()) Below Tc the magnetisation per spin ends near ±1; above it, it fluctuates near zero. Try T = 3 and compare; then plot energy against sweeps to see equilibration. Key takeaways Importance sampling with detailed balance produces Boltzmann-distributed samples; the Metropolis rule accepts with probability min(1, exp(-dE/kT)). Moves need not be physical: swap moves equilibrate chemical order far faster than dynamics can. Lattice models and cluster expansions carry DFT energetics into alloy thermodynamics at large size. Kinetic Monte Carlo advances physical time, dt = -ln(u)/R, using rates built from barriers. kMC is only as good as its event catalogue and barriers."},{"n":7,"l":"Topic 7","t":"Monte Carlo and kinetic Monte Carlo","h":"11. Check your understanding","u":"computational/monte-carlo-methods.html#check","x":"Why is a rejected move still counted in the averages? Because the Markov chain must stay in state a with the probability 1 − Σb P(a→b) for detailed balance to hold. Repeated states carry weight, which is how low-probability states are visited less often. Dropping rejected steps would bias the average toward high-energy states. A kMC run has rates summing to R = 106 s−1. About how much physical time does one step advance? On average Δt = 1/R = 10−6 s (the expectation of −ln(u)/R is 1/R). A million steps then represent about one second. Why does MC with swap moves equilibrate a ternary alloy much faster than MD? In MD, atoms must diffuse to rearrange, which at ordinary temperatures takes times far beyond what MD can reach. A swap move exchanges two atoms directly regardless of distance and barrier, so the arrangement can sample the equilibrium distribution of chemical order in a small number of sweeps."},{"n":7,"l":"Topic 7","t":"Monte Carlo and kinetic Monte Carlo","h":"12. References and further reading","u":"computational/monte-carlo-methods.html#refs","x":"Landau, D. P. and Binder, K., A Guide to Monte Carlo Simulations in Statistical Physics, Cambridge University Press. Newman, M. E. J. and Barkema, G. T., Monte Carlo Methods in Statistical Physics, Oxford University Press (1999). Metropolis, N. et al., Equation of state calculations by fast computing machines, J. Chem. Phys. 21, 1087 (1953). Hastings, W. K., Monte Carlo sampling methods using Markov chains and their applications, Biometrika 57, 97 (1970). Swendsen, R. H. and Wang, J.-S., Nonuniversal critical dynamics in Monte Carlo simulations, Phys. Rev. Lett. 58, 86 (1987); Wolff, U., Collective Monte Carlo updating for spin systems, Phys. Rev. Lett. 62, 361 (1989). Wang, F. and Landau, D. P., Efficient, multiple-range random walk algorithm to calculate the density of states, Phys. Rev. Lett. 86, 2050 (2001). Sanchez, J. M., Ducastelle, F. and Gratias, D., Generalized cluster description of multicomponent systems, Physica A 128, 334 (1984). Bortz, A. B., Kalos, M. H. and Lebowitz, J. L., A new algorithm for Monte Carlo simulation of Ising spin systems, J. Comput. Phys. 17, 10 (1975). Gillespie, D. T., Exact stochastic simulation of coupled chemical reactions, J. Phys. Chem. 81, 2340 (1977). Voter, A. F., Introduction to the kinetic Monte Carlo method, in Radiation Effects in Solids, Springer (2007). Anderson, M. P., Srolovitz, D. J., Grest, G. S. and Sahni, P. S., Computer simulation of grain growth, Acta Metall. 32, 783 (1984). Previous topicMolecular dynamics Next topicDiffusion and kinetics"},{"n":8,"l":"Topic 8","t":"Diffusion and kinetics","h":"Diffusion and kinetics","u":"computational/diffusion-and-kinetics.html","x":"Topics/KineticsTopic 8 Diffusion and kinetics Thermodynamics says where a material is heading; diffusion usually decides how fast it gets there. Homogenisation, carburising, precipitation, oxidation, sintering, creep and grain growth are all limited by how quickly atoms move. Reading timeabout 13 min Builds on4. Thermodynamics and CALPHADB4. Numerical methods Leads to9. Phase-field10. Finite elements CategoryKinetics After reading this page you should be able to: Write Fick’s laws and use the error-function and Gaussian solutions to estimate diffusion distances. Explain why the real driving force is a chemical-potential gradient, and when diffusion runs uphill. Relate the diffusion coefficient to atomistic jump mechanisms and the Arrhenius law. Outline how D is obtained by DFT, MD and kinetic Monte Carlo, and solve the diffusion equation with a stable finite-difference scheme."},{"n":8,"l":"Topic 8","t":"Diffusion and kinetics","h":"1. Fick’s laws","u":"computational/diffusion-and-kinetics.html#fick","x":"Fick’s first law says that the flux J of atoms (amount per area per time) is proportional to the concentration gradient and directed down it: J = −D ∇c The proportionality constant D is the diffusion coefficient (m² s−1). Combining this with conservation of matter gives Fick’s second law, the diffusion equation: ∂c/∂t = ∇ · ( D ∇c ) → ∂c/∂t = D ∂²c/∂x² (constant D, one dimension) Two closed-form solutions cover many practical problems. For a diffusion couple or a surface held at fixed concentration, the solution involves the error function; for a thin film of total amount M per unit area spreading into a large body, it is a Gaussian: Couple (step initial profile): c(x,t) = ½ (c1 + c2) − ½ (c1 − c2) erf( x / 2√(Dt) ) Surface at cs (carburising): c(x,t) = cs − (cs − c0) erf( x / 2√(Dt) ) Thin film: c(x,t) = M / √(4πDt) · exp( −x² / 4Dt ) A diffusion couple: a sharp step smears out, with the width growing as √(Dt). The curve is the error-function solution with the time shown by the bar below. All three depend on x/√(Dt), so the affected distance grows as the square root of time, and a rule of thumb is the diffusion length ≈ 2√(Dt) (or √(Dt)). Doubling the distance takes four times as long. This is why the time to homogenise a segregated structure scales with the square of the segregation spacing: halving the dendrite arm spacing cuts the required time by four. Worked example. Take carbon in ferrite with illustrative values D0 = 2×10−6 m² s−1 and Q = 84 kJ mol−1 at 700 °C (973 K): D = 2×10−6 exp[−84 000/(8.314 × 973)] ≈ 6×10−11 m² s−1. After one hour, √(Dt) ≈ 0.5 mm: carbon moves far in a thin sheet, while a substitutional element such as manganese, with a diffusivity many orders of magnitude lower at the same temperature, barely moves on the scale of a micrometre."},{"n":8,"l":"Topic 8","t":"Diffusion and kinetics","h":"2. The true driving force","u":"computational/diffusion-and-kinetics.html#force","x":"Fick’s first law with a constant D is an approximation. Atoms move to lower their free energy, so the driving force is the gradient of the chemical potential, not of concentration: Ji = − Mi ci ∇μi → Di = Mi kBT · Θ, Θ = ∂ln ai / ∂ln ci where Mi is the atomic mobility and Θ the thermodynamic factor. In an ideal solution Θ = 1 and Fick’s law holds in its simple form. If unlike atoms attract, Θ > 1 and diffusion is faster than the intrinsic atomic mobility suggests; if like atoms attract so strongly that the Gibbs energy curve has negative curvature (inside the spinodal; see CALPHAD), Θ < 0 and the effective D is negative: atoms diffuse uphill against the concentration gradient, which is spinodal decomposition. Uphill diffusion also occurs in alloys of multiple elements even outside a miscibility gap: in Darken’s classic experiment, carbon moved out of a high-silicon steel into a plain steel that started with the same carbon content, because silicon raises the chemical potential of carbon. Two practical consequences. First, mobilities are the fundamental kinetic quantity, and CALPHAD-style databases store them as functions of composition and temperature next to the thermodynamics. Second, in a binary substitutional couple the interdiffusion coefficient combines the two intrinsic mobilities (Darken): D̃ = ( xB D*A + xA D*B ) Θ The two species typically have different tracer diffusivities D*, so a net flow of vacancies accompanies the net atomic flux; inert markers placed at the original interface then shift relative to the ends of the specimen. This is the Kirkendall effect, and it can leave pores (Kirkendall voids) on the fast-diffusing side."},{"n":8,"l":"Topic 8","t":"Diffusion and kinetics","h":"3. Atomistic mechanisms","u":"computational/diffusion-and-kinetics.html#mechanisms","x":"An atom in a crystal sits in a potential well and occasionally gains enough thermal energy to jump to a neighbouring site. The pathway depends on the type of atom and the defects present: Vacancy mechanism. A substitutional atom swaps with a neighbouring vacancy. It is the dominant route for self-diffusion and for substitutional solutes (Mn, Cr, Ni in iron; Cu in aluminium). Atoms can only move when a vacancy is next to them, so the rate involves both the vacancy concentration and the jump rate. Interstitial mechanism. Small solutes (C, N, H, O) hop between interstitial sites without needing a vacancy. These jumps are fast, with low activation energy, which is why carbon equilibrates in steel in minutes while alloying elements do not. Short-circuit paths. Grain boundaries, dislocation cores and free surfaces have an open structure and diffusivities that can be many orders of magnitude higher than in the lattice, dominating at low temperature and in fine-grained or heavily deformed material. Others. Interstitialcy (a displaced pair), ring and exchange mechanisms matter in some systems, for example in covalent semiconductors. The vacancy mechanism. The dashed ring is the vacancy; the highlighted atom is displaced each time the vacancy passes it. The hop sequence in the animation is illustrative and is replayed in reverse to loop."},{"n":8,"l":"Topic 8","t":"Diffusion and kinetics","h":"4. Temperature dependence","u":"computational/diffusion-and-kinetics.html#arrhenius","x":"A hop requires crossing an energy barrier Em (the migration energy). The jump rate is thermally activated, and the diffusion coefficient follows an Arrhenius law: D = D0 exp( −Q / RT ) Plotting ln D against 1/T gives a straight line of slope −Q/R, and a change of slope signals a change of mechanism (for example lattice to grain-boundary diffusion). The microscopic content comes from the random-walk theory: for jumps of length λ at total rate Γ, D = Γλ²/6 in three dimensions. For vacancy diffusion in a cubic crystal this becomes D = f a² ν exp( −(Ef + Em) / kBT ) (fcc; vibrational entropy factors omitted) where f is the correlation factor (about 0.78 for fcc and 0.73 for bcc, since a tracer atom is slightly more likely to jump back into the vacancy it just left than elsewhere), a the lattice parameter, ν an attempt frequency, and the activation energy of self-diffusion is Q = Ef + Em, vacancy formation plus migration. For interstitial diffusion, Q = Em only, because the available interstitial sites are always there. A useful sanity check is the empirical rule that Q for vacancy self-diffusion in close-packed metals is about 17 to 18 RTm, where Tm is the melting temperature. For copper (Tm = 1358 K) this gives about 200 kJ mol−1, close to the measured value of roughly 200 kJ mol−1. It means that diffusion at a given fraction of the melting temperature (the “homologous temperature”) is similar across many metals."},{"n":8,"l":"Topic 8","t":"Diffusion and kinetics","h":"5. Computing D","u":"computational/diffusion-and-kinetics.html#compute","x":"Several complementary routes exist, matched to how fast the species moves. Method What it gives Strength Limit Molecular dynamics D from the mean-squared displacement, D = lim MSD/(6t); or from the velocity autocorrelation integral Direct, includes all anharmonicity; ideal for liquids, fast ionic conductors, grain boundaries at high T Only practical for D above roughly 10−11 m² s−1 within nanoseconds, so lattice diffusion in solids is out of reach well below the melting point DFT + transition-state theory Ef, Em (nudged elastic band), attempt frequency ν from phonons (Vineyard), then D by the formula above Parameter-free; works for any T; resolves each solute–vacancy interaction Needs many calculations; assumes harmonic transition-state theory and a defined mechanism Five-frequency model (Lidiard–LeClaire) Solute diffusion and vacancy drag in dilute fcc alloys from five jump frequencies computed by DFT Compact route from DFT to solute D and the Onsager coefficients Dilute limit; fcc geometry (other lattices need extensions) Kinetic Monte Carlo D from the MSD of tracer atoms in a lattice with environment-dependent barriers Reaches physical time for slow diffusion; handles concentrated alloys and correlations Needs the barrier model (see Monte Carlo) Databases and assessment Mobility parameters fitted to experiments Directly usable in diffusion codes with thermodynamic data Only for systems already assessed Whichever route is used, results should be compared with experiments at one or more temperatures. Agreement within a factor of two in D is considered good, because a 0.1 eV error in the barrier changes D by a factor of about 3 at 1000 K and by about 10 at 500 K."},{"n":8,"l":"Topic 8","t":"Diffusion and kinetics","h":"6. Solving the equation numerically","u":"computational/diffusion-and-kinetics.html#numerics","x":"When D depends on composition, when several phases are present, or when the geometry is complicated, there is no closed-form solution and the equation is solved on a grid. For one dimension with spacing Δx and time step Δt, the simplest scheme is the explicit (forward-time, centred-space) method: cin+1 = cin + r ( ci+1n − 2cin + ci−1n ), r = D Δt / Δx² It is stable only if r ≤ ½ (and r ≤ 1/4 in two dimensions, 1/6 in three); exceeding it makes errors grow exponentially and the solution oscillate. Refining the grid by a factor of two forces the time step down by four, so explicit schemes become expensive. Implicit schemes (backward Euler) are stable for any step; they require solving a tridiagonal linear system at each step, which is cheap in one dimension. The Crank–Nicolson average of the two is second-order accurate in time. For conserving the total amount of solute, which matters in diffusion couples, a finite-volume formulation (fluxes across cell faces) is preferred. Boundary and interface conditions Fixed concentration (Dirichlet): a surface in equilibrium with a carburising atmosphere. Zero flux (Neumann): a symmetry plane or an impermeable wall. Mass-transfer (Robin): the flux at the surface is J = β(cgas − csurface), with a finite transfer coefficient β. Moving interface between phases α and β: concentrations on either side are set by local equilibrium (from thermodynamics) and the interface velocity v follows from solute conservation, (cβ − cα)v = Jα − Jβ. This Stefan-type problem is the basis of diffusion-controlled transformation models in steels. An explicit solver in Python, with the surface held at c = 1 and zero flux at the far end, fits in a few lines. The time step is chosen with r = 0.2 for safety: import numpy as np D, L, nx = 1e-11, 1e-3, 201 # m^2/s, m, grid points dx = L / (nx - 1) dt = 0.2 * dx**2 / D # r = D*dt/dx^2 = 0.2 (stable: r <= 0.5) c = np.zeros(nx); c[0] = 1.0 # surface at c = 1, initially empty t = 0.0 while t < 3600: # one hour c[1:-1] += D * dt / dx**2 * (c[2:] - 2*c[1:-1] + c[:-2]) c[0], c[-1] = 1.0, c[-2] # fixed surface, zero flux at the far end t += dt Compare the result with the error-function solution c = 1 − erf(x/2√(Dt)) as a first verification, then change r to 0.6 and watch the solution blow up. This interactive solver needs JavaScript to run."},{"n":8,"l":"Topic 8","t":"Diffusion and kinetics","h":"7. Multicomponent diffusion","u":"computational/diffusion-and-kinetics.html#multi","x":"In an alloy with several solutes, the flux of each element depends on the gradient of all the others: Ji = − Σj Dij ∇cj The full Dij matrix has off-diagonal terms (cross-diffusion) of either sign, and Onsager reciprocity constrains it. Practically, one works with chemical potentials and mobilities, Ji = −Σj Lij ∇μj, with mobilities from a database and thermodynamics from CALPHAD, so cross effects (like carbon moving in a silicon gradient) come out automatically. The difference between interstitial and substitutional diffusivities, often six or more orders of magnitude, makes the equations stiff and motivates special treatments such as paraequilibrium, where carbon redistributes while substitutional elements are frozen."},{"n":8,"l":"Topic 8","t":"Diffusion and kinetics","h":"8. From diffusion to microstructure","u":"computational/diffusion-and-kinetics.html#micro","x":"Diffusion enters models of microstructure change in a few standard ways. Nucleation. Classical nucleation theory gives the barrier for a spherical nucleus, ΔG* = 16πγ³/(3ΔGv²), where γ is the interface energy and ΔGv the volume driving force (from CALPHAD, less any strain energy); the rate is proportional to exp(−ΔG*/kBT) times an atom-attachment term controlled by diffusion. Growth. Diffusion-controlled growth of a particle or plate is parabolic in time: thickness or radius ∝ √(Dt), with a proportionality set by the supersaturation. Coarsening. Small precipitates dissolve and large ones grow because of the Gibbs–Thomson effect (higher solubility around curved surfaces). The Lifshitz–Slyozov–Wagner (LSW) theory gives r3 − r03 = Kt, with K proportional to D, γ and the equilibrium solubility, which sets how long strengthening precipitates stay fine at service temperature. Overall transformation kinetics. The Johnson–Mehl–Avrami–Kolmogorov equation, f = 1 − exp(−ktn), combines nucleation and growth into a sigmoidal fraction-transformed curve; the exponent n hints at the mechanism. Models that resolve the microstructure itself, such as phase-field, solve diffusion and interface motion together. Key takeaways Diffusion distance grows as the square root of Dt, so the time needed scales with the square of the length. The real driving force is the chemical-potential gradient; diffusion can run uphill, and Darken and Kirkendall effects follow from unequal mobilities. D = D0 exp(-Q/RT). For vacancy diffusion Q is formation plus migration energy; for interstitials it is the migration energy alone. D can come from MD (fast species), DFT with transition-state theory, or kinetic Monte Carlo, and should be checked against experiment. An explicit finite-difference scheme is stable only for r = D dt/dx squared of at most one half in one dimension."},{"n":8,"l":"Topic 8","t":"Diffusion and kinetics","h":"9. Check your understanding","u":"computational/diffusion-and-kinetics.html#check","x":"A homogenisation anneal takes 10 hours for segregation with a 100 µm spacing. How long for a 50 µm spacing at the same temperature? The time scales as the square of the length (t ∝ x²/D), so halving the spacing needs one quarter of the time: 2.5 hours. Why is the activation energy of vacancy self-diffusion larger than the migration energy alone? An atom can only move if a vacancy is adjacent, and vacancies must be created thermally at an energy cost Ef. The equilibrium vacancy fraction contributes a factor exp(−Ef/kBT), so the total activation energy is Ef + Em. Your explicit diffusion solver gives wild oscillations. What do you check first? The stability number r = DΔt/Δx². It must be at most 0.5 in one dimension. Reduce Δt (or coarsen Δx), or switch to an implicit scheme."},{"n":8,"l":"Topic 8","t":"Diffusion and kinetics","h":"10. References and further reading","u":"computational/diffusion-and-kinetics.html#refs","x":"Crank, J., The Mathematics of Diffusion, Oxford University Press (1975). Shewmon, P., Diffusion in Solids, Springer. Mehrer, H., Diffusion in Solids: Fundamentals, Methods, Materials, Diffusion-Controlled Processes, Springer (2007). Balluffi, R. W., Allen, S. M. and Carter, W. C., Kinetics of Materials, Wiley (2005). Porter, D. A., Easterling, K. E. and Sherif, M. Y., Phase Transformations in Metals and Alloys, CRC Press. Darken, L. S., Diffusion, mobility and their interrelation through free energy in binary metallic systems, Trans. AIME 175, 184 (1948); Darken, L. S., Diffusion of carbon in austenite with a discontinuity in composition, Trans. AIME 180, 430 (1949). Smigelskas, A. D. and Kirkendall, E. O., Zinc diffusion in alpha brass, Trans. AIME 171, 130 (1947). Vineyard, G. H., Frequency factors and isotope effects in solid state rate processes, J. Phys. Chem. Solids 3, 121 (1957). Andersson, J.-O. and Ågren, J., Models for numerical treatment of multicomponent diffusion in simple phases, J. Appl. Phys. 72, 1350 (1992). Previous topicMonte Carlo and kinetic Monte Carlo Next topicPhase-field modelling"},{"n":9,"l":"Topic 9","t":"Phase-field modelling","h":"Phase-field modelling","u":"computational/phase-field-modelling.html","x":"Topics/MicrostructureTopic 9 Phase-field modelling Microstructure is made of interfaces: grain boundaries, phase boundaries, solidification fronts. Tracking each one explicitly through merging, splitting and branching is a bookkeeping nightmare. Phase-field models sidestep it by describing the whole microstructure with smooth fields and letting the interfaces take care of themselves. Reading timeabout 12 min Builds on4. Thermodynamics and CALPHAD8. Diffusion and kinetics Leads to— CategoryMicrostructure After reading this page you should be able to: Describe a diffuse interface and how it relates to interface energy and width. Write the Allen–Cahn and Cahn–Hilliard equations and say which describes which kind of process. Explain spinodal decomposition, curvature-driven motion and coarsening from the equations. List the inputs a quantitative phase-field model needs and the numerical constraints on grid and time step."},{"n":9,"l":"Topic 9","t":"Phase-field modelling","h":"1. The diffuse-interface idea","u":"computational/phase-field-modelling.html#idea","x":"In a sharp-interface description, two phases are separated by a surface of zero thickness, whose motion must be computed and tracked as part of the problem. In a phase-field description, an order parameter φ(r, t) takes one constant value inside each phase (say φ = −1 and φ = +1) and varies smoothly across a thin transition layer. The interface is wherever φ passes through its mid-value, found after the fact. The motion follows from the equations without special rules, and topology changes (two grains merging, a particle splitting, dendrite arms branching) need no extra treatment. Two types of field appear: Non-conserved order parameters, such as phase identity or crystal orientation, can change locally without the quantity being transported from elsewhere (a region of liquid can freeze in place). Conserved fields, such as composition, can change only by diffusion: material that arrives at one place must leave from another. Many materials problems combine both, such as solidification of an alloy (a phase field plus a concentration field)."},{"n":9,"l":"Topic 9","t":"Phase-field modelling","h":"2. The free-energy functional","u":"computational/phase-field-modelling.html#functional","x":"The model is built on the total free energy of the microstructure as a functional of the fields: F[φ, c] = ∫V [ f(φ, c, T) + (κφ/2) |∇φ|² + (κc/2) |∇c|² + fel + … ] dV The bulk term f carries the thermodynamics: for a two-phase system it has a double-well shape with a minimum for each phase, for example f = (A/4)(φ² − 1)². In alloys it is taken from CALPHAD (see thermodynamics). The gradient terms penalise sharp changes, so they set the cost of an interface: without them the field would jump discontinuously to whichever well is lower at each point. The competition between the double well (which wants φ at a minimum) and the gradient term (which wants φ uniform) gives a finite-width interface. For the double well above the equilibrium one-dimensional profile solves κφ″ = f′(φ), and the solution can be written exactly: φ(x) = tanh( x / ℓ ), ℓ = √(2κ/A), γ = (2√2 / 3) √(κA) The parameters κ and A are thus tied to two physical quantities: the interface energy γ and an interface width (about 2ℓ from the 10–90% points of the profile). Setting them to reproduce a chosen γ and width is the first step in building a model. Additional terms add other physics: elastic energy for misfitting precipitates, magnetic or electric energy, anisotropy in κφ (which yields crystal facets and dendrite arms)."},{"n":9,"l":"Topic 9","t":"Phase-field modelling","h":"3. Allen–Cahn: non-conserved fields","u":"computational/phase-field-modelling.html#ac","x":"The simplest dynamics is relaxation toward lower free energy at a rate proportional to the driving force: ∂φ/∂t = − L δF/δφ = L [ κ ∇²φ − f′(φ) ] with L a kinetic coefficient (interface mobility). This is the Allen–Cahn equation (originally for antiphase boundaries). Two consequences follow. The free energy never increases, dF/dt ≤ 0, so the system moves downhill. And an interface moves with normal velocity proportional to its mean curvature K, toward its centre of curvature: v = − L κ K → for a circle of radius R in two dimensions: d(R²)/dt = −2 L κ so the area of a circular grain shrinks linearly with time, and the smaller of two circles vanishes first, the essential mechanism of normal grain growth. The animation below solves the equation for two circular regions of different size: curved boundaries retreat, the small circle disappears first, and the large one shrinks more slowly because its curvature is lower. Allen–Cahn dynamics in two dimensions (a real numerical solution). The boundary of each disc moves toward its centre of curvature at a speed proportional to its curvature. For many grains, one uses a set of order parameters φ1, …, φn, one per orientation, coupled so that at most one is large at any point (the Fan–Chen model for grain growth) or constrained to sum to one (multi-phase-field models). Misorientation-dependent mobility and energy enter through the coefficients."},{"n":9,"l":"Topic 9","t":"Phase-field modelling","h":"4. Cahn–Hilliard: conserved fields","u":"computational/phase-field-modelling.html#ch","x":"For a conserved quantity such as composition, the flux is driven by the gradient of the chemical potential μ = δF/δc (the thermodynamic driving force from the diffusion page), and the equation is a continuity equation: ∂c/∂t = ∇ · ( M ∇μ ), μ = f′(c) − κ ∇²c This is the Cahn–Hilliard equation. It is fourth-order in space (the ∇² acts on μ, which already contains ∇²c), conserves the total amount of solute, and also lowers the free energy. Spinodal decomposition Suppose a homogeneous alloy with c = c0 sits inside the spinodal, where f″(c0) < 0 (see the Gibbs energy curve). Linearise the equation for a small fluctuation of wavenumber k: it grows as exp[R(k)t] with R(k) = − M k² ( f″(c0) + κ k² ) For f″ < 0 this is positive in a band of wavenumbers (k < √(−f″/κ)): long-wavelength fluctuations are slow because material must travel far, and short-wavelength ones are suppressed by the interface-energy cost. In between there is a fastest-growing wavenumber, km = √(−f″/2κ), which sets the characteristic spacing of the early microstructure, λm = 2π/km. No nucleation barrier is involved; any random noise seeds the pattern. After the compositions reach the two equilibrium values, the structure coarsens: small domains dissolve and large ones grow to lower the total interface area, with a mean domain size increasing as t1/3 (diffusion-limited coarsening). Cahn–Hilliard spinodal decomposition in two dimensions (a real numerical solution of the equation, periodic boundaries). A nearly uniform mixture, seeded with small random noise, develops composition waves, forms droplets of the minority phase and then coarsens. The mixture here is off-critical, which gives droplets; a 50:50 mixture would give an interconnected pattern."},{"n":9,"l":"Topic 9","t":"Phase-field modelling","h":"5. Models for materials","u":"computational/phase-field-modelling.html#models","x":"Process Typical model Key point Solidification of alloys Wheeler–Boettinger–McFadden (WBM); Kim–Kim–Suzuki (KKS); thin-interface models with an anti-trapping current (Karma) KKS separates interface and bulk contributions by assuming equal chemical potentials inside the interface; the thin-interface limit allows an interface much wider than physical while still giving correct kinetics Grain growth and recrystallisation Fan–Chen multiple order parameters; multi-phase-field (Steinbach) Boundary energy and mobility depend on misorientation; stored energy drives recrystallisation Precipitation and spinodal decomposition Cahn–Hilliard with elastic energy (Khachaturyan, Chen) Misfit strain selects shapes and alignment of precipitates Diffusional and displacive solid-state transformations Concentration field plus phase fields; martensite models with transformation strain Variants and habit planes from elastic energy Fracture Variational approach (Bourdin–Francfort–Marigo; Miehe) A damage field φ ∈ [0,1] degrades stiffness; regularised crack surface energy Domain structures in ferroelectrics and ferromagnets Time-dependent Ginzburg–Landau equations Polarisation or magnetisation as order parameter Corrosion, electrochemistry, sintering Coupled phase-field with transport Moving boundaries and multiple physics"},{"n":9,"l":"Topic 9","t":"Phase-field modelling","h":"6. Where parameters come from","u":"computational/phase-field-modelling.html#parameters","x":"A phase-field model is only quantitative if its inputs are. The ingredients and their usual sources: Bulk free energy: CALPHAD databases (compositions, driving forces, chemical potentials), with DFT for compounds not covered. Interface energies and their anisotropy: measured (dihedral angles, equilibrium shapes) or computed by atomistic simulation (see molecular dynamics and DFT). Mobilities: diffusion mobilities from databases (see diffusion); interface mobilities from experiments (grain growth, recrystallisation) or from MD. Elastic constants and misfit strains: from DFT, lattice parameters and experiments. Interface width: a numerical choice. Physical interfaces are of nanometre width, but resolving them across a micrometre-scale microstructure is prohibitive; the width is therefore chosen as large as the microstructure features allow, and the model is built (or the thin-interface analysis applied) so that results do not depend on it. Check that results are independent of the interface width and the grid spacing before drawing conclusions."},{"n":9,"l":"Topic 9","t":"Phase-field modelling","h":"7. Numerics","u":"computational/phase-field-modelling.html#numerics","x":"Resolution. The interface must be resolved by roughly 4 to 8 grid points across its width. With a coarser grid the interface becomes artificially pinned to the grid and moves in jumps. Explicit time stepping is simple but severely limited: for Allen–Cahn the time step must satisfy roughly Δt < Δx²/(4Lκ) in two dimensions, and for Cahn–Hilliard roughly Δt < Δx&sup4;/(32Mκ) because of the fourth-order term, so halving the grid spacing costs sixteen times as many steps. Semi-implicit spectral methods treat the linear gradient terms implicitly in Fourier space and the nonlinear bulk term explicitly. They are fast on periodic domains and allow much larger steps (Chen and Shen, 1998); the animation above uses this approach. Finite elements handle complex geometry and adaptive mesh refinement, which concentrates resolution at interfaces and can reduce cost by orders of magnitude in three dimensions. Large 3D simulations rely on parallel computing, GPUs and adaptive grids. Open-source frameworks include MOOSE (finite elements, with a phase-field module), PRISMS-PF, FiPy, OpenPhase and FEniCS-based codes; MICRESS and Pace3D are commercial and research multi-phase-field programs respectively."},{"n":9,"l":"Topic 9","t":"Phase-field modelling","h":"8. A short program","u":"computational/phase-field-modelling.html#code","x":"The following solves the Cahn–Hilliard equation on a periodic square with the semi-implicit Fourier scheme (non-dimensional units, f = (c² − 1)²/4, M = 1, grid spacing 1). Plot c every few hundred steps to reproduce the animation above. import numpy as np N, dt, kappa = 128, 0.2, 1.0 c = -0.25 + 0.03 * np.random.randn(N, N) # off-critical mixture plus noise k = 2 * np.pi * np.fft.fftfreq(N) k2 = k[:, None]**2 + k[None, :]**2 for step in range(5000): f_hat = np.fft.fft2(c**3 - c) # f'(c) for the double well c = np.real(np.fft.ifft2((np.fft.fft2(c) - dt * k2 * f_hat) / (1 + dt * kappa * k2**2))) print(\"mean c is conserved:\", c.mean()) Change the initial mean to 0 to get the interconnected pattern; change κ to alter the domain size; and check that the average composition stays constant, a property of the conservative equation."},{"n":9,"l":"Topic 9","t":"Phase-field modelling","h":"9. Strengths and limits","u":"computational/phase-field-modelling.html#limits","x":"Strengths. No front tracking; arbitrary topology; the free-energy structure makes it easy to couple thermodynamics, elasticity, electromagnetism and other physics; results are visual, with morphology directly comparable with micrographs. Limits. Cost grows quickly with domain size and with the need to resolve the interface; quantitative accuracy depends on how well the interface energy, mobility and (especially) anisotropy are known; many parameters are poorly known and are calibrated; the diffuse interface can create artefacts (solute trapping, spurious interface effects) unless the model is formulated carefully; and the result is a continuum description, so atomic-scale features (dislocation cores, interface structure) must be supplied from below. Three dimensions and long times are expensive. Phase-field is best used with a clear question about morphology or kinetics, in a regime where the microstructure scale (tens of nanometres to millimetres) is what matters. Key takeaways Interfaces are smooth transitions of an order parameter, so topology changes need no special treatment. Allen-Cahn (non-conserved) gives curvature-driven motion; Cahn-Hilliard (conserved) gives spinodal decomposition and t to the 1/3 coarsening. Interface energy and width fix the model parameters: gamma = (2 sqrt 2 / 3) sqrt(kappa A) and width ~ sqrt(2 kappa / A). Inputs come from CALPHAD (free energy), diffusion databases (mobility) and atomistics (interface energy); results must not depend on the interface width or the grid. Resolve the interface with about 4 to 8 grid points; explicit steps are tightly limited, so use semi-implicit spectral or adaptive finite elements."},{"n":9,"l":"Topic 9","t":"Phase-field modelling","h":"10. Check your understanding","u":"computational/phase-field-modelling.html#check","x":"For the double well f = (A/4)(φ² − 1)², what happens to the interface width and interface energy if κ is quadrupled? The width ℓ = √(2κ/A) doubles, and the interface energy γ = (2√2/3)√(κA) also doubles. Widths and energies cannot be set independently without changing both κ and A. A Cahn–Hilliard run with a time step typical for Allen–Cahn becomes unstable. Why? The Cahn–Hilliard equation has a fourth-order spatial operator, so the explicit stability limit scales as Δx4 instead of Δx². The same step that is safe for Allen–Cahn can be far too large. Use a smaller step or a semi-implicit scheme. Why does a mixture inside the spinodal decompose with a characteristic length scale, instead of at the largest wavelength? Growth rate R(k) is zero at k = 0 (no net transport is possible) and negative above a cut-off set by the interface cost, so it has a maximum at an intermediate wavenumber km. That mode grows fastest and dominates the early microstructure."},{"n":9,"l":"Topic 9","t":"Phase-field modelling","h":"11. References and further reading","u":"computational/phase-field-modelling.html#refs","x":"Provatas, N. and Elder, K., Phase-Field Methods in Materials Science and Engineering, Wiley-VCH (2010). Chen, L.-Q., Phase-field models for microstructure evolution, Annu. Rev. Mater. Res. 32, 113 (2002). Moelans, N., Blanpain, B. and Wollants, P., An introduction to phase-field modeling of microstructure evolution, Calphad 32, 268 (2008). Steinbach, I., Phase-field models in materials science, Modelling Simul. Mater. Sci. Eng. 17, 073001 (2009). Cahn, J. W. and Hilliard, J. E., J. Chem. Phys. 28, 258 (1958); Allen, S. M. and Cahn, J. W., Acta Metall. 27, 1085 (1979). Wheeler, A. A., Boettinger, W. J. and McFadden, G. B., Phase-field model for isothermal phase transitions in binary alloys, Phys. Rev. A 45, 7424 (1992). Kim, S. G., Kim, W. T. and Suzuki, T., Phase-field model for binary alloys, Phys. Rev. E 60, 7186 (1999). Karma, A. and Rappel, W.-J., Quantitative phase-field modeling of dendritic growth in two and three dimensions, Phys. Rev. E 57, 4323 (1998). Steinbach, I. et al., A phase field concept for multiphase systems, Physica D 94, 135 (1996). Fan, D. and Chen, L.-Q., Computer simulation of grain growth using a continuum field model, Acta Mater. 45, 611 (1997). Chen, L. Q. and Shen, J., Applications of semi-implicit Fourier-spectral method to phase field equations, Comput. Phys. Commun. 108, 147 (1998). Previous topicDiffusion and kinetics Next topicThe finite element method"},{"n":10,"l":"Topic 10","t":"The finite element method","h":"The finite element method","u":"computational/finite-element-method.html","x":"Topics/ContinuumTopic 10 The finite element method At the scale of components and processes, a material is treated as a continuum and its behaviour is governed by partial differential equations for heat, stress and flow. The finite element method (FEM) is the standard way to solve them on real geometries, and it is where the microstructure and properties computed at finer scales finally meet engineering use. Reading timeabout 13 min Builds on8. Diffusion and kineticsB4. Numerical methods Leads to— CategoryContinuum After reading this page you should be able to: State the governing equations of thermal and mechanical continuum problems. Explain the weak form, shape functions and the assembly of the stiffness matrix. Perform and interpret a mesh-convergence study. Describe how nonlinear, transient and coupled problems are solved and what inputs a material model needs."},{"n":10,"l":"Topic 10","t":"The finite element method","h":"1. Governing equations","u":"computational/finite-element-method.html#equations","x":"Continuum models express conservation laws together with a description of the material (the constitutive law). The most common in materials work are: Physics Equation Unknown Heat conduction ρcp ∂T/∂t = ∇·(k∇T) + q Temperature T Mechanical equilibrium (static) ∇·σ + b = 0, ε = ½(∇u + ∇uT) Displacement u Linear elasticity (isotropic) σ = λ tr(ε) I + 2μ ε Stress σ from strain ε Fluid flow (incompressible) ρ(∂v/∂t + v·∇v) = −∇p + μ∇²v + f, ∇·v = 0 Velocity v, pressure p Species transport ∂c/∂t + v·∇c = ∇·(D∇c) + r Concentration c Here σ is the Cauchy stress, b a body force, λ and μ the Lamé constants (μ is the shear modulus), k the thermal conductivity, q a heat source and ρ the density. Real problems couple these: heat generated by plastic work, thermal strain from temperature gradients, phase transformations that release latent heat and change volume."},{"n":10,"l":"Topic 10","t":"The finite element method","h":"2. From strong to weak form","u":"computational/finite-element-method.html#weak","x":"The equations above, the strong form, require derivatives that may not exist in real problems (a stress jump at an interface between two materials, a point load). The FEM starts from an equivalent weak form. Multiply the equilibrium equation by an arbitrary test function v (a virtual displacement), integrate over the body, and use integration by parts to move one derivative onto v. For linear elasticity: ∫Ω ε(v) : C : ε(u) dV = ∫Ω v · b dV + ∫Γt v · t dS for all admissible v This is the principle of virtual work: internal virtual work equals external. It needs only first derivatives, handles discontinuous material properties naturally, and has the boundary traction t built in, so natural boundary conditions (applied forces, fluxes) appear automatically, while essential boundary conditions (prescribed displacement or temperature) must be imposed on the trial function."},{"n":10,"l":"Topic 10","t":"The finite element method","h":"3. Discretisation","u":"computational/finite-element-method.html#discretise","x":"The domain is divided into small elements (triangles or quadrilaterals in 2D; tetrahedra, hexahedra or prisms in 3D) joined at nodes. Within each element the unknown is approximated by simple shape functions Ni(x) that equal 1 at node i and 0 at the other nodes, so the nodal values are the unknowns: u(x) ≈ Σi Ni(x) ui Choosing the test functions from the same space (the Galerkin method) turns the weak form into a linear system for the nodal values: K u = f, Kij = ∫ BiT C Bj dV, fi = ∫ Ni b dV + ∫ Ni t dS where B contains derivatives of the shape functions (it turns nodal displacements into strains). The stiffness matrix K is built element by element and assembled into the global matrix, with integrals evaluated by Gauss quadrature on a reference element and mapped to the real shape (isoparametric elements). It is sparse, symmetric and positive definite for stable problems, and is solved by direct sparse solvers for moderate sizes or iterative solvers (conjugate gradients with preconditioning, multigrid) for large ones. After imposing the essential boundary conditions, solve for u; strains and stresses follow by differentiation within each element. Element choice: linear elements represent displacement piecewise linearly (so strain is piecewise constant); quadratic elements use mid-side nodes and represent curved variation far better for the same number of elements. Higher-order elements converge faster in smooth regions; low-order elements are more robust for contact and large deformation."},{"n":10,"l":"Topic 10","t":"The finite element method","h":"4. A worked example","u":"computational/finite-element-method.html#example","x":"An elastic bar of length L, cross-section area A and modulus E is clamped at the left end and loaded by a force P at the right end. Use two equal linear elements of length h = L/2 with three nodes. For a two-node element the element stiffness matrix is ke = (EA / h) [ [1, −1], [−1, 1] ] Assembling the two elements gives a 3×3 matrix. With node 1 fixed (u1 = 0), the reduced system for nodes 2 and 3 is (EA / h) [ [2, −1], [−1, 1] ] [ u2, u3 ]T = [ 0, P ]T Solving gives u2 = Ph/(EA) and u3 = 2Ph/(EA) = PL/(EA), which is the exact analytical result for tip displacement. (In one dimension the nodal values are exact for such problems; in 2D and 3D, they are not.) The strain in each element is the difference of nodal displacements divided by h, giving constant strain P/(EA) and stress P/A everywhere, again exact."},{"n":10,"l":"Topic 10","t":"The finite element method","h":"5. Meshes and convergence","u":"computational/finite-element-method.html#mesh","x":"FEM solutions approximate the true solution, and the error depends on how fine and good the mesh is. For a sufficiently smooth solution and element polynomial degree p, the error in displacement falls as hp+1 and the error in strain and stress (the energy norm) as hp, where h is the element size. For linear elements, halving h cuts the displacement error by about four. Refining the mesh. The piecewise-linear approximation of u(x) = sin(πx) with 2, 4, 8 and 16 elements; the stated maximum error falls by a factor of about four each time the elements are halved. Mesh convergence study A result is trustworthy only if it stops changing when the mesh is refined. The procedure: solve on at least three meshes with a systematic refinement ratio, tabulate the quantity of interest (peak stress, displacement, temperature, reaction force), and check that it converges. Richardson extrapolation of the three values estimates both the converged value and the discretisation error. Mesh quality and refinement Refine where gradients are steep: around holes, notches, contact zones, welds, interfaces, crack tips. Adaptive refinement does this automatically from an error estimate. Element shape matters. Avoid very elongated or distorted elements; check aspect ratio, skewness and Jacobian. Stress singularities. At a sharp re-entrant corner or crack tip the exact stress is infinite, and refinement makes the computed peak stress grow without converging. Use a fillet of realistic radius, special crack-tip elements, or a quantity that is finite (such as the J-integral) instead of the peak stress. Locking. Low-order elements can be far too stiff when the physics constrains deformation: nearly incompressible behaviour (plasticity, rubber) causes volumetric locking; thin bending-dominated structures cause shear locking. Remedies are reduced integration (with hourglass control), selective or “B-bar” integration, mixed formulations and higher-order elements. A useful analytical check is the Kirsch problem: a small circular hole in a wide plate under uniaxial tension has a peak stress of three times the applied stress, at the edge of the hole. A model that does not recover a factor near 3 on a fine mesh has a set-up error."},{"n":10,"l":"Topic 10","t":"The finite element method","h":"6. Nonlinear and transient problems","u":"computational/finite-element-method.html#nonlinear","x":"Most interesting problems are nonlinear: material nonlinearity (plasticity, creep, temperature-dependent properties), geometric nonlinearity (large rotations and strains), and contact (boundary conditions that change with the solution). The equations become R(u) = fext − fint(u) = 0 and are solved iteratively by Newton–Raphson: linearise with the tangent stiffness KT = ∂fint/∂u, solve for the correction, update and repeat until the residual is small. The load is applied in increments so that each Newton solve starts close to the solution, and line searches or arc-length methods help with softening and snap-through. For time-dependent problems the semi-discrete equations have the form M dT/dt + KT = f (thermal) or Ma + Cv + Ku = f (dynamic, with v and a the nodal velocity and acceleration), and are advanced in time by a scheme analogous to those in the diffusion page. Explicit schemes (central difference) have no equation solve per step but are conditionally stable, with step limited by the time a wave takes to cross the smallest element (roughly h/c, with c the wave speed); they suit high-speed events and forming with complicated contact. Implicit schemes (backward Euler, Newmark) are stable for larger steps but require a nonlinear solve each step; they suit slow, quasi-static and thermal problems."},{"n":10,"l":"Topic 10","t":"The finite element method","h":"7. Constitutive models","u":"computational/finite-element-method.html#materials","x":"The model is only as good as the constitutive law, which is where the lower-scale methods feed in. Typical choices for metals: Elastic constants (E, ν, or the full tensor for anisotropic crystals), temperature-dependent. Plasticity: a yield criterion (von Mises for isotropic metals, Hill or Barlat for sheet anisotropy), a flow rule and a hardening law. Common hardening forms are the Hollomon power law σ = Kεn, the Voce saturation law and the Johnson–Cook model with strain-rate and temperature terms. Viscoplasticity and creep: Norton power law ε̇ = Aσn exp(−Q/RT), with the Arrhenius dependence linking to diffusion. Crystal plasticity: grain-level slip with orientation, giving texture and anisotropy from microstructure (Section 8). Thermo-physical data as functions of temperature: conductivity, specific heat (including latent heat), density, thermal expansion; and boundary conditions such as heat-transfer coefficients and friction coefficients, which are frequently the largest uncertainty in process models. Parameters are calibrated against tests (tension and compression across temperature and rate, relaxation, creep). Fit the model to some experiments and check it against others not used for the fit."},{"n":10,"l":"Topic 10","t":"The finite element method","h":"8. Applications in materials","u":"computational/finite-element-method.html#applications","x":"Application What is modelled Notes Casting and continuous casting Heat flow with latent heat release, fluid flow in the melt, shrinkage, stresses The enthalpy method handles latent heat; coupling with CALPHAD gives the solid-fraction curve Welding Moving heat source (for example the Goldak double-ellipsoid), distortion and residual stress, phase transformation in the heat-affected zone Residual stress depends strongly on transformation strains Heat treatment and quenching Thermal–metallurgical–mechanical coupling: transformation kinetics, volume change and transformation plasticity Distortion and cracking risk; cooling-rate dependent microstructure Hot and cold rolling, forging, extrusion Large plastic deformation, contact, friction, temperature Often explicit or implicit with remeshing; flow stress from physically based models Sheet forming and springback Anisotropic plasticity, bending, contact Yield criterion and hardening at large strain control accuracy; shear-locking-free elements Fatigue and fracture Stress concentrations, crack-tip fields, J-integral, cohesive-zone and extended FEM crack growth Mesh around the crack tip determines accuracy Crystal-plasticity FEM (CPFEM) Grain-level slip in a polycrystal or a representative volume element (RVE), predicting texture, anisotropy and local strain Spectral-solver alternatives (for example DAMASK) are common for periodic RVEs Homogenisation Effective properties of composites and multiphase steels from an RVE under periodic boundary conditions Results must lie between the Voigt (uniform strain) and Reuss (uniform stress) bounds The Voigt and Reuss bounds for the effective modulus of a mixture with volume fractions fi are EV = ΣfiEi and 1/ER = Σfi/Ei; any computed effective modulus outside this range indicates an error."},{"n":10,"l":"Topic 10","t":"The finite element method","h":"9. Verification and validation","u":"computational/finite-element-method.html#quality","x":"Verification: check against analytical solutions (bars, beams, the Kirsch plate, one-dimensional transient conduction), run the patch test (a mesh must reproduce uniform strain exactly), use manufactured solutions, and perform mesh and time-step convergence studies. Sanity checks: reaction forces equal applied loads; energy balances; symmetry is preserved; units are consistent (FEM codes are unit-less, so any consistent system works, but a mix of mm and Pa does not). Validation: compare with measurements not used in calibration, such as thermocouple histories, strain fields from digital image correlation, residual stress by diffraction, and measured distortion. Sensitivity: vary uncertain inputs (friction, heat-transfer coefficient, flow-curve parameters) to see which ones control the answer."},{"n":10,"l":"Topic 10","t":"The finite element method","h":"10. Software","u":"computational/finite-element-method.html#software","x":"Commercial: Abaqus, Ansys, COMSOL Multiphysics, MSC Marc, LS-DYNA (explicit); process-specific codes such as DEFORM (forming and heat treatment), Simufact, ProCAST and MAGMA (casting), SYSWELD (welding and heat treatment). Open source: CalculiX, Code_Aster, Elmer, FEniCSx, deal.II, MOOSE, FreeFEM; Gmsh for meshing and ParaView for visualisation; OpenFOAM for finite-volume fluid and heat transfer problems."},{"n":10,"l":"Topic 10","t":"The finite element method","h":"11. A short program","u":"computational/finite-element-method.html#code","x":"A complete finite element solution of a one-dimensional bar under uniform body force, in plain Python, shows assembly and boundary conditions. The problem is −u″ = 1 on (0, L) with u(0) = 0 and zero force at x = L, whose exact solution is u = Lx − x²/2: import numpy as np L, n = 1.0, 8 # bar length, number of elements x = np.linspace(0, L, n + 1) K = np.zeros((n + 1, n + 1)) f = np.zeros(n + 1) for e in range(n): # assemble element by element h = x[e + 1] - x[e] K[e:e + 2, e:e + 2] += (1.0 / h) * np.array([[1, -1], [-1, 1]]) # EA = 1 f[e:e + 2] += h / 2 * 1.0 # uniform body force of 1 u = np.zeros(n + 1) u[1:] = np.linalg.solve(K[1:, 1:], f[1:]) # u(0) = 0 imposed by removing node 0 exact = x * (L - x / 2) print(\"max nodal error:\", np.max(np.abs(u - exact))) The nodal error is at the level of rounding error: for this equation, linear elements in one dimension give exact nodal values. Change the load or the coefficient (for example a stiffness that varies with x) and the nodal values stop being exact; then compare the error for n = 4, 8, 16 to see the convergence rate directly. Key takeaways The weak form with shape functions turns a PDE into a sparse linear system, K u = f, assembled element by element. A mesh convergence study is mandatory. Displacement error falls as h to the p+1 for smooth solutions, while stress singularities never converge. Nonlinear problems use Newton-Raphson with load steps; time-dependent ones use explicit or implicit integration. The constitutive law and boundary conditions (heat transfer, friction) usually dominate the uncertainty. Verify against analytic cases (the Kirsch plate gives a factor of 3, the patch test) and validate against measurements."},{"n":10,"l":"Topic 10","t":"The finite element method","h":"12. Check your understanding","u":"computational/finite-element-method.html#check","x":"Why is the peak stress at a sharp internal corner not a reliable result? The exact elastic solution has a singularity there, so the computed peak stress increases without limit as the mesh is refined. A converged answer requires a physically realistic corner radius, or a different measure such as a stress intensity factor or J-integral. A transient thermal model of a quench gives implausible results. Name three inputs to check. The heat-transfer coefficient at the surface (it varies strongly with temperature and boiling regime), the temperature-dependent specific heat and latent heat of transformation, and the initial temperature distribution. Then check time-step size and mesh density near the surface where gradients are steepest. Your explicit dynamics run becomes unstable after you refine the mesh. Why? The stable time step is bounded by the time a stress wave takes to cross the smallest element, about h/c. Halving the element size halves the stable step, and a time step fixed from the coarse mesh now exceeds the limit."},{"n":10,"l":"Topic 10","t":"The finite element method","h":"13. References and further reading","u":"computational/finite-element-method.html#refs","x":"Zienkiewicz, O. C., Taylor, R. L. and Zhu, J. Z., The Finite Element Method: Its Basis and Fundamentals, Butterworth-Heinemann. Hughes, T. J. R., The Finite Element Method: Linear Static and Dynamic Finite Element Analysis, Dover. Bathe, K.-J., Finite Element Procedures, Prentice Hall. Belytschko, T., Liu, W. K., Moran, B. and Elkhodary, K. I., Nonlinear Finite Elements for Continua and Structures, Wiley. Cook, R. D., Malkus, D. S., Plesha, M. E. and Witt, R. J., Concepts and Applications of Finite Element Analysis, Wiley. Goldak, J., Chakravarti, A. and Bibby, M., A new finite element model for welding heat sources, Metall. Trans. B 15, 299 (1984). Roters, F. et al., Overview of constitutive laws, kinematics, homogenization and multiscale methods in crystal plasticity finite-element modeling, Acta Mater. 58, 1152 (2010). Roters, F. et al., DAMASK: the Düsseldorf Advanced Material Simulation Kit, Comput. Mater. Sci. 158, 420 (2019). Previous topicPhase-field modelling Next topicMachine learning for materials"},{"n":11,"l":"Topic 11","t":"Machine learning for materials","h":"Machine learning for materials","u":"computational/machine-learning-for-materials.html","x":"Topics/DataTopic 11 Machine learning for materials Machine learning (ML) fits flexible functions to data. In materials science it supplies fast surrogates for expensive simulations, extracts structure–property relationships from experiments, and guides which calculation or experiment to do next. It interpolates well and extrapolates badly, and most of the craft lies in knowing the difference. Reading timeabout 15 min Builds on5. Density functional theory6. Molecular dynamicsB5. Programming and workflow Leads to— CategoryData After reading this page you should be able to: Describe the main uses of ML in materials and the workflow for a supervised model. Choose descriptors and a model suited to the amount and kind of data. Validate a model honestly, including with grouped cross-validation and baselines. Explain how machine-learned interatomic potentials and active learning work, and recognise the common pitfalls."},{"n":11,"l":"Topic 11","t":"Machine learning for materials","h":"1. Where machine learning fits","u":"computational/machine-learning-for-materials.html#role","x":"The methods in the earlier topics are built from physical laws, which give reliable predictions but at a computational cost that limits how many materials, structures or conditions can be examined. ML trades some of that generality for speed: it learns a mapping from inputs to outputs from examples, then evaluates it in microseconds. The main uses in materials are: Property prediction. Predict a property (formation energy, band gap, strength, hardness, fatigue life) from composition, structure or processing history. Interatomic potentials. Reproduce DFT energies and forces for molecular dynamics at a fraction of the cost (Section 8). Surrogate models. Replace a slow simulation (a finite-element model, a phase-field run) by a fast approximation to allow optimisation or uncertainty analysis. Inverse design and discovery. Search a large space of compositions or processes for ones meeting a target, with the model proposing the next candidate (Section 9). Characterisation. Segment and classify micrographs, identify phases from diffraction patterns, analyse spectra, extract data from the literature. All of these rely on the premise that similar inputs give similar outputs. They work where the data cover the question and fail where they do not. The “learning” is statistics, not understanding, so ML is best treated as one more tool beside the physical models, and its predictions as hypotheses to test."},{"n":11,"l":"Topic 11","t":"Machine learning for materials","h":"2. The workflow","u":"computational/machine-learning-for-materials.html#workflow","x":"Define the target and the decision it informs: what accuracy is useful, and what error would be harmful? Collect and clean data (Section 3), documenting its origin. Featurise: convert each material or process into numbers (Section 4). Split the data into training, validation and test sets in a way that mimics the intended use (Section 6). Train and tune models, starting with simple ones and a trivial baseline. Validate on the held-out test set; examine errors, not only averages; quantify uncertainty. Deploy and monitor: check predictions against new measurements and retrain as data grow. Supervised learning (input–output examples) covers regression (continuous property) and classification (phase present or not, stable or unstable). Unsupervised learning finds structure without labels: clustering of microstructures, dimensionality reduction (PCA, t-SNE, UMAP) to visualise a composition space or a set of atomic environments."},{"n":11,"l":"Topic 11","t":"Machine learning for materials","h":"3. Data","u":"computational/machine-learning-for-materials.html#data","x":"Data are almost always the limiting factor. Computed data are abundant and consistent (Materials Project, OQMD, AFLOW, JARVIS, NOMAD; see the introduction), with hundreds of thousands of DFT entries; experimental data are scarcer, noisier and heterogeneous, with typical industrial datasets of tens to thousands of samples. Small data. A few hundred points constrain a model to be simple; complex models memorise. Use strong prior information (physics-based features), simple models and cross-validation. Bias. Published and tested materials are those that worked. A model trained only on successes cannot learn what fails; record negative results. Heterogeneity. The same nominal property measured in different laboratories, specimens or conditions differs. Record the conditions (temperature, strain rate, grain size, heat treatment) as features or normalise carefully. Quality. Remove duplicates, check units and outliers, and keep a record of every cleaning step. Errors in the training data are learned. Provenance. Store raw data, code and software versions so that results can be reproduced and data reused (FAIR principles: findable, accessible, interoperable, reusable)."},{"n":11,"l":"Topic 11","t":"Machine learning for materials","h":"4. Descriptors","u":"computational/machine-learning-for-materials.html#features","x":"A model needs each material as a fixed-length vector of numbers, the descriptor or feature vector. Good descriptors encode the physics that matters; they are the main way prior knowledge enters. Descriptor type Examples When to use Composition-based Statistics (mean, range, deviation) of elemental properties such as radius, electronegativity, valence electrons, melting point (the “Magpie” set) When the structure is unknown, for example in alloy and glass composition screening Structure-based Radial and angular distribution functions, Coulomb matrix, smooth overlap of atomic positions (SOAP), symmetry functions, atomic cluster expansion When atomic positions are given; essential for potentials and structure–property maps Graph-based (learned) Crystal graphs where atoms are nodes and bonds are edges; message-passing and equivariant neural networks learn the features Large datasets of structures Process and microstructure Composition, heating and cooling temperatures, times and rates, rolling reduction, grain size, phase fractions Experimental property prediction for alloys and processes Physics-derived Quantities from CALPHAD or DFT, such as phase fractions, driving forces, stacking-fault energy, diffusivity When the output is governed by thermodynamics or kinetics that a simulation can estimate The libraries matminer, pymatgen and DScribe compute many of these. Two rules help: features must be invariant to things that should not matter (atom labelling, rotation, translation, choice of unit cell), and the number of features should be small compared with the number of samples, or regularisation must control it."},{"n":11,"l":"Topic 11","t":"Machine learning for materials","h":"5. Models","u":"computational/machine-learning-for-materials.html#models","x":"Model Suited to Strengths Weaknesses Linear, ridge, LASSO Tens to thousands of samples Simple, interpretable; LASSO selects a sparse set of features Cannot capture strong nonlinearity Gaussian process, kernel ridge Small to medium data Smooth interpolation; Gaussian process gives uncertainty; basis of several interatomic potentials Cost grows as N3 with training set size (approximations exist) Random forest, gradient-boosted trees Tabular data of hundreds to hundreds of thousands Robust, little tuning, handle mixed features, give importances Cannot extrapolate beyond the training range; piecewise-constant predictions Neural networks (fully connected) Large data Flexible; scales to millions Needs more data and tuning; harder uncertainty Graph and equivariant networks Large structural datasets, potentials Learn descriptors; built-in symmetries give data efficiency Costly to train; need care with out-of-distribution structures Symbolic regression and sparse models (for example SISSO) Small data with physical intuition Produce compact formulas that can be inspected Search is expensive; results sensitive to the candidate features A sensible rule is to begin with the simplest model that could work and a trivial baseline (always predicting the mean, or a physical rule of thumb), and to add complexity only while the validation error keeps improving."},{"n":11,"l":"Topic 11","t":"Machine learning for materials","h":"6. Validation","u":"computational/machine-learning-for-materials.html#validation","x":"The goal of a model is accuracy on new cases, not on the data it was trained on. A flexible model can fit its training data perfectly and still predict poorly, as the animation shows: a straight line is too simple (underfitting, high bias), a moderate polynomial captures the trend, and a high-degree polynomial threads through every noisy point but oscillates wildly in between (overfitting, high variance). Fits of increasing flexibility to the same twelve noisy points. The error on the training points keeps falling as the degree rises, but the error against the true underlying curve is smallest for the moderate model and explodes for the most flexible one. Splits and cross-validation Reserve a test set that is never used for fitting or tuning, and use k-fold cross-validation on the rest to choose models and hyperparameters. Materials data need extra care because samples are not independent: many entries in a database are small variations of the same chemistry, so a random split puts near-duplicates in both training and test sets and gives optimistic scores. Better splits for materials are: Grouped splits: all members of a chemical family, composition system or alloy heat stay together (leave-one-cluster-out). Time-based splits: train on older experiments, test on newer ones, as the model will actually be used. Extrapolation tests: hold out the highest or lowest values of the target, or new chemical systems, and measure the degradation in accuracy. Metrics and diagnostics Report mean absolute error (MAE, in the units of the property), RMSE and R2, and always compare with a baseline model; a parity plot (predicted against measured) shows where the errors are; a learning curve (error against training-set size) shows whether more data would help or whether the model or descriptors are the limit. Absolute accuracy matters less than accuracy relative to the spread in the data and to the difference you need to resolve."},{"n":11,"l":"Topic 11","t":"Machine learning for materials","h":"7. Uncertainty","u":"computational/machine-learning-for-materials.html#uncertainty","x":"A prediction without an uncertainty is hard to act on. Two kinds exist: aleatoric (noise in the data, irreducible) and epistemic (lack of data in this region, reducible). Methods include the predictive variance of Gaussian processes, the spread of an ensemble of models trained on resampled data or with different initialisations, dropout-based estimates, and conformal prediction, which gives intervals with a guaranteed coverage under mild assumptions. Check calibration: among predictions with 90% intervals, about 90% of true values should fall inside. The applicability domain, the region of descriptor space where training data exist, should be part of the model: a prediction far from all training points is a guess, whatever the number looks like."},{"n":11,"l":"Topic 11","t":"Machine learning for materials","h":"8. Machine-learned interatomic potentials","u":"computational/machine-learning-for-materials.html#potentials","x":"The most successful use of ML in atomistic modelling is the machine-learned interatomic potential (MLIP). It writes the energy of a configuration as a sum of atomic contributions, each a learned function of the neighbourhood of an atom within a cutoff, and trains on DFT energies, forces and stresses: E = Σi ε( descriptor of the environment of atom i ), Fi = −∇i E Because forces come from differentiating a single energy function, energy is conserved. The function must be invariant to translation, rotation and swapping identical atoms, which is built into the descriptors (SOAP, symmetry functions, atomic cluster expansion) or into the architecture (equivariant message-passing networks such as NequIP and MACE). Families include the Behler–Parrinello neural-network potentials, Gaussian approximation potentials, moment tensor potentials, SNAP, and equivariant graph networks. Classical (EAM, Tersoff) Machine-learned DFT Cost per atom per step ∼ 1 µs ∼ 10 µs to 10 ms (depends on model) ∼ 1 to 1000 s Accuracy versus DFT (typical) Tens to hundreds of meV/atom for complex properties Few meV/atom in energy; 20–100 meV/Å in forces Reference Transferability Limited by the functional form Good inside training data; poor outside General Effort to build Months of parameter fitting by experts Generating training data and training None Building one properly Training data must span what the simulation will visit: perfect crystals, strained cells, defects, surfaces, liquid configurations, high-temperature MD snapshots, and saddle points if barriers matter. Active learning: run MD with the current potential, flag configurations where an ensemble of potentials disagrees (high uncertainty), label them with DFT, retrain, and repeat. This is how a potential becomes robust without enormous databases. Validate on properties, not only on a test set of energies. A low error on held-out DFT frames does not guarantee a correct lattice constant, elastic constants, phonons, defect energies, stacking-fault energies or melting point. Check those, as for any interatomic potential. Watch for the loss of physics at short range (atoms colliding) and long range (electrostatics, dispersion), which are not captured by a finite cutoff unless added explicitly. Universal (foundation) potentials such as M3GNet, CHGNet and MACE-MP-0 were trained on large databases of DFT relaxations covering most of the "},{"n":11,"l":"Topic 11","t":"Machine learning for materials","h":"9. Active learning and design","u":"computational/machine-learning-for-materials.html#active","x":"When evaluating the true function (an experiment, a DFT calculation) is expensive, ML can decide what to evaluate next. Bayesian optimisation fits a surrogate with uncertainty (typically a Gaussian process) to the data collected so far and picks the next point by maximising an acquisition function that balances exploitation (high predicted value) and exploration (high uncertainty), such as expected improvement or upper confidence bound. It can find good alloys or process settings in tens of experiments where a grid would need thousands. Multi-objective versions search for the Pareto front of trade-offs (for example strength against ductility). Combined with automation, these loops form “self-driving laboratories”. The same logic improves training sets for potentials and surrogates (Section 8). Design is an extrapolation task: the goal is a material better than those in the data. Treat the predictions as candidates to verify by experiment or by a physics-based calculation, not as results."},{"n":11,"l":"Topic 11","t":"Machine learning for materials","h":"10. Combining with physics","u":"computational/machine-learning-for-materials.html#physics","x":"Physics-based features (thermodynamic phase fractions, driving forces, computed diffusivities) as model inputs improve accuracy on small datasets. Hybrid models let ML correct the error of a physical model (a residual) or supply a term inside it, for example a flow stress inside a finite-element model, so the physical structure keeps the extrapolation sensible. Multi-fidelity and transfer learning: pre-train on abundant cheap data (simulation, lower-accuracy DFT) and fine-tune on scarce accurate data (experiments). Constraints such as monotonic dependence on temperature, positivity, conservation laws or symmetry can be imposed in the architecture or loss. Physics-informed neural networks add the residual of a differential equation to the loss so the network solves the PDE; they are useful for inverse problems with sparse data but are generally slower and less accurate than conventional solvers for forward problems. Interpretation. Feature importances, partial-dependence plots and SHAP values indicate which inputs drive predictions. They show association learned from the data, not causation, and should be compared against known mechanisms."},{"n":11,"l":"Topic 11","t":"Machine learning for materials","h":"11. Common pitfalls","u":"computational/machine-learning-for-materials.html#pitfalls","x":"Data leakage. Information from the test set influences training: near-duplicates across splits, normalisation computed on all data before splitting, features that are functions of the target. Tuning on the test set. Repeated peeking turns the test set into a validation set; keep a final untouched set. No baseline. A model with R2 = 0.8 may be worse than a simple rule that gives 0.85. Extrapolation presented as interpolation. High scores on random splits hide failure on new chemistry. Ignoring uncertainty and the applicability domain. Confusing correlation with mechanism, especially in observational process data where variables are correlated by how the plant is run. Irreproducibility. Unrecorded random seeds, software versions or data versions."},{"n":11,"l":"Topic 11","t":"Machine learning for materials","h":"12. A short program","u":"computational/machine-learning-for-materials.html#code","x":"A random-forest regression validated with grouped cross-validation and compared with a mean-value baseline, using scikit-learn. X, y and groups come from your own dataset: groups gives the chemical family or alloy system of each row so that related samples are never split between training and testing. import numpy as np from sklearn.ensemble import RandomForestRegressor from sklearn.model_selection import GroupKFold, cross_val_predict from sklearn.metrics import mean_absolute_error # X: (n_samples, n_features) descriptors; y: target property; groups: family id per row model = RandomForestRegressor(n_estimators=300, random_state=0) pred = cross_val_predict(model, X, y, cv=GroupKFold(n_splits=5), groups=groups) print(\"model MAE :\", mean_absolute_error(y, pred)) print(\"baseline MAE:\", mean_absolute_error(y, np.full_like(y, y.mean()))) Try replacing GroupKFold with ordinary shuffled k-fold and compare: if the error drops sharply, your data contain many near-duplicates, and the grouped score is the honest one. Key takeaways ML interpolates well and extrapolates badly: trust it inside the training domain and doubt it outside. Descriptors carry the physics and symmetry; small datasets favour simple models and physics-based features. Validate with grouped, time-based or extrapolation splits against a baseline, and report uncertainty. Machine-learned potentials reach near-DFT accuracy at far lower cost; build them with active learning and validate on properties, not only on energies. Active learning and Bayesian optimisation choose the next calculation or experiment; combine them with physical models."},{"n":11,"l":"Topic 11","t":"Machine learning for materials","h":"13. Check your understanding","u":"computational/machine-learning-for-materials.html#check","x":"A model trained on 50 000 database entries reports R2 = 0.98 with a random 80/20 split. Why might it still fail on a new compound? The database contains many closely related compositions, so a random split puts similar entries in training and test sets, and the score measures interpolation among near-duplicates. A new chemical family lies outside the training distribution. Test with grouped or leave-one-cluster-out splits. Why do machine-learned potentials need uncertainty estimates in active learning? During MD the potential may visit configurations not represented in the training data, where its prediction is unreliable but looks plausible. Disagreement between an ensemble of potentials flags these configurations; computing DFT only for them adds the most informative training data at the least cost. Training error keeps falling but validation error rises. What is happening and what can you do? The model is overfitting. Options: reduce model complexity, add regularisation, increase the amount of data, use better descriptors, stop training earlier, or average an ensemble."},{"n":11,"l":"Topic 11","t":"Machine learning for materials","h":"14. References and further reading","u":"computational/machine-learning-for-materials.html#refs","x":"Butler, K. T., Davies, D. W., Cartwright, H., Isayev, O. and Walsh, A., Machine learning for molecular and materials science, Nature 559, 547 (2018). Schmidt, J., Marques, M. R. G., Botti, S. and Marques, M. A. L., Recent advances and applications of machine learning in solid-state materials science, npj Comput. Mater. 5, 83 (2019). Hastie, T., Tibshirani, R. and Friedman, J., The Elements of Statistical Learning, Springer. Rasmussen, C. E. and Williams, C. K. I., Gaussian Processes for Machine Learning, MIT Press. Ward, L., Agrawal, A., Choudhary, A. and Wolverton, C., A general-purpose machine learning framework for predicting properties of inorganic materials, npj Comput. Mater. 2, 16028 (2016). Ward, L. et al., Matminer: an open source toolkit for materials data mining, Comput. Mater. Sci. 152, 60 (2018). Meredig, B. et al., Can machine learning identify the next high-temperature superconductor? Examining extrapolation performance for materials discovery, Mol. Syst. Des. Eng. 3, 819 (2018). Behler, J. and Parrinello, M., Phys. Rev. Lett. 98, 146401 (2007); Bartók, A. P. et al., Phys. Rev. Lett. 104, 136403 (2010). Bartók, A. P., Kondor, R. and Csányi, G., On representing chemical environments, Phys. Rev. B 87, 184115 (2013). Xie, T. and Grossman, J. C., Crystal graph convolutional neural networks, Phys. Rev. Lett. 120, 145301 (2018). Batzner, S. et al., E(3)-equivariant graph neural networks for data-efficient and accurate interatomic potentials, Nat. Commun. 13, 2453 (2022). Chen, C. and Ong, S. P., A universal graph deep learning interatomic potential for the periodic table, Nat. Comput. Sci. 2, 718 (2022). Lookman, T., Balachandran, P. V., Xue, D. and Yuan, R., Active learning in materials science with emphasis on adaptive sampling using uncertainties for targeted design, npj Comput. Mater. 5, 21 (2019). Deringer, V. L. et al., Gaussian process regression for materials and molecules, Chem. Rev. 121, 10073 (2021). Raissi, M., Perdikaris, P. and Karniadakis, G. E., Physics-informed neural networks, J. Comput. Phys. 378, 686 (2019). Previous topicThe finite element method"},{"n":0,"l":"Glossary","t":"Glossary","h":"atom","u":"glossary.html#atom","x":"The smallest unit of an element: a tiny nucleus surrounded by a cloud of electrons, about 0.1 to 0.3 nanometres across."},{"n":0,"l":"Glossary","t":"Glossary","h":"nucleus","u":"glossary.html#nucleus","x":"The tiny, dense core of an atom, made of protons and neutrons, holding almost all of its mass."},{"n":0,"l":"Glossary","t":"Glossary","h":"electron","u":"glossary.html#electron","x":"A very light, negatively charged particle in the cloud around the nucleus; electrons are what bond atoms together."},{"n":0,"l":"Glossary","t":"Glossary","h":"proton","u":"glossary.html#proton","x":"A positively charged particle in the nucleus; the number of protons decides which element an atom is."},{"n":0,"l":"Glossary","t":"Glossary","h":"neutron","u":"glossary.html#neutron","x":"An uncharged particle in the nucleus with nearly the same mass as a proton."},{"n":0,"l":"Glossary","t":"Glossary","h":"element","u":"glossary.html#element","x":"A kind of atom defined by its number of protons, such as iron or carbon."},{"n":0,"l":"Glossary","t":"Glossary","h":"atomic number","u":"glossary.html#atomic-number","x":"The number of protons in an atom's nucleus; it identifies the element."},{"n":0,"l":"Glossary","t":"Glossary","h":"valence electrons","u":"glossary.html#valence-electrons","x":"The outermost electrons of an atom, which take part in bonding."},{"n":0,"l":"Glossary","t":"Glossary","h":"periodic table","u":"glossary.html#periodic-table","x":"The chart of elements ordered by atomic number, with similar elements in the same column."},{"n":0,"l":"Glossary","t":"Glossary","h":"mole","u":"glossary.html#mole","x":"A counting unit equal to 6.022 x 10^23 items, used to count atoms and molecules."},{"n":0,"l":"Glossary","t":"Glossary","h":"Avogadro number","u":"glossary.html#avogadro-number","x":"6.022 x 10^23, the number of items in one mole."},{"n":0,"l":"Glossary","t":"Glossary","h":"bond","u":"glossary.html#bond","x":"The attraction that holds atoms together, arising from the electrons and nuclei of neighbouring atoms."},{"n":0,"l":"Glossary","t":"Glossary","h":"bond length","u":"glossary.html#bond-length","x":"The distance between two bonded atoms at which their energy is lowest."},{"n":0,"l":"Glossary","t":"Glossary","h":"bond energy","u":"glossary.html#bond-energy","x":"The energy needed to separate two bonded atoms; the depth of the energy valley."},{"n":0,"l":"Glossary","t":"Glossary","h":"metallic bond","u":"glossary.html#metallic-bond","x":"A bond in which outer electrons are shared by all atoms and move freely, giving metals their conductivity and ductility."},{"n":0,"l":"Glossary","t":"Glossary","h":"covalent bond","u":"glossary.html#covalent-bond","x":"A bond in which neighbouring atoms share pairs of electrons in definite directions."},{"n":0,"l":"Glossary","t":"Glossary","h":"ionic bond","u":"glossary.html#ionic-bond","x":"A bond in which one atom hands electrons to another and the resulting opposite charges attract."},{"n":0,"l":"Glossary","t":"Glossary","h":"van der Waals","u":"glossary.html#van-der-waals","x":"A weak attraction between neutral atoms or molecules caused by fleeting shifts of charge."},{"n":0,"l":"Glossary","t":"Glossary","h":"crystal","u":"glossary.html#crystal","x":"A solid in which atoms sit in a regular pattern that repeats in all directions."},{"n":0,"l":"Glossary","t":"Glossary","h":"unit cell","u":"glossary.html#unit-cell","x":"The smallest repeating block of a crystal pattern."},{"n":0,"l":"Glossary","t":"Glossary","h":"lattice","u":"glossary.html#lattice","x":"The regular array of points on which the atoms of a crystal are arranged."},{"n":0,"l":"Glossary","t":"Glossary","h":"grain","u":"glossary.html#grain","x":"A single small crystal inside a polycrystalline material."},{"n":0,"l":"Glossary","t":"Glossary","h":"grain boundary","u":"glossary.html#grain-boundary","x":"The surface where two grains of different orientation meet."},{"n":0,"l":"Glossary","t":"Glossary","h":"polycrystal","u":"glossary.html#polycrystal","x":"A solid made of many small crystals (grains) with different orientations."},{"n":0,"l":"Glossary","t":"Glossary","h":"microstructure","u":"glossary.html#microstructure","x":"The arrangement of grains, phases, particles and defects seen at micrometre scale."},{"n":0,"l":"Glossary","t":"Glossary","h":"glass","u":"glossary.html#glass","x":"A solid with no repeating atomic pattern, like a frozen liquid."},{"n":0,"l":"Glossary","t":"Glossary","h":"amorphous","u":"glossary.html#amorphous","x":"Without long-range atomic order, as in glass."},{"n":0,"l":"Glossary","t":"Glossary","h":"vacancy","u":"glossary.html#vacancy","x":"An empty atom site in a crystal."},{"n":0,"l":"Glossary","t":"Glossary","h":"interstitial","u":"glossary.html#interstitial","x":"An atom squeezed into the space between the normal sites of a crystal."},{"n":0,"l":"Glossary","t":"Glossary","h":"solute","u":"glossary.html#solute","x":"An atom of a minority element dissolved in a host material."},{"n":0,"l":"Glossary","t":"Glossary","h":"alloy","u":"glossary.html#alloy","x":"A metal made by mixing two or more elements."},{"n":0,"l":"Glossary","t":"Glossary","h":"dislocation","u":"glossary.html#dislocation","x":"A line defect in a crystal, like an extra half-sheet of atoms, whose motion lets metals deform permanently."},{"n":0,"l":"Glossary","t":"Glossary","h":"defect","u":"glossary.html#defect","x":"Any departure from a perfect crystal: vacancies, solutes, dislocations, boundaries and so on."},{"n":0,"l":"Glossary","t":"Glossary","h":"phase","u":"glossary.html#phase","x":"A region of material with uniform structure and composition, such as the solid or the liquid."},{"n":0,"l":"Glossary","t":"Glossary","h":"semiconductor","u":"glossary.html#semiconductor","x":"A material that conducts electricity only a little, in a way that can be controlled, as silicon does."},{"n":0,"l":"Glossary","t":"Glossary","h":"ceramic","u":"glossary.html#ceramic","x":"A hard, brittle, heat-resistant material, usually a compound of metal and non-metal atoms."},{"n":0,"l":"Glossary","t":"Glossary","h":"polymer","u":"glossary.html#polymer","x":"A material made of long chain molecules, such as polyethylene or nylon."},{"n":0,"l":"Glossary","t":"Glossary","h":"composite","u":"glossary.html#composite","x":"A material combining two or more constituents to get properties neither has alone."},{"n":0,"l":"Glossary","t":"Glossary","h":"ductile","u":"glossary.html#ductile","x":"Able to deform a lot permanently before breaking."},{"n":0,"l":"Glossary","t":"Glossary","h":"brittle","u":"glossary.html#brittle","x":"Breaking with little permanent deformation."},{"n":0,"l":"Glossary","t":"Glossary","h":"Miller indices","u":"glossary.html#miller-indices","x":"Small integers that label directions and planes in a crystal."},{"n":0,"l":"Glossary","t":"Glossary","h":"Burgers vector","u":"glossary.html#burgers-vector","x":"The vector that measures the size and direction of the lattice distortion of a dislocation."},{"n":0,"l":"Glossary","t":"Glossary","h":"Hall-Petch relation","u":"glossary.html#hall-petch-relation","x":"The rule that a metal gets stronger as its grain size gets smaller."},{"n":0,"l":"Glossary","t":"Glossary","h":"strain hardening","u":"glossary.html#strain-hardening","x":"The increase of a metal's strength as it is deformed, caused by dislocations multiplying and obstructing each other."},{"n":0,"l":"Glossary","t":"Glossary","h":"texture","u":"glossary.html#texture","x":"A preferred orientation of the grains in a polycrystal, which makes properties depend on direction."},{"n":0,"l":"Glossary","t":"Glossary","h":"nucleation","u":"glossary.html#nucleation","x":"The first formation of a tiny region of a new phase."},{"n":0,"l":"Glossary","t":"Glossary","h":"martensite","u":"glossary.html#martensite","x":"A very hard steel phase formed by rapid cooling, through a shear-like change of the lattice without atoms diffusing."},{"n":0,"l":"Glossary","t":"Glossary","h":"creep","u":"glossary.html#creep","x":"Slow permanent deformation of a material under constant load at high temperature."},{"n":0,"l":"Glossary","t":"Glossary","h":"fatigue","u":"glossary.html#fatigue","x":"Failure of a material under repeated loading at stresses well below its yield strength."},{"n":0,"l":"Glossary","t":"Glossary","h":"fracture toughness","u":"glossary.html#fracture-toughness","x":"A measure of how well a material resists the growth of a crack."},{"n":0,"l":"Glossary","t":"Glossary","h":"yield strength","u":"glossary.html#yield-strength","x":"The stress at which a material starts to deform permanently."},{"n":0,"l":"Glossary","t":"Glossary","h":"supercell","u":"glossary.html#supercell","x":"A larger cell made by repeating a unit cell, used so that a defect has room to sit in a simulation."},{"n":0,"l":"Glossary","t":"Glossary","h":"periodic boundary conditions","u":"glossary.html#periodic-boundary-conditions","x":"A simulation trick in which the box repeats in all directions, so an atom leaving one side re-enters on the other."},{"n":0,"l":"Glossary","t":"Glossary","h":"scientific notation","u":"glossary.html#scientific-notation","x":"Writing a number as a value between 1 and 10 times a power of ten, such as 6.0 x 10^23."},{"n":0,"l":"Glossary","t":"Glossary","h":"order of magnitude","u":"glossary.html#order-of-magnitude","x":"A factor of ten; two numbers differ by one order of magnitude if one is about ten times the other."},{"n":0,"l":"Glossary","t":"Glossary","h":"significant figures","u":"glossary.html#significant-figures","x":"The digits of a number that carry real information."},{"n":0,"l":"Glossary","t":"Glossary","h":"exponential","u":"glossary.html#exponential","x":"A function of the form A e^(kx), which grows or decays by a fixed factor over equal steps."},{"n":0,"l":"Glossary","t":"Glossary","h":"logarithm","u":"glossary.html#logarithm","x":"The power to which a base must be raised to give a number; log10(1000) = 3."},{"n":0,"l":"Glossary","t":"Glossary","h":"slope","u":"glossary.html#slope","x":"How much a line or curve rises for each step along; the rate of change."},{"n":0,"l":"Glossary","t":"Glossary","h":"derivative","u":"glossary.html#derivative","x":"The slope of a curve at a point: the instantaneous rate of change."},{"n":0,"l":"Glossary","t":"Glossary","h":"integral","u":"glossary.html#integral","x":"The area under a curve; the sum of many thin slices."},{"n":0,"l":"Glossary","t":"Glossary","h":"differential equation","u":"glossary.html#differential-equation","x":"An equation that relates a quantity to its rate of change."},{"n":0,"l":"Glossary","t":"Glossary","h":"half-life","u":"glossary.html#half-life","x":"The time for a decaying quantity to fall to half its value."},{"n":0,"l":"Glossary","t":"Glossary","h":"gradient","u":"glossary.html#gradient","x":"An arrow pointing in the direction of steepest increase of a quantity, with length equal to the steepness."},{"n":0,"l":"Glossary","t":"Glossary","h":"vector","u":"glossary.html#vector","x":"A quantity with both size and direction, drawn as an arrow, such as a force or velocity."},{"n":0,"l":"Glossary","t":"Glossary","h":"matrix","u":"glossary.html#matrix","x":"A rectangular table of numbers that represents a linear transformation."},{"n":0,"l":"Glossary","t":"Glossary","h":"eigenvalue","u":"glossary.html#eigenvalue","x":"A number by which a matrix stretches one of its special directions (eigenvectors); energies and vibration frequencies are eigenvalues."},{"n":0,"l":"Glossary","t":"Glossary","h":"eigenvector","u":"glossary.html#eigenvector","x":"A direction that a matrix only stretches, without turning it."},{"n":0,"l":"Glossary","t":"Glossary","h":"tensor","u":"glossary.html#tensor","x":"A generalisation of vectors; stress and strain are tensors with two indices."},{"n":0,"l":"Glossary","t":"Glossary","h":"Fourier transform","u":"glossary.html#fourier-transform","x":"A way of writing a signal as a sum of waves of different wavelengths."},{"n":0,"l":"Glossary","t":"Glossary","h":"probability","u":"glossary.html#probability","x":"A number from 0 to 1 saying how likely an event is."},{"n":0,"l":"Glossary","t":"Glossary","h":"standard deviation","u":"glossary.html#standard-deviation","x":"A measure of how spread out data are around their mean."},{"n":0,"l":"Glossary","t":"Glossary","h":"standard error","u":"glossary.html#standard-error","x":"The uncertainty of an average: the standard deviation divided by the square root of the number of samples."},{"n":0,"l":"Glossary","t":"Glossary","h":"normal distribution","u":"glossary.html#normal-distribution","x":"The bell-shaped curve that describes many random errors and averages."},{"n":0,"l":"Glossary","t":"Glossary","h":"central limit theorem","u":"glossary.html#central-limit-theorem","x":"The result that sums or averages of many independent random effects tend to a bell-shaped distribution."},{"n":0,"l":"Glossary","t":"Glossary","h":"random walk","u":"glossary.html#random-walk","x":"A path made of random steps, such as a coin-flip left or right each step; its distance grows as the square root of the number of steps."},{"n":0,"l":"Glossary","t":"Glossary","h":"least squares","u":"glossary.html#least-squares","x":"Fitting a line or curve by minimising the sum of the squared distances to the data points."},{"n":0,"l":"Glossary","t":"Glossary","h":"histogram","u":"glossary.html#histogram","x":"A bar chart showing how many data values fall into each range."},{"n":0,"l":"Glossary","t":"Glossary","h":"force","u":"glossary.html#force","x":"A push or pull that changes the motion of an object; measured in newtons."},{"n":0,"l":"Glossary","t":"Glossary","h":"velocity","u":"glossary.html#velocity","x":"How fast and in which direction something moves."},{"n":0,"l":"Glossary","t":"Glossary","h":"acceleration","u":"glossary.html#acceleration","x":"The rate at which velocity changes."},{"n":0,"l":"Glossary","t":"Glossary","h":"kinetic energy","u":"glossary.html#kinetic-energy","x":"Energy of motion, equal to half the mass times the speed squared."},{"n":0,"l":"Glossary","t":"Glossary","h":"potential energy","u":"glossary.html#potential-energy","x":"Energy stored because of position or arrangement, such as in a stretched spring or a chemical bond."},{"n":0,"l":"Glossary","t":"Glossary","h":"potential energy surface","u":"glossary.html#potential-energy-surface","x":"The energy of a system of atoms as a function of all their positions; atoms move over it like balls over hills and valleys."},{"n":0,"l":"Glossary","t":"Glossary","h":"equilibrium","u":"glossary.html#equilibrium","x":"A state with no net force, or in which nothing changes on average."},{"n":0,"l":"Glossary","t":"Glossary","h":"Newton's laws","u":"glossary.html#newton-s-laws","x":"Three laws of motion: objects keep their velocity unless a force acts, F = ma, and forces come in equal and opposite pairs."},{"n":0,"l":"Glossary","t":"Glossary","h":"Hooke's law","u":"glossary.html#hooke-s-law","x":"The rule that a spring's restoring force is proportional to its stretch, F = -kx."},{"n":0,"l":"Glossary","t":"Glossary","h":"stress","u":"glossary.html#stress","x":"Force per unit area acting inside a material."},{"n":0,"l":"Glossary","t":"Glossary","h":"strain","u":"glossary.html#strain","x":"The stretch of a material as a fraction of its original length."},{"n":0,"l":"Glossary","t":"Glossary","h":"Young's modulus","u":"glossary.html#young-s-modulus","x":"The stiffness of a material: the ratio of stress to strain in the elastic region."},{"n":0,"l":"Glossary","t":"Glossary","h":"stiffness","u":"glossary.html#stiffness","x":"How strongly something resists being stretched or squeezed."},{"n":0,"l":"Glossary","t":"Glossary","h":"temperature","u":"glossary.html#temperature","x":"A measure of the average kinetic energy of the random motion of atoms."},{"n":0,"l":"Glossary","t":"Glossary","h":"kelvin","u":"glossary.html#kelvin","x":"The unit of absolute temperature; 0 K is absolute zero and K = degrees Celsius + 273.15."},{"n":0,"l":"Glossary","t":"Glossary","h":"Boltzmann constant","u":"glossary.html#boltzmann-constant","x":"The constant k_B = 8.617 x 10^-5 eV/K that converts temperature into energy."},{"n":0,"l":"Glossary","t":"Glossary","h":"heat","u":"glossary.html#heat","x":"Energy transferred between bodies because of a temperature difference."},{"n":0,"l":"Glossary","t":"Glossary","h":"thermal energy","u":"glossary.html#thermal-energy","x":"The energy of random atomic motion, of order k_B T per atom."},{"n":0,"l":"Glossary","t":"Glossary","h":"electronvolt","u":"glossary.html#electronvolt","x":"A unit of energy equal to 1.602 x 10^-19 J, convenient for atomic-scale energies."},{"n":0,"l":"Glossary","t":"Glossary","h":"entropy","u":"glossary.html#entropy","x":"A measure of how many microscopic arrangements correspond to a given state; systems tend toward higher entropy."},{"n":0,"l":"Glossary","t":"Glossary","h":"free energy","u":"glossary.html#free-energy","x":"The quantity, combining energy and entropy, that is lowest at equilibrium at fixed temperature."},{"n":0,"l":"Glossary","t":"Glossary","h":"chemical potential","u":"glossary.html#chemical-potential","x":"The change in free energy when one particle is added; equal in all phases at equilibrium."},{"n":0,"l":"Glossary","t":"Glossary","h":"wavefunction","u":"glossary.html#wavefunction","x":"The quantum description of a particle; its square gives the probability of finding it at a place."},{"n":0,"l":"Glossary","t":"Glossary","h":"Schrodinger equation","u":"glossary.html#schrodinger-equation","x":"The basic equation of quantum mechanics, whose solutions give the allowed energies and states of electrons."},{"n":0,"l":"Glossary","t":"Glossary","h":"Pauli exclusion principle","u":"glossary.html#pauli-exclusion-principle","x":"No two electrons can occupy the same quantum state."},{"n":0,"l":"Glossary","t":"Glossary","h":"variational principle","u":"glossary.html#variational-principle","x":"Any trial wavefunction gives an energy at least as high as the true ground-state energy."},{"n":0,"l":"Glossary","t":"Glossary","h":"Born-Oppenheimer approximation","u":"glossary.html#born-oppenheimer-approximation","x":"Treating the nuclei as fixed while the much lighter electrons adjust instantly."},{"n":0,"l":"Glossary","t":"Glossary","h":"phonon","u":"glossary.html#phonon","x":"A quantised vibration of the atoms of a crystal."},{"n":0,"l":"Glossary","t":"Glossary","h":"Bloch's theorem","u":"glossary.html#bloch-s-theorem","x":"The result that electron states in a periodic crystal are plane waves modulated by the lattice periodicity."},{"n":0,"l":"Glossary","t":"Glossary","h":"Brillouin zone","u":"glossary.html#brillouin-zone","x":"The basic cell of reciprocal space in which electron and phonon wavevectors are labelled."},{"n":0,"l":"Glossary","t":"Glossary","h":"reciprocal lattice","u":"glossary.html#reciprocal-lattice","x":"The lattice of wave patterns that fit the crystal; it describes diffraction and electron states."},{"n":0,"l":"Glossary","t":"Glossary","h":"density of states","u":"glossary.html#density-of-states","x":"The number of available states per unit energy."},{"n":0,"l":"Glossary","t":"Glossary","h":"Fermi energy","u":"glossary.html#fermi-energy","x":"The highest occupied electron energy in a metal at zero temperature."},{"n":0,"l":"Glossary","t":"Glossary","h":"band gap","u":"glossary.html#band-gap","x":"The range of energies with no electron states, which makes a material a semiconductor or insulator."},{"n":0,"l":"Glossary","t":"Glossary","h":"diffraction","u":"glossary.html#diffraction","x":"The pattern formed when waves scatter from the regular planes of atoms in a crystal."},{"n":0,"l":"Glossary","t":"Glossary","h":"simulation","u":"glossary.html#simulation","x":"The step-by-step calculation of what a model predicts."},{"n":0,"l":"Glossary","t":"Glossary","h":"model","u":"glossary.html#model","x":"A simplified description of a system that keeps what matters and ignores the rest."},{"n":0,"l":"Glossary","t":"Glossary","h":"time step","u":"glossary.html#time-step","x":"The small interval of time by which a simulation advances at each step."},{"n":0,"l":"Glossary","t":"Glossary","h":"convergence","u":"glossary.html#convergence","x":"The result settling to a stable value as a numerical setting (step size, mesh, sample size) is improved."},{"n":0,"l":"Glossary","t":"Glossary","h":"verification","u":"glossary.html#verification","x":"Checking that the equations are solved correctly."},{"n":0,"l":"Glossary","t":"Glossary","h":"validation","u":"glossary.html#validation","x":"Checking that the model agrees with independent experiments."},{"n":0,"l":"Glossary","t":"Glossary","h":"ensemble","u":"glossary.html#ensemble","x":"The set of states a system can be in under given conditions (fixed temperature, volume and so on)."},{"n":0,"l":"Glossary","t":"Glossary","h":"partition function","u":"glossary.html#partition-function","x":"The sum of Boltzmann factors over all states, from which free energy and averages follow."},{"n":0,"l":"Glossary","t":"Glossary","h":"Boltzmann distribution","u":"glossary.html#boltzmann-distribution","x":"The probability that a system at temperature T is in a state of energy E, proportional to exp(-E/kT)."},{"n":0,"l":"Glossary","t":"Glossary","h":"equipartition","u":"glossary.html#equipartition","x":"The rule that each quadratic term in the energy carries, on average, half of k_B T."},{"n":0,"l":"Glossary","t":"Glossary","h":"ergodic","u":"glossary.html#ergodic","x":"Visiting all accessible states in proportion to their probability, so time averages equal ensemble averages."},{"n":0,"l":"Glossary","t":"Glossary","h":"density functional theory","u":"glossary.html#density-functional-theory","x":"A method that calculates the energy and electronic structure of a material from the electron density."},{"n":0,"l":"Glossary","t":"Glossary","h":"exchange-correlation","u":"glossary.html#exchange-correlation","x":"The part of the electron-electron interaction that DFT must approximate."},{"n":0,"l":"Glossary","t":"Glossary","h":"pseudopotential","u":"glossary.html#pseudopotential","x":"A replacement for the nucleus and core electrons so that only valence electrons are treated explicitly."},{"n":0,"l":"Glossary","t":"Glossary","h":"k-points","u":"glossary.html#k-points","x":"Sample points in the Brillouin zone used for integrals in electronic-structure calculations."},{"n":0,"l":"Glossary","t":"Glossary","h":"self-consistent field","u":"glossary.html#self-consistent-field","x":"An iteration in which the electron density and the potential it creates are repeated until they agree."},{"n":0,"l":"Glossary","t":"Glossary","h":"molecular dynamics","u":"glossary.html#molecular-dynamics","x":"A simulation that follows atoms by calculating forces and integrating Newton's equations in small time steps."},{"n":0,"l":"Glossary","t":"Glossary","h":"interatomic potential","u":"glossary.html#interatomic-potential","x":"A function giving the energy of a set of atoms from their positions, used to calculate forces."},{"n":0,"l":"Glossary","t":"Glossary","h":"thermostat","u":"glossary.html#thermostat","x":"An algorithm that keeps a simulation at a chosen temperature."},{"n":0,"l":"Glossary","t":"Glossary","h":"barostat","u":"glossary.html#barostat","x":"An algorithm that keeps a simulation at a chosen pressure."},{"n":0,"l":"Glossary","t":"Glossary","h":"velocity-Verlet","u":"glossary.html#velocity-verlet","x":"A standard, accurate and stable method for stepping atoms forward in time in molecular dynamics."},{"n":0,"l":"Glossary","t":"Glossary","h":"Monte Carlo","u":"glossary.html#monte-carlo","x":"A family of methods that sample states by random moves accepted or rejected with set probabilities."},{"n":0,"l":"Glossary","t":"Glossary","h":"Metropolis algorithm","u":"glossary.html#metropolis-algorithm","x":"The rule that accepts a move if it lowers the energy, or otherwise with probability exp(-dE/kT)."},{"n":0,"l":"Glossary","t":"Glossary","h":"detailed balance","u":"glossary.html#detailed-balance","x":"The condition that the flow between two states is the same in both directions at equilibrium."},{"n":0,"l":"Glossary","t":"Glossary","h":"kinetic Monte Carlo","u":"glossary.html#kinetic-monte-carlo","x":"A method that picks real events (such as atom hops) with their rates and advances physical time."},{"n":0,"l":"Glossary","t":"Glossary","h":"cluster expansion","u":"glossary.html#cluster-expansion","x":"A way of writing the energy of any arrangement of atoms on a lattice as a sum over small clusters of sites."},{"n":0,"l":"Glossary","t":"Glossary","h":"Ising model","u":"glossary.html#ising-model","x":"A lattice of up and down spins that prefer to align with neighbours; the simplest model of a phase transition."},{"n":0,"l":"Glossary","t":"Glossary","h":"CALPHAD","u":"glossary.html#calphad","x":"A method that models the free energy of each phase and calculates phase diagrams and equilibria for alloys."},{"n":0,"l":"Glossary","t":"Glossary","h":"phase diagram","u":"glossary.html#phase-diagram","x":"A map showing which phases are stable at each temperature and composition."},{"n":0,"l":"Glossary","t":"Glossary","h":"miscibility gap","u":"glossary.html#miscibility-gap","x":"A range of composition and temperature in which a solution separates into two phases."},{"n":0,"l":"Glossary","t":"Glossary","h":"spinodal decomposition","u":"glossary.html#spinodal-decomposition","x":"Spontaneous separation of a solution into two phases, with no barrier to start it."},{"n":0,"l":"Glossary","t":"Glossary","h":"common tangent","u":"glossary.html#common-tangent","x":"The straight line touching a free-energy curve at two points, which gives the compositions of coexisting phases."},{"n":0,"l":"Glossary","t":"Glossary","h":"lever rule","u":"glossary.html#lever-rule","x":"The rule that gives the fractions of two coexisting phases from the overall composition."},{"n":0,"l":"Glossary","t":"Glossary","h":"diffusion","u":"glossary.html#diffusion","x":"The spreading of atoms or molecules by random motion."},{"n":0,"l":"Glossary","t":"Glossary","h":"Fick's laws","u":"glossary.html#fick-s-laws","x":"The laws stating that diffusive flux is proportional to the concentration gradient and that concentration changes accordingly."},{"n":0,"l":"Glossary","t":"Glossary","h":"diffusion coefficient","u":"glossary.html#diffusion-coefficient","x":"A number that sets how fast diffusion happens; MSD = 2Dt in one dimension."},{"n":0,"l":"Glossary","t":"Glossary","h":"Arrhenius law","u":"glossary.html#arrhenius-law","x":"The rule that a thermally activated rate grows as exp(-Q/kT) with temperature."},{"n":0,"l":"Glossary","t":"Glossary","h":"activation energy","u":"glossary.html#activation-energy","x":"The energy barrier a process must overcome to happen."},{"n":0,"l":"Glossary","t":"Glossary","h":"phase-field","u":"glossary.html#phase-field","x":"A method that describes microstructure with smooth fields so that moving interfaces need no special tracking."},{"n":0,"l":"Glossary","t":"Glossary","h":"order parameter","u":"glossary.html#order-parameter","x":"A field that is different in different phases or grains, such as +1 in one phase and -1 in another."},{"n":0,"l":"Glossary","t":"Glossary","h":"Allen-Cahn","u":"glossary.html#allen-cahn","x":"The phase-field equation for fields such as grain orientation that are not conserved; it moves interfaces by curvature."},{"n":0,"l":"Glossary","t":"Glossary","h":"Cahn-Hilliard","u":"glossary.html#cahn-hilliard","x":"The phase-field equation for conserved fields such as composition; it describes spinodal decomposition."},{"n":0,"l":"Glossary","t":"Glossary","h":"finite element method","u":"glossary.html#finite-element-method","x":"A numerical method that divides a body into small elements and solves a differential equation on them."},{"n":0,"l":"Glossary","t":"Glossary","h":"mesh","u":"glossary.html#mesh","x":"The division of a body into small elements used in finite element and similar methods."},{"n":0,"l":"Glossary","t":"Glossary","h":"machine learning","u":"glossary.html#machine-learning","x":"Fitting flexible functions to data so that they can predict new cases."},{"n":0,"l":"Glossary","t":"Glossary","h":"descriptor","u":"glossary.html#descriptor","x":"A set of numbers that describes a material or atomic environment for use in a machine-learning model."},{"n":0,"l":"Glossary","t":"Glossary","h":"overfitting","u":"glossary.html#overfitting","x":"A model fitting the noise in its training data so closely that it predicts new data badly."},{"n":0,"l":"Glossary","t":"Glossary","h":"cross-validation","u":"glossary.html#cross-validation","x":"Testing a model on data held out from training, repeated over several splits."},{"n":0,"l":"Glossary","t":"Glossary","h":"active learning","u":"glossary.html#active-learning","x":"Letting a model choose which calculation or experiment to do next, to learn as efficiently as possible."},{"n":0,"l":"Glossary","t":"Glossary","h":"Python","u":"glossary.html#python","x":"A readable programming language widely used in science."},{"n":0,"l":"Glossary","t":"Glossary","h":"NumPy","u":"glossary.html#numpy","x":"A Python library for fast calculations with arrays of numbers."},{"n":0,"l":"Glossary","t":"Glossary","h":"git","u":"glossary.html#git","x":"A tool that records the history of a project so that you can go back and collaborate."},{"n":0,"l":"Glossary","t":"Glossary","h":"Slurm","u":"glossary.html#slurm","x":"A common job scheduler for computer clusters."},{"n":0,"l":"Glossary","t":"Glossary","h":"MPI","u":"glossary.html#mpi","x":"A standard for programs that run on many processors by passing messages."},{"n":0,"l":"Glossary","t":"Glossary","h":"floating point","u":"glossary.html#floating-point","x":"The way computers store real numbers with a fixed number of significant digits."},{"n":0,"l":"Glossary","t":"Glossary","h":"condition number","u":"glossary.html#condition-number","x":"A measure of how much a problem amplifies small errors in its input."}];
